{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:17:32.917Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"tool","toolName":"read_files","toolCallId":"toolu_01TujDyxdwvvzy8DfFVPH23W","output":[{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\NEXT.md","result":" 1 | # NEXT TASK: T1, core rule engine v2 (tests first)\n 2 | \n 3 | T0 reviewed: tests pass (3/3), and the commit is in. Answer to your question: `docs/sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md` is intentional; it is demo input for **T3** (not T4).\n 4 | \n 5 | **Important changes since T0 (read before coding):**\n 6 | - `memory-bank/projectbrief.md` is now **v2** and `docs/schemes.md` is **rule packs v2** (provenance, verified vs provisional, two verdicts, defensive name matching, corrected widow pension 40–79 and ₹300).\n 7 | - The language changed: **Assamese default + English** (not Hindi).\n 8 | \n 9 | ## Step 0 (small, 5 min)\n10 | - Refresh `memory-bank/productContext.md`, `systemPatterns.md` and `activeContext.md` so they match brief v2 (two verdicts, provenance, Assamese).\n11 | - Switch the app title to \"KaagazCheck: কাগজ চেক\" with the tagline \"Check before you apply\" / \"আবেদনৰ আগতে পৰীক্ষা কৰক\", set `<html lang=\"as\">`, and update the smoke test accordingly.\n12 | \n13 | ## Then do `tasks/backlog/T1-core-engine.md` exactly (it is already updated for v2).\n14 | \n15 | **Done when:** everything in T1's \"Done when\" holds, plus Step 0 is done; LOG.md updated; committed \"T1: core rule engine v2\".","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\schemes.md","result":" 1 | # Rule packs v2: the three schemes (corrected 10 Oct 2026)\n 2 | \n 3 | Source of truth for the rule engine. v2 applies corrections from a check against government sources: widow pension age and amount, PM-KISAN bank checks, and Orunodoi 3.0 rules downgraded to provisional.\n 4 | \n 5 | ## Golden rules for every rule\n 6 | 1. **Every rule carries provenance:** `sourceUrl`, `sourceTitle`, `clause` (if known), `effectiveDate` (if known), `verifiedOn` (2026-10-10), `version`, and `status: verified | provisional`.\n 7 | 2. **Provisional rules never give a definitive pass or fail.** They return `needs_verification` with \"confirm at the office\" and say what to ask.\n 8 | 3. **Document photos can't prove external facts.** Whether a bank account is active or Aadhaar-seeded, whether land is verified, whether eKYC is done: these are `needs_verification` (the user confirms, or checks the official status page), never `pass` from a photo.\n 9 | 4. **Fuzzy name matching never proves identity.** See \"Name consistency\" below.\n10 | 5. **Eligibility and readiness are reported separately.** Eligible ≠ application complete, and complete ≠ government approval.\n11 | 6. **Money values are versioned and split:** `centralShare`, `stateTopUp` (null = unconfirmed), `effectiveDate`, and `source`. Never show an unconfirmed total.\n12 | \n13 | ## Result model\n14 | - **Eligibility:** `likely_eligible` · `not_eligible` (a verified criterion failed) · `needs_verification`\n15 | - **Readiness:** `ready_for_review` (no known issues found by our checks) · `action_required` (mismatch, missing mandatory document, unmet verified criterion) · `needs_verification` (unclear image, provisional rule, external status we can't see)\n16 | - Each check has `{ id, result: ok | action_required | needs_verification | not_applicable, messageEn, messageAs, fixWhere, ruleProvenance }`.\n17 | - Wording promise: \"We help you catch preventable mistakes. We do not guarantee approval; the department decides.\"\n18 | \n19 | ---\n20 | \n21 | ## 1. PM-KISAN (id `pm-kisan`): the FLAGSHIP end-to-end demo\n22 | sourceUrl: https://pmkisan.gov.in/ · summary: https://en.vikaspedia.in/viewcontent/agriculture/policies-and-schemes/crops-related/comprehensive-guide-to-the-pradhan-mantri-kisan-samman-nidhi-(pm-kisan)-scheme?lgn=en · status: verified (core criteria)\n23 | \n24 | - Benefit: ₹6,000/year in 3 instalments of ₹2,000 by DBT (centralShare 6000/yr, stateTopUp 0).\n25 | - Eligible: a landholding farmer family (husband, wife, minor children) owning cultivable land per state land records, **regardless of holding size** (the earlier 2-hectare limit was removed in 2019). **Do not add any landholding-size limit.**\n26 | - Exclusions (verified; any \"yes\" → not_eligible): `holds_constitutional_post`, `govt_employee` (serving or retired; not MTS, Class IV or Group D), `income_tax_payer` (last assessment year), `practising_professional` (doctor, engineer, lawyer, CA, architect), `pension_10k_plus` (₹10,000+/month; not MTS, Class IV or Group D).\n27 | - Mandatory documents: `aadhaar`, `bank_passbook`, `land_record`.\n28 | - Document-level checks (our engine CAN decide): all mandatory docs present; name consistency across aadhaar, bank_passbook and land_record; IFSC format `^[A-Z]{4}0[A-Z0-9]{6}$`; account number present (9–18 digits); the land record shows the applicant (or a family member) as owner.\n29 | - External checks (always `needs_verification`, with how to check): Aadhaar seeded to the bank account (ask at the bank branch); account active; eKYC done (PM-KISAN portal OTP, or CSC biometric); land verified by the state.\n30 | \n31 | ## 2. Orunodoi 3.0, Assam (id `orunodoi-3`): local relevance, verified rules only\n32 | Official page: https://dhemaji.assam.gov.in/scheme-page/orunodoi (Govt of Assam, last updated 8 May 2025). Secondary: https://www.publicservicesmap.in/schemes/orunodoi-3.0--aos?lang=en\n33 | \n34 | **Verified (from the official page):**\n35 | - Benefit: ₹1,250/month to families with **valid ration cards** (centralShare 0, stateTopUp 1250, recurring). Note: a reported one-time ₹1,500 payment for October 2026 is a temporary change; don't treat it as the recurring amount.\n36 | - **No income certificate required** (official page says so).\n37 | - Mandatory: valid ration card. Preliminary selection is \"based on eligibility/ineligibility conditions\" through an undertaking-cum-checklist (Annexure A).\n38 | \n39 | **Provisional (secondary sources, or the 2020 original guideline; NOT confirmed for 3.0):**\n40 | - The NFSA enrolment precondition (myScheme lists it; the official page says \"valid ration card\").\n41 | - The eligibility any-of list (widow, PwD, AAY, household income under ₹2 lakh, etc.).\n42 | - The whole exclusion list (elected representatives, professionals, teachers, government staff and pensioners, contractors, traders, large landholders, four-wheeler owners, similar state cash schemes).\n43 | - Supporting documents (Aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, self-declaration).\n44 | \n45 | **Engine behaviour:** the ration card is a mandatory check (missing → action_required). Each provisional criterion is asked as a question, and any answer that would exclude → `needs_verification` (\"This may make you ineligible. Confirm at the Gram Panchayat.\"), never `not_eligible`. Supporting documents are shown as a checklist labelled \"commonly asked; confirm the current list\".\n46 | - Name consistency across ration_card, aadhaar and bank_passbook (if provided).\n47 | \n48 | ## 3. Widow Pension, IGNWPS under NSAP (id `nsap-widow`): the simple, reliable rule demo\n49 | sourceUrl: https://www.publicservicesmap.in/schemes/indira-gandhi-national-widow-pension-scheme--ignwps (aligns with the Ministry of Rural Development statement cited by the reviewer) · status: verified (age, BPL, central amount)\n50 | \n51 | - Eligible: widow, **age 40–79 inclusive**, household **BPL** per GoI criteria.\n52 | - Age: computed from the Aadhaar DOB **on an explicit `asOfDate`** (default today; injectable for tests).\n53 | - Benefit: **centralShare ₹300/month** (ages 40–79); stateTopUp for Assam = null (unconfirmed → show \"plus any state top-up; confirm locally\").\n54 | - Mandatory (provisional list; the source has no official document list): `aadhaar`, `death_certificate` (husband), `bank_passbook`; BPL proof. Mark the list as \"commonly required; confirm locally\".\n55 | - Checks: age window (verified → can say not_eligible); `bpl_household` question (no → not_eligible); name consistency across aadhaar and bank_passbook; death certificate present (missing → action_required).\n56 | - Age below 40 → not_eligible. Age 80+ → not_eligible for the widow pension band here; suggest checking the Old Age Pension (a scheme the agent can onboard in the demo).\n57 | \n58 | ---\n59 | \n60 | ## Name consistency (core showcase, defensive version)\n61 | Output levels, from strongest to weakest:\n62 | - `same`: identical after formatting normalisation (case, punctuation, extra spaces, joined vs spaced words, honorifics SHRI/SMT/MR/MRS/KUMARI/LATE). → check `ok`.\n63 | - `possible_match`: differs only by known abbreviations or variants (KR↔KUMAR, MD/MOHD↔MOHAMMAD, DEVI↔DEBI, SK↔SHEIKH), transliteration (Devanagari/Assamese/Bengali script ↔ Latin), or Jaro-Winkler ≥ 0.85 on the normalised form. → `needs_verification`: \"These look like the same person written differently. Officials may still reject this. Ask the bank/Aadhaar centre to make them identical.\"\n64 | - `initials_only`: \"R. KUMAR\" vs \"RAMESH KUMAR\" → `needs_verification`.\n65 | - `mismatch`: below that threshold (e.g. \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\", \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\") → `action_required`, saying which document to correct and where.\n66 | - The score is shown for transparency but **never** turns a check into `ok` by itself; only `same` does.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\activeContext.md","result":" 1 | # Active context\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Current state\n 4 | T0 (Memory Bank + scaffold) is done. The app is a placeholder home page titled \"KaagazCheck: कागज़ चेक\" with a smoke test.\n 5 | \n 6 | ## Decisions made in T0\n 7 | - Next.js 16.4.0 scaffolded in a temp folder and copied in (create-next-app refuses non-empty folders).\n 8 | - `src/` layout with `@/*` alias. Core logic lives in `src/core/`; the smoke test (`src/core/appInfo.test.ts`) also exercises zod and fast-check.\n 9 | - Vitest runs in the Node environment on `src/**/*.test.ts`; no jsdom or React rendering tests yet.\n10 | - `.gitignore`: ignores `.env`, `.env*.local`, `node_modules`, `.next`, `*.db`; keeps `.env.example` and `.cline-runs/` tracked.\n11 | - `<html lang=\"hi\">`; page title and description come from `src/core/appInfo.ts`.\n12 | \n13 | ## Next step\n14 | T1, core engine (`tasks/backlog/T1-core-engine.md`): schemas, name matcher, rule engine for the 3 schemes, tests first.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\productContext.md","result":" 1 | # Product context: KaagazCheck (कागज़ चेक)\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Why it exists\n 4 | Eligible people lose welfare benefits *after* applying: names that don't match across Aadhaar, bank and land records; Aadhaar not seeded to the bank; missing certificates; rejection reasons they cannot understand. Documented example: Rajasthan 2019, only about 23% of registered PM-KISAN farmers got the 3rd instalment, mostly because of Aadhaar name mismatches (sources in `docs/research.md`).\n 5 | \n 6 | KaagazCheck checks the papers **before** the person applies, in Hindi (English toggle), and says exactly what to fix and where.\n 7 | \n 8 | ## Users\n 9 | - **Citizen** (farmer, widow, woman of an Assam household) on a basic Android phone, often helped by a family member or a CSC/NGO helper.\n10 | - **Admin / helper organisation** that adds new schemes through the Scheme Onboarding Agent (Cline SDK).\n11 | \n12 | ## Schemes in scope\n13 | PM-KISAN (`pm-kisan`) · Orunodoi 3.0, Assam (`orunodoi-3`) · Widow Pension IGNWPS (`nsap-widow`). Rules are in `docs/schemes.md`.\n14 | \n15 | ## Main demo path (3 minutes)\n16 | 1. Pick a scheme.\n17 | 2. Add documents: upload a photo or tap \"Use sample\" (clean / name mismatch / missing document).\n18 | 3. AI reads each document → fields + confidence; low confidence → the user confirms or edits.\n19 | 4. Answer 3–5 yes/no exclusion questions.\n20 | 5. Rule engine → readiness report: 🟢 ready / 🔴 fix first / ⚪ not eligible / 🟡 confirm at office, each check with a reason, where to fix it and its source.\n21 | 6. Hindi report + printable cover sheet.\n22 | \n23 | ## Second mode\n24 | Rejection decoder (T4): rejection text → known reason code → fix steps + a drafted Hindi letter.\n25 | \n26 | ## Cline inside the product\n27 | Scheme Onboarding Agent (T3): guideline text → rule definition + test cases → run through the rule engine → **pending** proposal → a human approves.\n28 | \n29 | ## Trust rules\n30 | - The AI only reads and explains; the rule engine decides.\n31 | - Aadhaar is masked to the last 4 digits everywhere; uploaded images are processed in memory, never stored.\n32 | - When unsure, say \"confirm at the office\"; never guess.\n33 | - Demo uses fake sample documents only.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\progress.md","result":" 1 | # Progress\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Done\n 4 | - [x] T0: Memory Bank (productContext, systemPatterns, techContext, activeContext, progress), Next.js 16 scaffold (TS strict, Tailwind v4, ESLint), Vitest + fast-check + zod, npm scripts, `.gitignore`, `.env.example`, placeholder page, smoke test, first commit.\n 5 | \n 6 | ## To do\n 7 | - [ ] T1: core engine (types/zod schemas, name matcher, rule engine, 3 schemes, sample sets)\n 8 | - [ ] T2: main flow (scheme picker → documents → questions → readiness report, Hindi/English, AI gateway `src/server/ai.ts`)\n 9 | - [ ] T3: Scheme Onboarding Agent (`@cline/sdk`, `node:sqlite` store, admin approval)\n10 | - [ ] T4: rejection decoder + polish\n11 | - [ ] T-last: freeze, full README, demo path check\n12 | \n13 | ## Known issues\n14 | - `npm install` warns that eslint@9 is deprecated (it is the version create-next-app 16.4.0 pins with eslint-config-next). Harmless for now.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\projectbrief.md","result":" 1 | # Project brief v2: KaagazCheck (কাগজ চেক): \"Check before you apply\"\n 2 | \n 3 | **Pitch:** We help citizens catch preventable welfare-application mistakes *before* they submit their papers. Our AI reads the documents; a tested rule engine checks them; and Cline maintains the rules as official guidelines change.\n 4 | **Promise:** we reduce preventable errors. We do NOT guarantee approval; the department decides.\n 5 | \n 6 | ## Problem (evidence: docs/research.md)\n 7 | Eligible people lose benefits after applying because their documents disagree (name spellings across Aadhaar, bank and land record; account not Aadhaar-seeded; missing papers) or because rejections arrive unexplained. In Rajasthan in 2019, only about 23% of registered PM-KISAN farmers got the third instalment, mostly due to name mismatches with Aadhaar.\n 8 | \n 9 | ## Users\n10 | - **Citizen** in Assam on a basic Android phone, often helped by family or a CSC/NGO helper.\n11 | - **Rule maintainer** (helper organisation) who keeps scheme rules current using the Cline agent.\n12 | \n13 | ## Languages\n14 | **Assamese (default) + English toggle.** All fixed UI and check messages live in one `src/i18n.ts` with `as` and `en` keys. Assamese strings must be reviewed by a native speaker before the demo (Das will arrange). AI-generated Assamese (letters, explanations) is labelled \"draft\".\n15 | \n16 | ## Scope: rule packs v2 (docs/schemes.md is the source of truth)\n17 | - **PM-KISAN: flagship, full end-to-end demo** (name mismatch, missing document, uncertain bank status).\n18 | - **Orunodoi 3.0: Assam relevance, verified rules only;** provisional criteria → \"needs verification\".\n19 | - **Widow Pension (IGNWPS): simple, reliable rules** (age 40–79, BPL, central ₹300/month).\n20 | \n21 | ## Architecture (the trust design)\n22 | 1. **Document reader (AI):** extracts names, dates, account details and document type, with per-field confidence. Low confidence → user confirms.\n23 | 2. **Deterministic rule engine (pure code, tested):** normalises fields, checks consistency, evaluates verified criteria, finds missing documents. Provisional rules and external facts → `needs_verification`.\n24 | 3. **Two separate verdicts:** *Eligibility* (likely_eligible / not_eligible / needs_verification) and *Readiness* (ready_for_review / action_required / needs_verification).\n25 | 4. **Next steps:** corrections, where to go (bank, Aadhaar centre, circle office, Gram Panchayat), the source of each rule, and a printable cover sheet.\n26 | 5. **Explainer (AI, optional):** friendly wording in Assamese, built only from the engine's result.\n27 | \n28 | ## Core loop (must-ship demo path)\n29 | Pick scheme → add documents (photo, or \"Use sample A/B/C\") → confirm low-confidence fields → answer yes/no questions → two-verdict report with fixes and sources → print cover sheet.\n30 | Samples (clearly fake): A = clean PM-KISAN farmer → ready_for_review (+ bank seeding needs verification). B = PM-KISAN with \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" (bank, possible match) and \"Rameshwar Sharma\" (land record, mismatch) → action_required. C = widow pension, missing death certificate → action_required.\n31 | \n32 | ## Cline inside the product: Rule Maintenance Agent (T3)\n33 | Upload or paste a scheme notice → a `@cline/sdk` Agent proposes a **rule change** (new scheme or edited rule, with provenance: sourceUrl, clause, effectiveDate) → writes **regression tests** → runs them through the engine (existing scheme tests must still pass) → shows a diff + test results → a **human approves** → a new rule-pack version is published (old version kept). Demo inputs: `docs/sample-guideline-ignoaps.md` (add a scheme) and `docs/sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md` (a one-time payment change, to show versioning).\n34 | \n35 | ## Trust rules (non-negotiable)\n36 | - AI reads and explains; **code decides**. No eligibility claim from model text.\n37 | - Aadhaar masked to the last 4 digits everywhere; images processed in memory, never stored.\n38 | - Every rule carries provenance; provisional → needs_verification.\n39 | - A fuzzy name score never equals identity.\n40 | - Demo uses fake documents only.\n41 | \n42 | ## Data model sketch\n43 | - `RuleProvenance` { sourceUrl, sourceTitle, clause?, effectiveDate?, verifiedOn, version, status: verified | provisional }\n44 | - `SchemeRule` { id, version, nameEn, nameAs, benefit { centralShare, stateTopUp|null, period, effectiveDate, provenance }, mandatoryDocs[], supportingDocs[], ageRange?, eligibilityAnyOf[], exclusions[], externalChecks[], each criterion with provenance }\n45 | - `ExtractedDoc` { docType, fields { name, dob, aadhaarLast4, accountNo, ifsc, ownerNames[], husbandName, ... }, confidence per field }\n46 | - `Report` { eligibility, readiness, checks[], schemeVersion, asOfDate }\n47 | - DB (T3): `rule_versions` { schemeId, version, ruleJson, testsJson, testResultJson, status: proposed | published | rejected, sourceNote, createdAt, approvedAt }\n48 | \n49 | ## Stretch\n50 | Assamese voice readout (only if the browser has an as-IN voice; otherwise hide) · rejection-notice decoder (T4) · helper mode for many applicants.\n51 | \n52 | ## Cut list (cut in this order)\n53 | Voice → decoder letter drafting → real photo upload (keep samples + manual entry) → cover-sheet styling. **Never cut:** the two-verdict report, provenance, the Cline agent with tests + approval.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\README.md","result":"1 | # Memory Bank\n2 | Empty until the problem statement is released. In task T0, Cline creates:\n3 | `projectbrief.md` (from Claude's brief), `productContext.md`, `systemPatterns.md`, `techContext.md`, `activeContext.md`, `progress.md`.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\systemPatterns.md","result":" 1 | # System patterns\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Core pipeline: AI reads → rule engine decides → AI explains\n 4 | 1. **Read (AI):** a document image or text → `ExtractedDoc` (fields + per-field confidence), validated with zod.\n 5 | 2. **Decide (pure code):** `src/core/` rule engine takes `SchemeRule` + `ExtractedDoc[]` + `Answers` → `ReadinessReport`. Deterministic, unit- and property-tested. No model text ever decides a status.\n 6 | 3. **Explain (AI, optional):** friendlier wording or a letter draft, built only from the report's codes and fields.\n 7 | \n 8 | ## Module boundaries\n 9 | - `src/core/`: pure TypeScript (name matching, rule engine, schemas, scheme definitions). No React, network, DB or `process.env` imports.\n10 | - `src/server/ai.ts`: the **only** module that calls a model. Structured JSON output → zod validation → one retry → safe fallback. Logs the model, prompt version and an input hash (never raw personal data).\n11 | - `src/server/store.ts`: the **only** database writer (`node:sqlite`, WAL mode, transactions). Used from T3 for `scheme_proposals`.\n12 | - `src/server/agent/`: `@cline/sdk` Scheme Onboarding Agent (T3); its tools call into `src/core`.\n13 | - `src/app/api/**/route.ts`: thin route handlers with `export const runtime = \"nodejs\"`.\n14 | - `src/app/**`: UI. Calls API routes, never the model directly.\n15 | \n16 | ## Status vocabulary\n17 | - Check: `pass | fail | uncertain`.\n18 | - Report: `ready | fix_first | not_eligible | confirm_at_office`.\n19 | - Unclear rule or data → `uncertain` / `confirm_at_office`.\n20 | \n21 | ## Name matching (showcase)\n22 | Normalise (uppercase, strip punctuation, collapse spaces, drop honorifics) → expand abbreviations → Devanagari→Latin → Jaro-Winkler. Pass ≥ 0.92, uncertain 0.80–0.92, fail < 0.80. Initials never pass. See `docs/schemes.md`.\n23 | \n24 | ## Privacy\n25 | Aadhaar masked to the last 4 digits in UI, logs and printouts. Images are processed in memory only. Collect the minimum personal data.\n26 | \n27 | ## Testing\n28 | Vitest; fast-check property tests for the matcher and engine. Write tests first for core logic.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\memory-bank\\techContext.md","result":" 1 | # Tech context\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Stack\n 4 | - One Next.js 16 app (App Router, `src/` dir, `@/*` → `./src/*`), React 19, TypeScript strict, Tailwind CSS v4 (via `@tailwindcss/turbopack` loader in `next.config.ts`).\n 5 | - Validation: zod 4.\n 6 | - Tests: Vitest 5 (Node environment, `src/**/*.test.ts`) + fast-check 4.\n 7 | - Lint: ESLint 9 flat config (`eslint-config-next`); `next lint` no longer exists in Next 16.\n 8 | - Database (T3): built-in `node:sqlite` (WAL); fall back to `better-sqlite3` only if needed. `node:sqlite` loads on this machine.\n 9 | - AI: Cline API (OpenAI-compatible), base URL `https://api.cline.bot/api/v1`, all calls via `src/server/ai.ts`.\n10 | - In-product agent (T3): `@cline/sdk` `Agent`, `providerId: \"openai-compatible\"`, tools via `createTool` with zod schemas.\n11 | \n12 | ## Environment\n13 | - Windows, PowerShell 5.1; the path contains a space (`C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build`). Use npm scripts and cross-platform Node code.\n14 | - Node v24.21.0, npm 11.19.0, git 2.55.0.\n15 | - Secrets only in `.env.local` (git-ignored): `CLINE_API_KEY`, `CLINE_MODEL` (e.g. `anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5`, never a `:batch` model). The template is `.env.example`.\n16 | - Write files containing Hindi with the editor tool (UTF-8), not PowerShell redirection.\n17 | \n18 | ## npm scripts\n19 | | Script | Command |\n20 | |---|---|\n21 | | `dev` | `next dev` |\n22 | | `build` | `next build` |\n23 | | `start` | `next start` |\n24 | | `lint` | `eslint .` |\n25 | | `typecheck` | `tsc --noEmit` |\n26 | | `test` | `vitest run` |\n27 | | `test:watch` | `vitest` |\n28 | \n29 | ## Gotchas found in T0\n30 | - Vitest 5 needs `@types/node` ≥ 22 (peer dep), so `@types/node` is `^22`.\n31 | - The Vitest config is `vitest.config.mts` (ESM); a `.ts` config triggers a Vite \"configLoader native\" warning because `package.json` has no `\"type\": \"module\"`.\n32 | - The global `LayoutProps<\"/\">` type only exists after `next build`/`next dev` generate `.next/types`, so `layout.tsx` uses an explicit `Readonly<{ children: React.ReactNode }>` to keep `npm run typecheck` working on a clean checkout.\n33 | - Vite writes warnings to stderr, which PowerShell reports as `NativeCommandError`. Check the real exit code (e.g. run via `cmd /c ... & echo %ERRORLEVEL%`).\n34 | - `npm install` is slow here (about 5 minutes for a fresh install); long commands can exceed the tool timeout, so run them in the background with logs.\n35 | \n36 | ## Scaffold notes\n37 | Generated with `create-next-app@16.4.0` in a temp folder and copied in (it refuses non-empty folders). Defaults kept: `cacheComponents: true`, `partialPrefetching: true`. `AGENTS.md` is written by Next (`next dev` re-adds it), so it is committed.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\LOG.md","result":" 1 | # Build log (Cline appends after every task)\n 2 | \n 3 | ## T0: scaffold + memory bank (run-133358-c1ef, 2026-10-10)\n 4 | Followed the plan from run-133031-4cma.\n 5 | \n 6 | **Files created/changed**\n 7 | - Memory Bank: `memory-bank/productContext.md`, `systemPatterns.md`, `techContext.md`, `activeContext.md`, `progress.md` (`projectbrief.md` untouched).\n 8 | - Scaffold (create-next-app@16.4.0 in a temp folder, copied in): `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `tsconfig.json` (strict), `next.config.ts`, `eslint.config.mjs` (+ ignores for `.cline-runs/`, `docs/`, `memory-bank/`, `tasks/`), `next-env.d.ts`, `AGENTS.md`, `public/*`, `src/app/{layout.tsx,page.tsx,globals.css,favicon.ico}`.\n 9 | - New: `src/core/appInfo.ts` (title/taglines, pure), `src/core/appInfo.test.ts` (smoke test, also exercises zod + fast-check), `vitest.config.mts`, `.env.example` (`CLINE_API_KEY=`, `CLINE_MODEL=`), `README.md` (short placeholder), `.gitignore` (ignores `.env`, `.env*.local`, `node_modules`, `.next`, `*.db*`; keeps `.env.example` and `.cline-runs/`).\n10 | - Deps added: `zod@^4.6.5`, dev `vitest@^5.0.3`, `fast-check@^4.10.2`, `@types/node@^22`.\n11 | - Scripts: `dev`, `build`, `start`, `lint` (`eslint .`), `typecheck` (`tsc --noEmit`), `test` (`vitest run`), `test:watch`.\n12 | \n13 | **Results**\n14 | - `npm test`: 1 file, 3 passed, 0 failed.\n15 | - `npm run typecheck`: pass. `npm run lint`: pass (0 problems). `npm run build`: pass (Next 16.4.0, Turbopack; `/` prerendered static).\n16 | - Verified the built HTML contains \"KaagazCheck: कागज़ चेक\" and `lang=\"hi\"`, and the CSS contains the Tailwind utilities used.\n17 | \n18 | **Deviations from the plan**\n19 | - `@types/node` bumped from `^20` to `^22` (Vitest 5 peer requirement; matches Node 22+).\n20 | - Vitest config is `.mts` instead of `.ts` (avoids a Vite ESM/CJS config warning).\n21 | - `layout.tsx` uses explicit `Readonly<{ children: React.ReactNode }>` instead of the generated global `LayoutProps<\"/\">`, so `typecheck` passes before `.next/types` exists.\n22 | - No `postcss.config.mjs`: create-next-app 16.4 wires Tailwind v4 via the `@tailwindcss/turbopack` loader in `next.config.ts`; Tailwind confirmed working in the build.\n23 | \n24 | **Open issues**\n25 | - npm warns `eslint@9` is deprecated (pinned by eslint-config-next 16.4.0) and that `unrs-resolver` has an unapproved postinstall script; neither blocks lint or build.\n26 | - `docs/sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md` appeared in `docs/` during this run (not created by Cline); committed as-is with `git add -A`.\n27 | \n28 | **Requests for Claude**\n29 | - None blocking. Please confirm that `docs/sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md` is intended for T4 (rejection decoder) and reference it in the task spec if so.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\README.md","result":"1 | # tasks/\n2 | - `NEXT.md` — the one task Cline does next (written by Claude).\n3 | - `backlog/` — the day's tasks. T0 and T-last are ready; Claude writes T1–T4 after reading the problem statement.\n4 | - `LOG.md` — Cline's report after each task.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\tasks\\backlog\\T1-core-engine.md","result":" 1 | # T1: Core rule engine v2, tests first (about 75 min)\n 2 | \n 3 | Mode: ACT. Pure TypeScript in `src/core/` only: no UI, network, AI or DB imports. **Read `docs/schemes.md` (rule packs v2) first; it is the source of truth.**\n 4 | \n 5 | 1. `src/core/types.ts` + zod schemas: `RuleProvenance`, `SchemeRule` (with `version`), `ExtractedDoc`, `Answers`, `Check`, `Report` (two verdicts: `eligibility`, `readiness`), as in memory-bank/projectbrief.md.\n 6 | 2. `src/core/schemes/pm-kisan.ts`, `orunodoi-3.ts`, `nsap-widow.ts`: data validated by `SchemeRule`. Every criterion and benefit has provenance with `status: verified | provisional` exactly as docs/schemes.md marks it. Question text in Assamese + English. Registry: `getScheme(id)`, `listSchemes()`, `registerScheme(rule)` (used by T3).\n 7 | 3. `src/core/nameMatch.ts` → `{ level: same | possible_match | initials_only | mismatch, score }` per docs/schemes.md. Normalisation, honorifics, abbreviations, transliteration of Devanagari and Assamese/Bengali script to Latin (basic table), Jaro-Winkler.\n 8 | 4. `src/core/age.ts`: age on an explicit `asOfDate`.\n 9 | 5. `src/core/aadhaar.ts`: `maskAadhaar()` → `XXXX XXXX 1234`.\n10 | 6. `src/core/benefit.ts`: `benefitOn(scheme, date)`, supporting time-bounded overrides (needed for the T3 demo).\n11 | 7. `src/core/evaluate.ts`: `evaluate(scheme, docs, answers, asOfDate) → Report`. Rules:\n12 |    - verified criterion failed → eligibility `not_eligible`\n13 |    - provisional criterion triggered → `needs_verification` (never not_eligible)\n14 |    - missing mandatory doc or a name `mismatch` → readiness `action_required`\n15 |    - `possible_match`, `initials_only`, low-confidence field, or any external check (bank seeding, account active, eKYC, land verified) → `needs_verification`\n16 |    - otherwise `ready_for_review`\n17 |    - Each check has `messageEn`, `messageAs`, `fixWhere`, `provenance`.\n18 | 8. `src/core/rejectionCodes.ts`: enum (`NAME_MISMATCH_AADHAAR`, `AADHAAR_NOT_SEEDED_BANK`, `INVALID_ACCOUNT_IFSC`, `ACCOUNT_INACTIVE`, `LAND_NOT_VERIFIED`, `EKYC_PENDING`, `PENDING_STATE_ACTION`, `UNKNOWN`) → fix steps in English + Assamese.\n19 | \n20 | **Tests (write first):**\n21 | - nameMatch: \"Ramesh Kumar\" vs \"RAMESH KUMAR \" → same; \"RAMESHKUMAR\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" → same; \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" vs \"SUNITA DEBI\" → possible_match; \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" → possible_match; \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" → initials_only; \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" → mismatch; \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" → mismatch.\n22 | - fast-check: a name is `same` as itself; level is symmetric; `maskAadhaar` never outputs more than 4 digits; **possible_match never yields an `ok` check**.\n23 | - evaluate, PM-KISAN: clean → ready_for_review with bank seeding as needs_verification; bank \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" → needs_verification; land \"Rameshwar Sharma\" → action_required (fixWhere circle office); missing land_record → action_required; income tax yes → not_eligible; large landholding has NO effect (regression test for the removed 2-ha limit).\n24 | - evaluate, widow: age 39 → not_eligible; 40 → likely_eligible; 79 → likely_eligible; 80 → not_eligible; benefit centralShare 300; stateTopUp null.\n25 | - evaluate, Orunodoi: no ration card → action_required; four-wheeler yes → needs_verification (not not_eligible).\n26 | - benefitOn: Orunodoi with a 2026-10 override → 1500 in October, 1250 in November.\n27 | \n28 | **Done when:** all tests pass, typecheck clean, LOG.md updated, committed \"T1: core rule engine v2\".","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\PS.md","result":" 1 | # Cline AI Builder Hackathon: Round 2 Problem Statement\n 2 | \n 3 | India has problems that millions of people live with every day. What would you build with AI to solve one of them?\n 4 | \n 5 | India is building at a scale few countries have ever attempted, yet for hundreds of millions of people daily life still runs into the same walls. A farmer cannot tell what is wrong with his crop until the damage is done. A patient from a small town travels for hours to meet a doctor who has only a few minutes to give her. A student studies from notes written in a language she does not think in. A citizen loses months to a government scheme he qualified for but never heard about.\n 6 | \n 7 | These are not rare cases. They repeat across healthcare, education, agriculture, governance, finance, safety and climate, and across languages, incomes and literacy levels. AI can finally make a real dent: it understands Indian languages and voice, reads messy documents, reasons over local data and can reach anyone with a basic smartphone.\n 8 | \n 9 | **The challenge:** pick one real problem that Indian society faces and, in 8 hours, build an AI-powered solution for it, using Cline as your AI coding partner from the first commit to the last. You can also build Cline into the solution itself, for example with the Cline SDK. Not a slide deck, but a working product that a real person could use tomorrow.\n10 | \n11 | The problem is yours to choose. Healthcare, education, agriculture, governance, finance, safety and climate are only some examples, so look at the problems around you and pick one that matters. For example, potholes are a problem on roads across India, and one solution is a dashcam in a car with AI that spots potholes as it drives and prepares the complaint automatically.\n12 | \n13 | ## Deliverables\n14 | - **Codebase on GitHub (with README):** a public repository with a clear README covering the problem, your solution, architecture, tech stack, setup steps and how Cline was used in the build.\n15 | - **PPT:** a presentation covering the problem, your solution, the architecture, the AI models and tools involved, how Cline was used (in the build and inside the solution), challenges faced and future scope.\n16 | - **Demo video (mandatory):** a short walkthrough of your working solution with a real user scenario, showing Cline in action during the build.\n17 | - **Social media post (brownie points):** share your build, demo, experience or participation shoutout, and tag Cline.\n18 | \n19 | ## Evaluation criteria\n20 | Creativity & uniqueness · Innovation · Real-world usefulness · Effective use of Cline · Product usability & overall execution · Technical implementation. **Bonus** for solutions that use Cline inside the product itself, not only to build it.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\README.md","result":"1 | # docs/\n2 | - `PS.md`: the Round 2 problem statement (owned by Claude and the user; Cline does not edit).\n3 | - `research.md`: evidence and pitch for README and slides.\n4 | - `schemes.md`: **rule packs v2**, the source of truth for the rule engine (provenance, verified vs provisional).\n5 | - `sample-guideline-ignoaps.md`: demo input for the Rule Maintenance Agent (add a new scheme).\n6 | - `sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md`: demo input for the Rule Maintenance Agent (a versioned temporary change).","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\research.md","result":" 1 | # Research summary (for README and slides)\n 2 | \n 3 | ## Why not a \"scheme finder\"\n 4 | Saturated: the government's myScheme portal already finds schemes, and Microsoft + AI4Bharat launched Jugalbandi, a voice WhatsApp scheme bot in Indian languages, in 2023 (https://techcrunch.com/2023/05/24/microsoft-ai-chatgpt-reaches-rural-india/).\n 5 | \n 6 | ## The real gap: people lose benefits AFTER applying\n 7 | - **Rajasthan, PM-KISAN (2019):** only about 23% of about 51 lakh registered farmers received the third instalment; names on applications did not match Aadhaar. Under 1% were paid in Ganganagar district. Source: Hindustan Times, https://www.pressreader.com/india/hindustan-times-st-jaipur/20191007/281586652343870\n 8 | - **Andhra Pradesh, PM-KISAN (LibTech India, Jan 2022):** about 15.2 lakh farmers missed instalments, about ₹1,343 crore pending; 47% bank rejections, 51% \"action required by the state\" with no reason given; only 12.5% of 33,526 studied cases were resolved, some pending for 1,060+ days. Source: https://libtech.in/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/‘PM-KISAN-Samman-Nidhi-AP-25th-January.pdf\n 9 | - **Documents as the gate (Haqdarshak):** a cited study attributes 20% of beneficiary exclusion to documentation issues; 45% of surveyed drivers had trouble submitting forms; middlemen charged ₹150–300 for a card priced at ₹35. Advocacy source. https://yojanacard.haqdarshak.com/?p=9509\n10 | \n11 | Present these as documented examples (old and regional), not current national totals.\n12 | \n13 | ## Pitch\n14 | \"He qualified. He applied. He got nothing, because of one letter in his name. KaagazCheck catches that before he applies.\"","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\sample-guideline-ignoaps.md","result":" 1 | # Sample input for the Scheme Onboarding Agent (demo)\n 2 | \n 3 | *Paste this text into the admin \"Add a scheme\" page. Summary of the Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme (IGNOAPS) under NSAP. Source: https://en.vikaspedia.in/viewcontent/social-welfare/social-security/pension-benefits-under-nsap?lgn=en*\n 4 | \n 5 | ---\n 6 | \n 7 | Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme (IGNOAPS), a component of the National Social Assistance Programme, Ministry of Rural Development.\n 8 | \n 9 | Eligibility: The applicant must be aged 60 years or above and must belong to a household living below the poverty line (BPL) according to the criteria prescribed by the Government of India.\n10 | \n11 | Central assistance: Rs. 200 per month for beneficiaries aged 60 to 79 years, and Rs. 500 per month for beneficiaries aged 80 years and above. State governments are urged to contribute an additional amount.\n12 | \n13 | Documents generally required: Aadhaar card (age proof), BPL proof, bank account passbook.","success":true},{"query":"C:\\Dev\\cline hackathon\\build\\docs\\sample-notice-orunodoi-oct2026.md","result":" 1 | # Sample input for the Rule Maintenance Agent: a temporary payment change (demo)\n 2 | \n 3 | *Demo notice modelled on a reported October 2026 change (The Economic Times report of a one-time ₹1,500 Orunodoi payment for that month). Before quoting on slides, confirm against the official Government of Assam notification.*\n 4 | \n 5 | ---\n 6 | \n 7 | Orunodoi: For the month of October 2026, eligible beneficiaries will receive a one-time amount of Rs. 1,500 instead of the regular Rs. 1,250. From November 2026, the regular monthly amount of Rs. 1,250 continues.\n 8 | \n 9 | ---\n10 | \n11 | Expected agent behaviour: propose a new version of `orunodoi-3` with a time-bounded benefit override (effective 2026-10-01 to 2026-10-31, amount 1500), keep the recurring 1250, mark provenance as provisional until an official notice URL is added, write regression tests (October date → 1500, November date → 1250, eligibility checks unchanged), run all existing tests, and wait for human approval.","success":true}],"durationMs":21}}
