{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:23:11.588Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"With full context now, I'm planning the update: first refreshing the memory bank docs to v2, then updating appInfo.ts with the Assamese app name \"কাগজ চেক\", bilingual title, and taglines in both English and Assamese.\n\nI need to update the test first and set the layout's lang attribute to \"as\". Then I'm designing the core type system with Zod schemas covering rule status, provenance metadata with source tracking and verification dates, localized bilingual text, and document type enums, while checking which Zod 4 API variants (like date and URL validators) are available in the installed version.\n\nNow I'm working through the Benefit schema, including overrides with date ranges, amounts, and provenance, plus a benefitOn function that resolves the applicable benefit amount for a given date -- handling both regular central/state shares and special override cases like Orunodoi's flat amount.\n\nI'm also thinking about how to never present an unconfirmed total by computing it only when both components are known. Then I move to criteria structures for scheme eligibility — question-based criteria that trigger exclusion based on a specific answer value, and age-range criteria for min/max bounds.\n\nFor the schema, I'm sketching a simplified representation with an `exclusions` array (yes-answers that disqualify) and a `requirements` array (no-answers that disqualify), each carrying bilingual question text, provenance, and an optional reason message.\n\nFor the widow case specifically, I'm treating it as a requirement tied to a death certificate rather than a standalone question, alongside the BPL household check. For Orunodoi's provisional eligibility group, if every question in that group is answered no, the result should fall back to needs_verification rather than a hard rejection, and any unanswered question anywhere should likewise push the status toward needs_verification with a prompt to answer rather than assuming ineligibility.\n\nNow I'm designing the ExtractedDoc structure to hold document type, extracted fields like names, dates, account numbers, and IFSC codes, plus a per-field confidence score between 0 and 1. Fields below 0.8 confidence trigger needs_verification until the user confirms them, bumping confidence to 1, and for privacy I'm only storing the last four digits of Aadhaar numbers via a masking helper.\n\nFor each scheme, I'm setting up mandatory and supporting document lists with provenance, labels, and fix locations -- mandatory docs are strict (missing ones mark readiness as action_required), while supporting docs appear as a checklist users should confirm locally, as with the widow scheme's provisional death certificate requirement.\n\nI'll treat bpl_proof as a mandatory doc too, so missing it reasonably triggers action_required. For name consistency checks, I'm deciding to anchor comparisons on aadhaar when present, since it's the most reliable reference document, and only fall back to ration_card for schemes like Orunodoi where aadhaar might not be central.\n\nFor land records specifically, since ownership might be listed under a family member rather than the applicant, I need to compare the applicant's name against each owner name entry and take the best match level rather than a strict single comparison. Each pairing between the anchor document and other submitted docs becomes its own named check with a result level and a suggested fix location — bank branch for passbook mismatches, circle office for land record mismatches, and so on.\n\nI'll simplify: keep a single name check against land record owners, with a fallback to needs_verification if no owner name is readable at all, and the mismatch message should note that a family member (husband/wife) could apply instead or get the record mutated at the circle office. Then I'll add document-level field checks for PM-KISAN like IFSC format and account number length, using a small set of validators keyed by field kind.\n\nFor these field checks, I'll treat a missing field as needs_verification (likely unreadable) rather than action_required, since the document should normally have it; an invalid format when confidence is high becomes action_required pointing to the bank branch, but if confidence is low on the field, that overrides everything and falls back to needs_verification to confirm manually.\n\nFor the low-confidence rule, any field within a relevant document's confidence map scoring below 0.8 triggers a needs_verification flag listing that field, with a message asking to confirm what was read — this applies to name mismatches too, since a low-confidence OCR read shouldn't be treated as a hard action_required failure.\n\nExternal checks always appear as needs_verification regardless of other findings, but this creates tension with the PM-KISAN clean case, which expects overall readiness of ready_for_review even though bank seeding (an external check) shows as needs_verification — so readiness can't simply be \"any needs_verification anywhere forces non-ready.\"\n\nI think the resolution is that external checks are reported separately as needs_verification items without downgrading the overall readiness verdict itself; readiness ready_for_review really just means the core document checks found no problems, while external check results are surfaced alongside but don't count against readiness.\n\nI'm still untangling whether a triggered provisional criterion should flag readiness too, since readiness's action_required bucket already covers unmet verified criteria—so maybe provisional rules should map to eligibility status only, keeping readiness distinct from eligibility logic.\n\nFor Orunodoi specifically, I'll treat supporting docs as a checklist field in the report rather than checks that downgrade readiness, and handle the eligibility criterion so a \"yes\" to any option counts as ok while all no's or unanswered map to needs_verification. I'm also considering giving checks a category field (document, name, field, eligibility, external, data_quality) so the eligibility verdict can be derived separately: any verified action_required in eligibility checks means not_eligible, otherwise needs_verification if any pending, otherwise likely_eligible.\n\nReadiness should factor in all checks except the external category, and eligibility checks do affect readiness since an unmet verified criterion matters — unanswered questions push both to needs_verification. I'm also tying eligibility to age derived from Aadhaar DOB: missing Aadhaar means the age check is needs_verification, which cascades to eligibility, and I'm working through widow test cases (age 39, missing death certificate, age 80+) to confirm doc issues don't affect eligibility while widow status itself becomes a verified requirement.\n\nFor the 80+ case, I'm settling on not_eligible with a suggestion pointing to Old Age Pension included in messageEn. For the check shape, I'm reconciling the field name mismatch between schemes.md's \"ruleProvenance\" and T1's \"provenance\" — going with provenance, and deciding it should be nullable since low-confidence data-quality checks won't always have a scheme rule backing them.\n\nActually, I'll simplify by making provenance required everywhere, falling back to the scheme's top-level provenance for data-quality checks that aren't tied to a specific rule. For fixWhere, I'm defining an enum covering bank branch, aadhaar centre, circle office, gram panchayat, CSC, PM-Kisan portal, ration office, registrar, block office, self, and none, with bilingual labels in a FIX_WHERE map — death certificates map to registrar.\n\nI'll allow fixWhere to be null for checks that already pass. I'm also adding an optional nameMatch field with score, level, and compared docs for transparency on name-matching checks, and sketching the overall report shape covering scheme info, eligibility, readiness, checks, checklist, benefit, and disclaimers.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the name normalization algorithm, being careful about how Indic script transliteration interacts with any Unicode normalization step.\n\nI'm working through the honorific-stripping logic — transliterating Devanagari/Bengali characters to Latin, uppercasing, stripping punctuation, tokenizing, then deciding exactly which honorific tokens (SHRI, SMT, KUMARI, LATE, etc.) to drop, while making sure a name isn't wiped out entirely if it's just an honorific alone.\n\nFor \"same,\" I'm defining it as comparing names after joining tokens with no spaces, so formatting differences like spacing collapse away, but any non-Latin script or transliteration pushes the match down to \"possible_match\" instead. I'm also considering how to handle Latin diacritics versus true script transliteration, and now moving into how name variant expansions (like KR→KUMAR or MD/MOHD/MOHAMMED) factor into possible_match scoring.\n\nI'm working through a Jaro-Winkler comparison on \"RAMESH\" vs \"SURESH\" to verify that genuinely different names score low enough to count as mismatches, while variant-expanded names with high similarity still qualify as possible matches.\n\nChecking \"RAMESHWARSHARMA\" vs \"RAMESHKUMARSHARMA\" next, computing the match window and stepping through character matches like R, A, M, E, S, H lining up, then working through the trickier remaining letters to see how many matches and transpositions occur.\n\nCounting 7 diffs total, giving transpositions t=3, which pushes Jaro-Winkler to about 0.901 — still above the 0.85 threshold despite being a bad match. This tells me compact-string JW is too lenient here, so I should switch to a token-based comparison approach instead.\n\nI'm thinking about requiring token count alignment for possible_match: cases like \"RAMESHWAR SHARMA\" vs \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMA\" have different token counts, so they shouldn't qualify. For something like \"SUNITA DEVI\" vs \"SUNITA\" with a missing surname, that's reasonably treated as a mismatch requiring action, since officials would reject a missing middle/last name. Now I'm considering per-token JW comparisons between individual name components.\n\nI'm designing a scoring approach: when token counts are equal, pair tokens in order (and also try a sorted pairing to catch swapped name order, which is common across documents), then take the minimum per-token JW score as the overall measure so that one weak token pulls down the whole match.\n\nFor unequal token counts, I need a different handling since a simple compact-string JW could misleadingly pass the threshold — the spec's own example (Rameshwar Sharma vs Ramesh Kumar Sharma) shows it should fail. So I'm leaning toward a token-alignment scheme where each token in the longer name finds its best match among the shorter name's tokens, with unmatched tokens penalized toward zero.\n\nThat approach is giving a bad high score, so I'm reconsidering: maybe possible_match should require equal token counts with every aligned pair scoring at least 0.85, and for unequal counts penalize by the ratio of matched to total tokens so an unmatched token like KUMAR drags the score down.\n\nA min-based score of 0 might look odd for cases like \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma,\" but I think it's fine — I could even report both the min score and per-token scores for transparency. I'll settle on score = minimum token similarity, rounded to 2 decimals, so the weakest matching part always drives the decision honestly.\n\nI'm checking transliteration variants with Jaro-Winkler: \"SUNITA\" vs \"SUNEETA\" likely scores around 0.9, a reasonable possible_match. Short tokens like \"RAM\" vs \"RAJ\" compute to roughly 0.844, safely under the 0.85 threshold, which seems right. I also need to handle initials — if one side has a single-letter token matching the first letter of a corresponding token on the other side, and the remaining tokens align, that should count as a match.\n\nFor equal token counts, I'm working out an alignment strategy: classify as same if the compacted/raw forms match exactly, possible_match if canonicalized or all token pairs score ≥0.85 without initials, initials_only if the alignment relies on initial-letter matches, otherwise mismatch. I'm trying in-order pairing first, then considering alternate pairings since initials could break simple sorting.\n\nI'm also checking that canonical compact equality should trigger possible_match even with different spellings like \"MOHD ALI\" vs \"MOHAMMADALI\", while differing token counts with non-equal compacts stay conservative mismatches. For scoring, I'll use the minimum token similarity within the chosen pairing as the displayed score.\n\nActually, simplest: score should be the minimum pairwise similarity across matched tokens, with unpaired tokens treated as zero similarity, so the score directly tracks the mismatch decision — if it's below 0.85, it's flagged as a mismatch, keeping the displayed percentage and verdict consistent.\n\nI'm mapping common abbreviation variants to canonical forms — KR/KUM/KMR to KUMAR, MD/MOHD/MOHAMMED etc. to MOHAMMAD, DEBI to DEVI, SK/SHAIKH to SHEIKH. I also need to think through honorifics like KUMARI and LATE, since these can appear as titles at the start of a name but also function as legitimate name parts elsewhere, so removal should probably only apply to leading tokens.\n\nWorking through the matching logic: names like \"Sunita Devi\" vs \"Sunita Debi\" should canonicalize to the same form and count as a possible match, similarly for \"Ramesh Kr Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\". For cases like \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar,\" I'm checking how the initial token compares against the full first name.\n\nI'm also verifying punctuation handling—whether marks like apostrophes or periods are replaced by spaces or removed entirely—since comparing compact (no-space) forms makes both approaches equivalent, as seen with \"D'Souza\" vs \"Dsouza.\" This confirms the same/compact/canonical equality checks remain symmetric after stripping honorifics.\n\nFor Jaro-Winkler itself, I'm worried standard greedy matching implementations might not be perfectly symmetric in rare transposition edge cases, even though the algorithm is theoretically symmetric. To guarantee symmetry regardless, I'll canonically order the pair (e.g., swap so the lexicographically smaller string comes first) before computing the score, making the result deterministic and symmetric.\n\nFor the permutation-based alignment, choosing the best-scoring bijection among all token pairings naturally produces symmetric results since the same set of possible pairings exists from either direction, including for initials and the capped in-order pairing when token count exceeds the permutation limit. I also want to verify the identity property — that any string matched against itself yields a \"same\" result — including edge cases like empty strings, punctuation-only strings, or names consisting only of honorifics.\n\nActually, I think \"possible_match\" covers formatting-only differences from transliteration, so I should define sameness as: compact form equal without transliteration first, and only fall back to transliteration/variant expansion if that fails, then canonical compare. So any string with at least one letter should match itself trivially under this rule, including edge cases with only honorifics.\n\nI need to decide what happens when a whole name reduces to an honorific like \"SHRI\" — it becomes empty, but that should still count as a valid self-match since it's just a name being compared to itself. For Indic script normalization, uppercasing is a no-op, but I need to be careful with NFD decomposition since Devanagari nukta characters and vowel signs behave differently than Latin diacritics.\n\nI'm restricting diacritic stripping to only the Latin combining range (U+0300–U+036F) so Indic vowel marks stay intact, and using a Unicode-aware regex to keep only letters and marks while replacing everything else with spaces. For transliteration, I'm working out schwa deletion rules for how Devanagari/Bengali consonants with inherent vowels get rendered in Latin script.\n\nChecking \"কুমার\" and other medial schwa cases confirms consistent rendering. Moving to Bengali/Assamese, I'm mapping their distinct letters like Assamese ৰ, ৱ, and the variant pronunciations of শ/ষ/স to appropriate Latin equivalents, keeping standard transliteration conventions like SH/S rather than phonetic 'x' sounds.\n\nI'm also handling anusvara, chandrabindu, visarga, nukta, and khanda ta diacritics, noting these minor variants (like SH vs S) still keep Jaro-Winkler similarity high enough. Now I'm setting up the transliteration tables mapping consonant code points across scripts.\n\nSince Devanagari and Bengali blocks share a parallel layout with a fixed offset, I can derive one table and shift it, handling exceptions like Assamese RA/WA, Bengali RRA/RHA/YYA, and khanda ta separately where positions diverge.\n\nI'm weighing how to romanize Bengali য — phonetically it's often J (as in Jogesh, Jadav) rather than Y, though the ya-phala conjunct form behaves differently in pronunciation. I'll keep it simple and map it to \"J\" in the base table rather than overcomplicating with positional rules.\n\nNow I'm building out the consonant mapping table by Unicode offset for the Devanagari block, assigning each codepoint its Roman transliteration equivalent (K, KH, G, GH, NG, CH, J, T, D, N, P, PH, etc.) across the standard consonant range.\n\nStill mapping Bengali-specific overrides and then moving through the independent vowel characters and vowel signs for both scripts, noting where Devanagari diverges with its own E/AI/O/AU forms.\n\nNow I'm working out how to handle anusvara, chandrabindu, visarga, virama, and nukta marks, deciding to drop unmappable ones, and sketching the transliteration algorithm's logic for tracking a pending schwa after each consonant before processing the next character.\n\nTesting cross-script matches like \"रमेश कुमार\" against \"Ramesh Kumar\" should yield possible matches via transliteration similarity. I also need to handle honorifics across scripts—श्री/শ্ৰী transliterating to SHRI, श्रीमती to SHRIMATI with abbreviation SMTI, and similar forms like স্বৰ্গীয় (late) that should be stripped before comparison.\n\nI should apply honorific detection on the transliterated form but keep the raw token for the compact comparison. My plan: tokenize the raw string into {raw, latin} pairs, drop leading tokens whose Latin form is an honorific, then build the compact raw string from remaining raw tokens and the canonical string from Latin tokens with variant expansion. I need to strip zero-width joiners/non-joiners before splitting, since those are format characters that would otherwise break tokens apart incorrectly, while viramas, nuktas, and vowel signs are marks that should stay attached to their base letters.\n\nFor diacritic handling, I realize transliteration must run on NFC-normalized text rather than NFD, since NFD would decompose conjunct vowel signs like Bengali ো into separate pieces and garble the output — so Latin diacritic stripping should use NFD+strip separately, while the actual translit step stays on NFC.\n\nFor Jaro-Winkler, I'll implement the standard version with a prefix limit of 4 and a scaling factor of 0.1, applying the boost unconditionally rather than gating it behind the typical 0.7 threshold, to keep things simple.\n\nNow I'm working out how to treat single-letter tokens as initials, defining a pairing rule for when two tokens should be considered an initial match.\n\nFor pair classification, I'm setting thresholds: identical canonical tokens count as exact matches, similarity scores above 0.85 count as close matches, initial pairings get their own category, and everything else fails. When checking bijections, any failed pair signals a mismatch, any initial pair without failures signals an initials-only match, and otherwise it's treated as a possible match, with the overall score computed as the minimum Jaro-Winkler similarity across all paired tokens—verifying this works reasonably even for low-scoring initial pairs like \"R\" vs \"RAMESH\". I'm now figuring out how to rank and select the best bijection among candidates.\n\nI'm walking through test cases to validate the classification logic: initial-based matches like \"R KUMAR\" vs \"RAMESH KR\" should resolve to initials-only, while completely different single-token names like \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" should correctly fail as mismatches. I also want to handle honorific or middle tokens like \"MD\" as expansions rather than failures, and I'm considering edge cases around possible matches.\n\nFor verifying this, I'm setting up a property-based test mapping each match level to its expected check result, ensuring the evaluator only returns \"ok\" when names are at the same level — testing this both as a standalone mapping function and through full PM-KISAN document evaluation with randomized name pairs.\n\nSeparately, I'm designing an age calculation function that parses date strings directly to sidestep timezone issues, computing age by comparing year difference and adjusting if the \"as of\" month/day falls before the birth month/day — including tricky leap-year edge cases like Feb 29 birthdays checked against Feb 28 in non-leap years.\n\nI should validate date inputs and throw a RangeError on malformed dates or if the birth date is after the reference date, with the evaluator catching such errors and falling back to \"needs_verification.\" The evaluator's reference date should be an explicit, injectable parameter defaulting to today's local date via a utility function, keeping the core logic pure. I'm also considering how to handle Aadhaar records that only specify year of birth, but deciding to stick with strict YYYY-MM-DD format and treat anything else as needing verification. Now I'm working out how to find the correct benefit amount for a given date by matching it against override date ranges using string comparison.\n\nFor the override test case, I'm constructing a test scheme that spreads in the original benefit object and adds an override array with a specific date range and zero central share but a 1500 state top-up — this way the base scheme stays untouched while the test exercises the override logic independently. I'm leaving benefit calculations before the scheme's effective date unhandled for now, and moving on to designing the rejection-code system: an enum mapped to bilingual titles and fix steps, with tests ensuring every code has non-empty English and Assamese guidance.\n\nNow I'm designing the scheme registry with functions to get, list, and register schemes, validating each registration against the schema and storing multiple versions per id in a map so I can retrieve the latest version or a specific one, while also considering whether registration should reject duplicate id-version pairs and whether a reset function is needed for test isolation.\n\nI'll implement this as a factory function creating a registry instance with get/list/register methods, plus export a default instance bound to module-level functions so tests can create fresh registries while the app uses a shared one. I also need to write Assamese text for the scheme strings, acknowledging it'll need native speaker review.\n\nSince the brief wants check messages centralized but T1 is scoped to src/core only, I'll place message templates with en/as keys in src/core/messages.ts rather than creating src/i18n.ts directly, noting this decision in the log so a later task can wire up the UI-facing i18n file. Fix-location labels go in the same messages file, and now I'm starting to draft the scheme data with provenance constants for each scheme.\n\nFor PM-KISAN I set up the source record pointing to the official portal with a verification date and version, plus a summary source from Vikaspedia. For each eligibility criterion I need to mark provenance as verified against the specific source clause, but I pause on fields like IFSC code format -- that's really governed by RBI standards, not the PM-KISAN source itself, so I should be careful not to misattribute that provenance.\n\nTo avoid fabricating an RBI URL, I decide to just reference the pmkisan.gov.in source with a clause noting that bank details including IFSC must be valid for DBT purposes. For versioning, I settle on a simple semver-style string like \"2.0.0\" to keep things consistent across the scheme and provenance records. Then I move on to setting up the official source reference for the Orunodoi scheme.\n\nFor Orunodoi, I'm noting the Dhemaji district government page as the verified official source, though its \"last updated\" date isn't quite the same as an effective date so I flag that as a clause. I add a secondary provisional source from publicservicesmap.in, and work out which documents are mandatory versus optional — ration card is verified mandatory, while aadhaar and bank passbook are only needed \"if provided.\" I also consider whether name-consistency checks across documents belong as a provisional eligibility rule or just an engine-level behavior note.\n\nSince provisional rules can't produce a definitive pass/fail, I decide a name mismatch should downgrade to \"needs_verification\" rather than \"action_required,\" while a name match stays \"ok\" since that's just a factual check, not a provisional judgment call. I settle on a general approach: whenever a rule's provenance is provisional, any action_required outcome gets softened to needs_verification with a message prompting the user to confirm at the office, and I think about whether this logic should extend to document requirements too.\n\nSo for Orunodoi, name mismatch becomes needs_verification since provenance is provisional there, while PM-KISAN's name match is verified since the source explicitly requires Aadhaar-matching names. For the widow scheme, since the source doesn't address name consistency directly, I'll treat that provenance as provisional too, so a mismatch there also becomes needs_verification rather than action_required.\n\nFor orunodoi, I'm working through the mandatory documents and eligibility logic—NFSA enrollment as a provisional precondition, and an any-of set covering widows, PWD members, AAY cardholders, and income under 2 lakh, alongside exclusions like elected representatives, professionals, teachers, government staff, pensioners, contractors, traders, and large landholders.\n\nI'm settling on exclusion IDs: elected_representative, practising_professional, a combined teacher_or_govt_staff category, contractor_or_trader, large_landholder, owns_four_wheeler, and other_state_cash_scheme — noting that the test references owns_four_wheeler specifically, and the large landholder threshold needs clarification since it depends on a government-set land limit.\n\nFor unanswered exclusion questions, I'm treating them as needs_verification since eligibility can't be confirmed either way — this applies broadly to Orunodoi's larger set of exclusion criteria compared to PM-KISAN's five. For the widow case, I'm confirming is_widow, bpl_household, and age range as verified requirements, with aadhaar and death certificate as provisional mandatory documents.\n\nI'm adding bank_passbook and bpl_proof as further provisional docs, checking name consistency between aadhaar and the bank passbook. I'm deciding to skip adding a bank-aadhaar-seeding check for widows since the spec doesn't list it for this scheme, even though it would be harmless to include — I'll stick to spec exactly rather than extrapolating. Similarly, I'm skipping any husband-name cross-check on the death certificate since that's not specified, and now I'm working out the widow benefit structure with a central share of 300 and no state top-up.\n\nFor PM-KISAN I'm setting the central share at 6000 per year with no state top-up, noting the three installments of 2,000, while Orunodoi gets no central share but a 1,250 monthly state top-up with a note about the October 2026 one-time payment not being recurring. I'm also listing PM-KISAN's external verification steps — aadhaar seeding and account status at the bank branch, e-KYC via the portal or CSC, and land verification through the \"Know Your Status\" check — along with the land ownership requirement that records show the applicant or a family member as owner.\n\nI'm working out the name-consistency logic: comparing land record owner names against the aadhaar anchor name, flagging mismatches as needing a fix at the circle office, and treating missing or unreadable names as needing verification rather than outright failure, since land could legitimately be in a family member's name.\n\nFor the anchor document, I'm making it scheme-specific — PM-KISAN uses aadhaar, bank passbook, and land record, while Orunodoi uses aadhaar, ration card, and bank passbook, picking whichever is present first as the anchor. Since ration cards list a head of household plus members, I'm generalizing the name extraction into a helper that pulls all possible names off a document — primary name, owner names, and member names — so the comparison works consistently across doc types.\n\nNow I'm figuring out which document to flag for correction when names mismatch: comparing against the Aadhaar anchor and pointing the user to fix the other document (bank branch for passbook, circle office for land record, ration office for ration card), or alternatively suggesting they correct Aadhaar itself if that's the one that's off.\n\nFor confidence scoring, I'm setting a threshold of 0.8 and only surfacing a verification note when a field falls below it, keeping the report clean by skipping \"ok\" confirmations unless there's an actual issue. For duplicate documents of the same type, I'll just use the first one and ignore documents that aren't relevant to the scheme.\n\nI'm naming check IDs by category — document, name, field, age range, requirement, exclusion, any-of group, external, confidence — grouped under categories like \"document,\" \"field,\" \"eligibility,\" and \"confidence.\" For the eligibility verdict, I'm deriving it from the checks: any action-required flips it to not_eligible, any needs_verification makes it needs_verification, otherwise it's likely_eligible — though I'm reconsidering whether a missing Aadhaar for an age-dependent case (like a widow) should also trigger needs_verification.\n\nFor readiness, I'm determining that any non-external check needing action pushes it to action_required, any needing verification to needs_verification, otherwise ready_for_review — confirming this against test cases like a clean PM-KISAN application, an income-tax disqualification, and a large landholding scenario that shouldn't affect eligibility since unknown answer keys are ignored. I'm now working through widow age test cases using a fixed reference date and date of birth to verify the age calculation logic.\n\nFor Orunodoi, missing a ration card forces action_required readiness, while owning a four-wheeler only triggers needs_verification rather than outright disqualification. Now I need to write message templates in both English and Assamese, so I'm drafting the vocabulary for document labels like Aadhaar.\n\nNow I'm noting where to get each document fixed, mapping bank branches, Aadhaar centres, circle offices, gram panchayats, CSCs, the PM-KISAN portal, and ration/food offices to their Assamese names.\n\nI'm also adding birth-death registrar and block office locations, plus standard phrases for \"Missing,\" \"Add your X,\" and \"confirm at the office,\" along with the Assamese translation of the disclaimer clarifying I help catch mistakes but don't guarantee approval.\n\nI'm finalizing the app branding: naming it \"KaagazCheck\" with the Assamese title \"কাগজ চেক\" and tagline \"Check before you apply\" / \"আবেদনৰ আগতে পৰীক্ষা কৰক,\" removing the Hindi exports, and double-checking the Bengali script Unicode code points to make sure the Assamese text renders correctly.\n\nI'm also considering whether to add Noto Sans Bengali for better font support, but deciding that's out of scope for now. I need to verify the zod version being used to confirm the correct API syntax for things like `z.record`, `z.iso.date()`, and `z.url()`, and I'm thinking through how default values interact with input/output type inference for the scheme rule data.\n\nNow I'm drafting the actual zod schema definitions — setting up an ISO date type, a status enum distinguishing verified versus provisional rules, and a provenance schema capturing source URL, title, clause, effective date, verification date, version, and status...\n\nAdding more schema definitions...\n\nDefining more schema fields...\n\nI should add validation refinements to ensure question ids stay unique across requirements, exclusions, and eligibility groups, plus a `listQuestions` helper for the UI. I also need an ExtractedDoc schema capturing document fields like account number, IFSC, names, and dates, along with a confidence score per field.\n\nFor privacy, I'll only store the last four Aadhaar digits, never the full number. Then I'm defining Answers as a record of booleans, a Check schema with result, messages, fix locations, provenance, and optional name-match scoring, and starting a Report schema with scheme id, version, as-of date, and eligibility results.\n\nI'm also planning a finalize helper that downgrades \"action_required\" to \"needs_verification\" when provenance is provisional, with mandatory docs exempted, plus regex validation for IFSC codes and cleanup logic for account numbers.\n\nFor the name consistency check, I'm thinking through requiring at least two provided docs before comparing names, using the first provided doc as the anchor, and generating a needs_verification message for each other doc if the anchor's name field can't be read -- while making sure I never echo the actual account number in any message since that's personal data.\n\nIf the other doc's name list is empty, that's also needs_verification; otherwise I rank candidates by match level and map same→ok, possible_match/initials_only→needs_verification, mismatch→action_required (unless provisional, which softens to needs_verification). I'm also handling low-confidence readings so mismatches with uncertain OCR get a gentler \"please confirm\" message rather than a hard rejection, and I want to export nameLevelToResult as a pure function with a property test asserting ok happens only when the level is same.\n\nNow I'm moving on to writing messages.ts, drafting bilingual (English/Assamese) labels for document types like Aadhaar, bank passbook, land record, and ration card, along with templates for the fixWhere guidance.\n\nI'm now mapping out where each document can be obtained, like bank branches, Aadhaar centres, revenue circle offices, Gram Panchayats, and Common Service Centres, along with their Assamese translations.\n\nFinishing the docMissing template, I'm making sure the Assamese genitive construction \"ৰ পৰা\" attaches correctly after each source phrase so it reads naturally across different examples like Aadhaar center, village panchayat, and CSC.\n\nI'm also working through the \"name matches\" template, confirming the locative suffix \"ত\" attaches properly after each document type like Aadhaar card, bank passbook, and land records.\n\nFor the possible-match case, I'm drafting wording in Assamese explaining that names on two documents appear to belong to the same person but are written differently, and advising the user to ask officials to make them identical.\n\nFor initials_only, I'm considering whether to echo back partial name data like initials, deciding it's acceptable since it's the user's own reported information.\n\nI'm standardizing the format to use a \"{doc}: ...\" prefix consistently across mismatch messages, and adding a special case for land records where the name belongs to a family member, noting the record may need updating at the circle office. I'm also drafting a fallback message for when the name on the document can't be read, prompting the user to type it in manually.\n\nI'm drafting the exclusion-triggered messaging with the exact spec wording about ineligibility and confirming at the Gram Panchayat, then thinking through how to phrase the \"not triggered\" case concisely for each criterion, probably needing a short label per question.\n\nFor triggered exclusions I need to include the specific reason from the data, like noting income tax payers are excluded from PM-KISAN, plus a prompt for unanswered questions asking the user to respond. For the age criterion, I'm drafting bilingual messages confirming whether the age on a given date falls within the min-max range.\n\nI also need to handle the \"out of range\" case, explaining the scheme's required age bracket, and a fallback message for when the date of birth couldn't be read from the Aadhaar card, prompting manual entry using the appropriate document label. Then I'm moving into translating the \"AnyOf\" condition logic, starting with the case where at least one sub-condition applies.\n\nFor the IFSC code field, I'm drafting messages for invalid format (noting it should be 11 characters like SBIN0001234) and the valid confirmation message, both in English and Assamese.\n\nNow I'm moving to the account number field, setting up validation messages for digit length requirements (9-18 digits) and the success confirmation, again in both languages.\n\nI'm also generalizing field labels like \"IFSC code\" and \"account number\" for reuse, plus building messages for fields that can't be verified from a photo and a combined confirmation message for documents with multiple hard-to-read fields.\n\nFor the disclaimer note about commonly asked items, and deciding on numeral consistency in age ranges, I'm settling on using Western digits throughout rather than mixing Assamese and Western numerals for clarity.\n\nNow I'm mapping out rejection reason codes with bilingual step-by-step fix instructions. For \"Name does not match Aadhaar,\" I'm drafting the resolution steps: comparing the name across Aadhaar, bank passbook, and land records, updating Aadhaar at a centre if wrong, correcting bank/land records to match if those are wrong, then requesting correction on the PM-KISAN portal.\n\nFor \"Aadhaar not seeded to bank,\" I'm writing steps about visiting the bank branch to link Aadhaar for DBT and confirming active status, with the fix location set to bank branch. I'm starting on \"Invalid account/IFSC\" next, beginning with checking the passbook's first page for correct details.\n\nFor unknown cases, I should direct them to bring the written reason and all documents to the block office or gram panchayat. Each entry needs English and Assamese titles and steps with matching lengths enforced by tests, plus a schemes registry file and a place to put the date utility. I'll write the tests first, check the zod API, then implement to make the failing tests pass.\n\n"}}
