{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:34:12.518Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm starting with the memory bank refresh and title switch, then moving into T1 by designing name-matching test cases first — checking normalization for spacing, case differences, and joined versus spaced name variants like \"Ramesh Kumar\" vs \"RAMESHKUMAR\".\n\nWorking through trickier cases like honorific-dropping and spelling variants (\"Devi\" vs \"Debi\"), abbreviation matches (\"KR\" vs \"Kumar\"), and initials-only comparisons like \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\". The hard case is \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" — I need this to be a mismatch, but a naive Jaro-Winkler on the full string might score deceptively high due to the shared prefix, so I'm computing the actual value to check. This tells me I need a token-based comparison instead of pure string similarity — comparing token counts and per-token similarity scores rather than treating the whole name as one blob, so that differing token counts (unless explained by initials/abbreviations) correctly produce a mismatch.\n\nFor normalization I need to uppercase, handle diacritics, and transliterate non-Latin scripts like Devanagari to Latin before comparison. Punctuation should become spaces and get collapsed, and honorific tokens dropped before checking equality—whether that's a straight match or a joined-token match. If either name uses a non-Latin script, I'll transliterate first, but any match found only after transliteration should count as possible_match rather than same, since transliteration itself introduces variation.\n\nFor canonical matching, I need a variant mapping table (like KR→KUMAR, MD/MOHD/MOHAMMED→MOHAMMAD, DEBI→DEVI, SK→SHEIKH) so tokens map to a normalized form before comparing—equal canonical tokens or joined canonical tokens yield possible_match. For initials, I'm checking that token counts align and each pair either matches canonically or one token is a single letter matching the other's initial, with at least one such initial present, classifying that case as initials_only—like \"R KUMAR\" vs \"RAMESH KUMAR\".\n\nNow I'm thinking through fuzzy matching: equal token counts with each aligned pair scoring high on Jaro-Winkler, or the whole joined string scoring high, should count as possible_match, while differing token counts (like a missing middle name) should default to mismatch to stay conservative. I also want to confirm that joined forms like \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMA\" and \"RAMESHKUMAR SHARMA\" are treated as the same after joining, and now I'm defining the scoring logic based on Jaro-Winkler over the canonical joined strings.\n\nI'm also worried about Jaro-Winkler's symmetry — greedy character matching can make some implementations give different scores depending on argument order — so I want to force symmetry by sorting the two input strings before computing the score, ensuring consistent results regardless of input order.\n\nFor token-level fuzzy matching, I should treat reordered tokens as equivalent too — e.g. \"SHARMA RAMESH\" matching \"RAMESH SHARMA\" by comparing sorted token sets. I also need to handle the edge case of empty or fully-stripped names: if either normalized string ends up empty, that should count as a mismatch rather than a false \"same\" result for the self-equality property test.\n\nFor generating test cases, I'll build strings from letter tokens joined with spaces and random punctuation, occasionally including honorifics — making sure that if stripping honorifics leaves nothing, I fall back to the original tokens rather than producing an empty name. I'm also considering whether `fc.string()`'s default ASCII-based generation is suitable, or whether I need a more targeted name-like string generator to properly test self-equality.\n\nI also need symmetric property tests pairing arbitrary strings with name-like strings, relying on the fact that normalization is computed independently per name so comparisons stay symmetric by construction, with Jaro-Winkler applied to the ordered pair. For transliteration, I'm sketching out a basic Devanagari/Bengali-to-Latin mapping handling consonants, matras, virama, and schwa deletion at word endings (e.g., Hindi \"राम\" becoming \"RAM\" rather than \"RAMA\"), plus equivalent handling for Bengali and Assamese scripts.\n\nSince Devanagari and Bengali share the same ISCII-based layout, I'm designing a single transliteration table keyed by codepoint offset from each script's base (Devanagari U+0900, Bengali U+0980), with special cases for Assamese-specific letters like ৰ, ৱ, ড়, ঢ়, য়, and nukta forms in Devanagari.\n\nStill mapping remaining consonants, matras, and special marks like anusvara, visarga, and the Bengali retroflex characters to their transliteration codes.\n\nFor the inherent vowel rule, I decide a consonant gets no vowel if followed by a matra or virama, otherwise \"A\" — with schwa deletion at word-end (राम → RAM, रमेश → RAMESH), though medial schwa deletion like \"Kamla\" is trickier and I'll let JW handle that case. Bengali names like দেবী → DEBI check out fine, and the inherent 'a'-vs-'o' pronunciation quirk in Bengali/Assamese shouldn't matter since names are typically transliterated with 'a'. I'm now considering NFC vs NFD normalization issues for Bengali text.\n\nComposed vowel signs like ো may decompose under NFD, so I need NFC normalization, but nukta characters like क़ and য় are composition-excluded, meaning I should handle those by stripping the nukta mark separately (ড় → D, য় → Y via offset mapping). For Latin diacritics, I plan to apply NFKD and strip combining marks after the Indic transliteration step, since Indic matras are also combining marks and I don't want to strip those prematurely.\n\nI then uppercase the result and replace anything outside A-Z with a space, stripping digits and punctuation entirely so only letters remain -- testing cases like \"R. Kumar\" → \"R KUMAR\" and \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" → \"SUNITA DEVI\" after removing honorifics. I'm building a list of honorific titles to strip (SHRI, SMT, MR, MRS, KUMARI, LATE, and variants like SRI, SHREE, SHRIMATI, SUSHRI, LT), while deliberately excluding DR since that could be a meaningful professional title rather than just an honorific.\n\nI realize \"KUMARI\" is risky since it can be a genuine given name rather than a title, so I'll only strip honorifics when they appear as leading tokens, repeatedly peeling off things like \"LATE SHRI\" from the front. I also confirm that Devanagari transliterations like श्री → SHRI and श्रीमती → SHRIMATI work correctly, while स्व. (late) is trickier and I'll skip handling it for now. Now I'm mapping common name abbreviation variants to canonical forms, like KR/KMR → KUMAR and MD/MOHD/MOHAMAD → a unified Mohammed variant.\n\nFor joined-token comparisons, if the canonical forms match exactly after joining, I treat them as a match. I'm also handling initials, like \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" or \"R K SHARMA\" vs \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMA\", by aligning tokens and checking whether a single-letter token matches the first character of the corresponding full token, while still requiring at least one fully matching non-initial token to avoid false positives.\n\nFor fuzzy matching, I'm considering same token count with per-token Jaro-Winkler similarity above 0.85, or allowing reordering if sorted tokens align, which should also handle transliterated names like Bengali script converting to Latin equivalents.\n\nFor same/possible_match distinction, I'm settling on a rule: if any input comes from a non-Latin script requiring transliteration, the match level caps at possible_match rather than same, since transliteration introduces uncertainty—exact equality only counts as same when comparing within the same script.\n\nBut I realize transliteration can be lossy — distinct Devanagari letters like श and ष both become \"SH\" in Latin, which would incorrectly flag two different native strings as \"same.\" So I need a stricter rule: for non-Latin scripts, require both the transliterated match AND an exact match of the native-script key itself (NFC-normalised, punctuation stripped) before calling them the same.\n\nSince leading honorifics like \"श्री\" would otherwise break native-key matching, I'll tokenize the native string by letter/mark boundaries, transliterate each token individually, and drop any leading tokens whose transliteration is a known honorific before comparing the remaining native tokens.\n\nFor each remaining token I transliterate and normalize to uppercase ASCII, but need to handle unmapped scripts (like Chinese) where the Latin form comes out empty — better to fall back to keeping the original uppercased characters so self-comparisons still succeed.\n\nThen I drop leading honorific tokens unless that empties the list, and compute a script signature (latin/deva/beng/other) from the remaining native characters to compare formats between the two names, checking emptiness and signature equality before scoring the match level.\n\nFor canonical matching I map tokens to their Latin variants, treating equal or reordered canon sequences as possible matches. Initials-only comparisons get their own category, and fuzzy matching requires equal token counts with each pair scoring at least 0.85 on Jaro-Winkler — though I'm weighing whether to also allow a joined-form comparison for cases where token counts differ, like \"RAMESHKUMAR SHARMA\" against \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMMA\".\n\nFor scoring, I'm using Jaro-Winkler on the ordered, space-joined canonical strings, giving exact matches a perfect score and rounding other results to a few decimal places for consistency. I'm validating this against examples: \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" vs \"SUNITA DEBI\" should match through canonical mapping of DEBI to DEVI, and \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" should also resolve to a possible match via canonicalization.\n\nWorking through more edge cases now — \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" should succeed as initials-only since R matches Ramesh's first letter and Kumar matches fully; \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" should correctly fail since the token counts and canonical strings don't align; \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" should fail based on a Jaro-Winkler score around 0.778. I'm also considering tricky cases like when a full name's token coincidentally looks like an initial on the other side.\n\nChecking Jaro-Winkler for short tokens like \"R\" vs \"RA\" seems fine, and for \"SUNITA DEVI\" vs \"SUNITA DEVA\" the score comes out around 0.883, above threshold, so that'd be a possible match needing verification. The concerning case is \"RAMESH\" vs \"RAMESHWAR\" scoring about 0.933 — high enough to be flagged as a possible match, meaning \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Sharma\" would also need verification rather than being a clean rejection.\n\nI want to make sure the spec's rule holds as a property: any name-check result that isn't an exact same-level match should never resolve to \"ok\" — so for any level other than exact match, the overall result must be something other than ok. I'm thinking of modeling this as a mapping function from match level to result, then verifying it across arbitrary name pairs in the evaluation logic.\n\nFor the property test, I'll generate names where the bank name is an abbreviated variant of the Aadhaar name (like replacing KUMAR with KR), producing a possible_match level, then assert the check isn't marked ok. I'll also add a broader property asserting ok only ever occurs when the level is exactly same.\n\nNow I'm setting up the types file using zod, starting with a rule status enum and provenance schema with source URL, title, and clause fields.\n\nFor doc types I'm debating between a strict enum (aadhaar, bank passbook, land record, ration card, etc.) versus a free-form string—leaning toward an enum so unknown document types fail validation and force explicit handling.\n\nNow I'm drafting the Assamese labels for each document type, like \"আধাৰ কাৰ্ড\" for aadhaar and \"ৰেচন কাৰ্ড\" for ration card, working through the list one by one.\n\nI'm deciding to keep core bilingual messages in src/core/messages.ts, letting T2's i18n layer re-export them, and flagging the Assamese strings for native review. I'm sketching the Criterion data model with fields for id, kind (exclusion or requirement), the bilingual question, failsWhen condition, bilingual messages, fixWhere, and provenance, starting with the exclusions array structure.\n\nNow I'm adding requirements and any-of eligibility logic — widow/BPL style all-of conditions versus Orunodoi-style any-of conditions (widow, PwD, AAY, income threshold), including provisional versus verified states, plus thinking through whether PM-KISAN needs a separate land-ownership question tied to document checks.\n\nI'm sketching the SchemeRule data shape: scheme metadata and provenance, benefit details with central share, state top-up, period, and effective date, benefit override entries for historical changes, and mandatory versus supporting document lists with their own provenance and notes.\n\nI'm defining the eligibility structure - an optional age range tied to Aadhaar verification, a required list of criteria where any \"no\" fails, and an \"any-of\" eligibility group where at least one \"yes\" is needed, with the group only counting as verified if all its criteria are individually verified. I'm also adding exclusion criteria that fail on \"yes,\" plus external checks like bank seeding, account activity, eKYC, and land verification, each with localized messages.\n\nNow I'm structuring document checks as a list of field checks like IFSC format, account number, and land owner, each carrying provenance, with questions embedded directly in the criteria objects. I'm also defining the Criterion shape itself with bilingual text, provenance, fail messages, and an optional \"fixWhere\" pointer, thinking through how provisional exclusions should phrase their failure messages.\n\nFor the confirmation message I'm templating it as a generic notice that tells the applicant where to confirm eligibility, using a scheme-level \"confirmAt\" field tied to a FixWhere enum covering places like bank branch, Aadhaar centre, circle office, gram panchayat, block office, CSC, or self-answered questions, each with bilingual labels. I'm also sketching the ExtractedDoc type to hold document fields like name, DOB, last four Aadhaar digits, account number, and IFSC.\n\nNow I'm defining a low-confidence threshold around 0.7, where any field used in a check falling below it triggers a \"needs_verification\" flag prompting the user to confirm those fields, which then get bumped to full confidence once confirmed. I'm also setting up an Answers record mapping question IDs to yes/no values, where missing answers for verified criteria likewise get flagged as needing verification.\n\nWorking through the test cases, I realize external checks like bank seeding are always flagged needs_verification per spec, but surprisingly this doesn't downgrade the overall readiness status — a \"clean\" case can still be ready_for_review even with an external check pending. So external checks and low-confidence/possible-match issues both produce needs_verification flags, but only some of these actually block the ready_for_review status.\n\nThe readiness hierarchy seems to be: action_required wins if any check flags that, otherwise needs_verification unless the only flagged checks are external ones, otherwise ready_for_review. I'm now wondering whether a failed eligibility criterion (not_eligible) should also force readiness to action_required, since that's a substantive issue rather than just something needing confirmation.\n\nI'll organize checks by category — document, name, eligibility, external, data_quality — and derive readiness from all non-external checks, while eligibility status comes from the eligibility-specific checks alone.\n\nEligibility logic: a failed verified check means not_eligible, an unresolved provisional check or unknown age means needs_verification, and otherwise it's likely_eligible. The tricky part is that provisional rules should never give a definitive pass per the golden rule, so when someone answers \"no\" to a provisional exclusion question, I shouldn't mark it as a hard \"ok\" pass — instead I need the check to reflect that the rule simply doesn't apply based on their answer, keeping the result non-definitive rather than closing off eligibility entirely.\n\nFor Orunodoi, any unanswered item in the provisional any-of exclusion list or an unmet NFSA enrollment precondition should push the result to needs_verification. Similarly, missing Aadhaar or an unclear date of birth should trigger needs_verification (and a missing document flag), while a low-confidence DOB should do the same.\n\nFor the widow scheme, I'm checking boundary ages: 39 fails, 40 and 79 pass, 80 fails. Since is_widow and bpl_household are verified, I need mandatory documents like Aadhaar, death certificate, bank passbook, and BPL proof — a missing death certificate should still flag as action_required even when the document list itself is provisional, since it's about readiness rather than source certainty.\n\nNow I'm thinking through name matching for land records — comparing the applicant's Aadhaar name against the ownerNames array, since the land could legitimately be registered to a family member rather than the applicant. For a mismatch case like \"Rameshwar Sharma,\" I want to flag action_required with guidance pointing to the circle office, noting that if the land is held by a family member the applicant should clarify that locally.\n\nI'm settling on Aadhaar as the master reference name per PM-KISAN rules, falling back to the first available document if Aadhaar is missing, then comparing bank passbook and land record names against it — mismatches route to fixWhere locations like bank_branch or circle_office, and I'm still deciding the right office label for ration card corrections in Assam.\n\nFor possible matches, I'm directing users to ask their bank or Aadhaar centre to make the names identical. I'm drafting message templates that include the actual names from each document so users can clearly see the discrepancy, like comparing the Aadhaar name against the bank passbook spelling.\n\nThen I'm working through document field checks: IFSC format needs normalization (uppercase, remove spaces) before validation, with missing values flagged as needs_verification and invalid formats as action_required; account numbers get similar digit-only validation with a 9-18 length range; and I'm deciding the land owner check is just handled by the existing name check on land records rather than a separate rule.\n\nExternal checks like bank seeding, account activity, e-KYC, and land verification always resolve to needs_verification without blocking readiness, which matches the expected test case for PM-KISAN. I'm also considering adding low-confidence field checks per document type, flagging anything under 0.7 confidence as needs_verification.\n\nFor the name mismatch logic, I'll keep it simple: run a separate data-quality check for low-confidence fields rather than folding it into the name comparison itself, since that comparison already produces ok/needs_verification/action_required on its own.\n\nFor duplicate documents of the same type, I'll just use the first one submitted. Unanswered questions should trigger needs_verification for verified criteria, and eligibility itself should become needs_verification when questions are unanswered.\n\nI'm sketching out the report structure — schemeId, version, date, eligibility, readiness, an array of checks, benefit info, supporting docs, and bilingual disclaimers — along with the shape of each Check object, settling on using `provenance` as the property name to match the T1 spec rather than `ruleProvenance`. Now I'm defining the benefitOn function signature that takes a scheme and ISO date and returns central/state benefit details.\n\nI'm working through the Orunodoi override logic: the base scheme has a recurring ₹1,250 monthly state top-up, but there's a documented one-time override of ₹1,500 for October 2026 only, which reverts back to ₹1,250 in November. The key realization is that this override should come from the T3 demo's agent parsing the sample notice, not be baked into the base scheme — so in the test, I need to construct the scheme object with the override spread in separately.\n\nFor dates, I'm standardizing on ISO date strings and need an age-calculation function that takes DOB and an as-of date. I also need to consider edge cases like Aadhaar records that only have a year of birth rather than a full date, and how to handle Feb 29 birthdays when computing age in non-leap years.\n\nI'm also planning a masking function for Aadhaar numbers: it should extract digits and reveal only the last four, formatting the rest as masked blocks, plus a small property test ensuring age is monotonic and output never exposes more than four real digits.\n\nFor rejection codes, I'm defining an enum alongside a map of fix instructions in English and Assamese with step-by-step guidance and where to fix each issue, keeping a simple `rejectionFix(code)` helper rather than building a full text-matching guesser. For the scheme registry, I'm designing functions to fetch, list, and register schemes, validating new entries with zod and handling version replacement while preserving older versions in history.\n\nI'm settling on storing versions in a map keyed by id, with `getScheme` returning the latest or a specified version and `registerScheme` rejecting duplicate id+version pairs, plus considering a registry factory function for test isolation. For versioning I'll use a semver-style string like \"2.0.0\" consistently across both the scheme and provenance metadata, and now I'm turning to writing natural Assamese text for the fix instructions.\n\nContinuing with the government employee question, translating it with the clarification that MTS, Class IV, or Group D roles don't count, then moving into drafting the income tax payer exclusion question.\n\nTranslating the pension question, then I'm noting the owns_cultivable_land requirement about land records needing to be included, and thinking through whether test cases need a helper function to generate sample answers for all the requirements.\n\nSince progress notes mention \"sample sets\" for T1 even though the spec doesn't explicitly require it, I decide to add a small `src/core/samples.ts` with sample documents A, B, C as pure data fixtures — useful for tests while staying small and within scope.\n\nFor Orunodoi, I'm working out the requirement structure: ration_card as mandatory (verified), nfsa_enrolled as a provisional requirement question, and for eligibilityAnyOf I'm considering income_below_2_lakh, aay_card, and widow/divorced status, since the scheme prioritizes women heads of household, especially widows, divorced women, and PwD individuals.\n\nFor exclusions, I'm thinking through categories like elected representatives, government employees or pensioners (including teachers), professionals (doctors, engineers, lawyers, CAs), and possibly registered contractors or traders, plus large landholders — though I'm unsure whether to specify the old 15-bigha guideline since it isn't confirmed in the current spec.\n\nFor supporting documents, I'm leaning toward listing them as informational guidance (aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, self-declaration) rather than as a formal check, since treating them as a check could prevent Orunodoi from ever reaching ready_for_review status. I'll put this in the report as a separate supportingDocs field with a note to confirm the current list, rather than making it a pass/fail check.\n\nFor name matching, I'm figuring out candidate name extraction across document types — using the name field for ration cards (which typically lists the head of family, potentially the husband rather than the applicant woman) alongside memberNames, and ownerNames for land records — building a general best-match approach across these candidate lists. I'm also setting confirmAt to gram_panchayat and noting the Orunodoi 3 benefit structure: zero central share, 1250 state top-up, monthly period, with effective date still unknown.\n\nI'm wrestling with whether effectiveDate should be required or nullable — the spec implies money values are versioned with an effectiveDate, so I'll make it a nullable ISO date field rather than inventing a value when it's genuinely unknown. For PM-KISAN I know the scheme effective date is 2018-12-01, but for the widow pension central share (IGNWPS, revised to ₹300 around 2012), the spec doesn't specify a date, so I'll leave that null rather than guess. No external checks apply for Orunodoi.\n\nFor the widow pension requirements, I'm verifying eligibility fields like is_widow and bpl_household status, with age range 40–79 derived from Aadhaar DOB. The mandatory document list includes aadhaar, death certificate, bank passbook, and BPL proof, with name consistency checked across aadhaar and bank passbook — and I'm figuring out whether confirmAt should point to the Block Development Office, since that's typically where NSAP pensions are processed in Assam.\n\nMoving to benefits, I'm setting the centralShare at ₹300/month with no stateTopUp, and skipping external checks like account_active since they're not specified in the spec. For provenance, I'm pulling URLs from the PM-KISAN official portal and cross-referencing exclusion categories with a verified vikaspedia summary, planning to attach pmkisan.gov.in as the primary source for most benefit and exclusion clauses while still verifying the Orunodoi scheme details.\n\nI'm noting the Orunodoi district page as verified but without a clear effective date, so I'll leave that field unset and reference the last-updated note instead, treating the publicservicesmap entry as provisional. I'm also confirming the widow pension scheme URL and setting verifiedOn to 2026-10-10, while double-checking that the vikaspedia URL with parentheses will still pass z.url() validation.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the evaluation algorithm: for each scheme I gather provided documents by type, then walk through mandatory docs to mark each as satisfied or flag missing ones with where to obtain them, followed by checking data quality on low-confidence extracted fields and verifying enough name-bearing documents are present for cross-referencing.\n\nThen I continue layering in document field checks like IFSC and account numbers, age range validation, requirement rules with any-of and exclusion logic, and external verification steps. Finally I derive the overall readiness verdict by scanning the non-external checks: any action_required check flips the whole result to action_required, any needs_verification check (absent action_required) flips it to needs_verification, otherwise it's ready_for_review — and I'm tracing through the age-mismatch, fuzzy-name-match, and clean-application test cases to confirm this logic lines up with expected outcomes.\n\nFor eligibility, I'm separating out criteria-based checks into their own category: a verified action_required eligibility check makes the applicant not_eligible, any needs_verification bumps it to needs_verification, and otherwise it defaults to likely_eligible, with missing documents only affecting readiness rather than eligibility itself. I'm also handling unanswered verified questions by generating a needs_verification result with a prompt asking the applicant to answer that specific question.\n\nFor provisional requirements, a \"no\" answer or an unanswered question both produce needs_verification urging confirmation at the Gram Panchayat, rather than an outright rejection. For anyOf groups, I'm working out the logic: at least one \"yes\" is fine, all verified \"no\" answers means action_required (not eligible), unverified \"no\" answers mean needs_verification, and if none are answered yes with some unanswered, it's also needs_verification — I'm structuring this as an `eligibilityAnyOf` array of criteria to support that evaluation.\n\nI also want to export a `questionsFor(scheme)` helper returning requirement, anyOf, and exclusion questions in order for the UI to consume. Now I'm drafting bilingual (English/Assamese) message templates in messages.ts covering cases like document present, document missing, and provisional requirement notes, each paired with its Assamese translation.\n\nI'm also adding name-matching messages: a straightforward \"names match\" confirmation, and a more nuanced \"possible match\" warning that flags when names across two sources look like the same person written differently, noting officials may still reject it and suggesting the user get the records made identical — with full Assamese translations for both.\n\nAdding more translation entries for the mismatch case, noting the full name correction should align exactly with Aadhaar, and I'm considering how to phrase the reference-document case when Aadhaar isn't the reference.\n\nNow I'm drafting the \"needs_verification\" message for when the name on a document can't be read, and building out the bilingual labels for each correction location—bank branch, Aadhaar centre, circle office, gram panchayat—in both English and Assamese.\n\nI'm also adding entries for registrar (births & deaths office, for death certificates) and self (when the user should answer directly), while noting \"none\" should be avoided in favor of self. Then I'm moving on to map each document type to where it's obtained, starting with aadhaar going to the aadhaar centre.\n\nFor the rest of the where-to-obtain map, I'm assigning bank passbook to bank branch, land record to