on in both languages.\n\nFor invalid IFSC, I'm writing messages explaining the code should be 11 characters like SBIN0001234 and suggesting they verify with their bank branch, with Assamese translations included. For missing IFSC, I note the system couldn't read it from the passbook so the user needs to enter it manually, and for account numbers I'm deciding to mask sensitive digits, showing only \"ending 1234\" for privacy.\n\nNow I'm drafting the low-confidence field message, telling users we're unsure certain fields on a document were read correctly and asking them to verify, keeping field names in English with Assamese translations. I'm also thinking through provenance for document-level checks like IFSC—whether to use scheme-level provenance for these engine-level validations.\n\nFor the disclaimer, I'm translating a message clarifying we help catch preventable mistakes but don't guarantee approval since the department decides. Then I'm working out benefit calculation logic: total equals central plus stateTopUp when stateTopUp isn't null, otherwise total stays null, with overrides applied based on effective dates.\n\nComparing dates as ISO strings with last-matching override winning, returning amounts along with period, provenance, and source tagging. I'm designing the override schema with id, date ranges, shares, and bilingual notes, then testing against a scenario where the benefit changes from 1500 in October to 1250 in November using specific dates. Now I want to quickly check the sample notice to make sure the override fields match what's needed for the third task.\n\nFor the age calculation, I'm deciding `ageOn` should return null rather than throw for invalid dates or when the dob is after the as-of date, parsing and validating the ISO format with a roundtrip through Date.UTC to catch invalid dates like February 30th, and I'll write a custom `isValidIsoDate` helper in age.ts that gets reused in the zod schema validation.\n\nFor testing the self-comparison property — a name matching itself — I'm thinking through using fast-check's string arbitrary with graphemes, checking that my tokenization (splitting on non-letter/mark characters) still produces matching tokens since comparing a deterministic string against itself should trivially succeed as long as it yields non-empty tokens.\n\nI need full symmetry across every step: ordering inputs by string compare, testing initials both directions, comparing sorted tokens, and rounding scores consistently. For Jaro-Winkler I should use code points via Array.from to handle surrogate pairs correctly. Now I'm thinking through generating name variants (like KUMAR→KR, DEVI→DEBI, MOHAMMAD→MD) to test whether PM-KISAN bank name matching ever fails to flag a possible match when it should.\n\nI also want a broader property: that a name check returning \"ok\" always implies the underlying name match level is \"same,\" tested across arbitrary name pairs, not just the specific KISAN scheme case. I'm also verifying the Rameshwar Sharma land record test — the Aadhaar name \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMA\" against land owner name \"Rameshwar Sharma\" should correctly produce a mismatch.\n\nSeparately, I need a test confirming large landholding size has no effect on report outcome — adding `areaHectares: 12` to a land record shouldn't change the ready-for-review status, and no scheme criterion or question should reference landholding size or hectares at all. For the fields schema, I'm leaning toward adding an optional `landAreaHectares` number field, relying on Zod's default stripping behavior for any other unexpected extracted fields.\n\nFor confidence scores, I'm deciding that missing confidence entries should default to treating the value as confident (1), since those usually come from manual user entry rather than uncertain AI extraction — I'll document this behavior clearly. Now I'm mapping out the full set of field keys for ExtractedDoc: things like name, dob, gender, aadhaarLast4 with a 4-digit regex, bank account details, owner and member name arrays, ration card info with AAY/PHH type, and death/deceased details for succession cases.\n\nI'm deciding to keep the full account number in memory for validation (9-18 digit check) but only ever display the last 4 digits for privacy. I'm skipping the husbandName vs deceasedName cross-check for widow cases since it's not required, and noting ration card candidates should include both name and memberNames.\n\nNow I'm moving to the Step 0 app info changes — setting the Assamese app name and title, English/Assamese taglines, and updating the test to verify the Bengali-script Unicode range for the Assamese text.\n\nI'm also noting that Geist font only covers Latin script, so Assamese text will fall back to the system font — worth flagging as a minor concern. Before writing the memory bank updates (productContext, systemPatterns, activeContext), I want to quickly check the sample notice data shape for the override, then write tests first and run them.\n\nNow I should verify z.url handles the vikaspedia URL correctly given its parentheses and query string.\n\n"}}
