{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:39:56.414Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm planning the design starting with appInfo.ts, setting the app name, title, and taglines in both Assamese and English, and updating the corresponding test file to match.\n\nThen I'm sketching out the type system with zod schemas: a rule status enum for verified/provisional states, a provenance schema capturing source URL, title, clause, dates, and version info, and a document type structure that needs to stay extensible since schemes are onboarded later. I'm leaning toward a flexible string-based doc type ID with a regex pattern rather than a closed enum, paired with a labels map for known types, so new schemes can register their own document types without code changes. I'm also setting up localized text objects that pair English and Assamese strings together.\n\nFor eligibility criteria, I'm designing a model with exclusions (yes-answer disqualifies, routed to not_eligible or needs_verification depending on provenance status), eligibilityAnyOf (at least one yes required, useful for provisional schemes), and requirements (all answers must be yes, needed for cases like widow status plus BPL household membership).\n\nFor answers, I'm leaning toward a record mapping question IDs to boolean or null values, where null represents \"don't know\" and routes to needs_verification, while missing answers also fall into that bucket — this keeps fully-answered clean cases like PM-KISAN flowing straight to ready_for_review. I also need to account for external verification checks like bank seeding, active accounts, eKYC, and land verification feeding into the same needs_verification logic.\n\nLooking at the spec more closely though, there seems to be a distinction between overall readiness status and individual check status — a case can be ready_for_review overall while a specific external check (like bank seeding) is still flagged as needs_verification. So readiness and the per-check verification status aren't the same signal; I need to track both separately rather than letting one external check downgrade the whole application's readiness.\n\nThat bank name case counts as a name possible_match, which is fine. Now I'm trying to figure out how eligibility interacts with readiness — docs/schemes.md defines readiness action_required for mismatches, but it's unclear whether an unmet verified criterion (eligibility not_eligible) should also force readiness to action_required, even though that reads oddly. I'll treat docs/schemes.md as the source of truth here rather than guessing.\n\nI'm also wrestling with provisional criteria and external statuses conflicting between readiness needs_verification and the sample's ready_for_review with bank seeding flagged separately — since two sources agree clean cases should be ready_for_review, I'll keep external checks from downgrading overall readiness and just note the ambiguity for later, including whether a confirmed seeding answer should still trigger needs_verification.\n\nI'll keep external checks always needs_verification since that's the spec, even though an unanswered external check isn't really a document problem. For readiness, I'll map verified-failed criterion checks to action_required, provisional-triggered to needs_verification, and unanswered checks also to needs_verification — matching the Orunodoi four-wheeler test case where eligibility stays needs_verification rather than not_eligible.\n\nFor the clean Orunodoi case where no ration card exists, readiness should be action_required, but when all provisional exclusions are answered \"no\" and nothing triggers, I'll treat that as \"ok\" with a note that the rule is provisional and worth reconfirming, since provisional rules can't give a definitive pass. If the user matches none of an any-of provisional list, that stays needs_verification.\n\nFor the valid ration card itself, that's the verified mandatory requirement. For supporting documents, I'll treat them as a checklist item rather than a hard eligibility check, emitting an info-level check with result needs_verification and a note to confirm the current document list.\n\nActually readiness should just ignore \"supporting\" and \"external\" categories since they're advisory, while still counting document/name/field/criterion categories — present supporting doc is ok, missing isn't penalized.\n\nFor the Widow scheme, mandatory provisional docs like death certificate still drive action_required when missing, even though provisional rules can't give a definitive pass/fail — action_required just means \"get this document,\" not \"ineligible,\" so missing mandatory docs get flagged with a note like \"commonly required; confirm locally\" when the rule is provisional.\n\nI'm also including bpl_proof as mandatory since it's listed in that same sentence alongside aadhaar, death_certificate, and bank_passbook — even though this means the widow demo now requires four documents. For age, I'll derive it from the Aadhaar DOB field, with missing DOB triggering needs_verification and low-confidence DOB doing the same.\n\nOnce age is verified and outside the 40-79 window, that's not_eligible, and I'll add a message suggesting the Old Age Pension for 80+ cases. I'm deciding to skip adding a separate is_widow question since the death certificate requirement already implies widow status, so I'll just track requirements for age window and bpl_household verification without extra questions.\n\nFor PM-KISAN, the \"owns cultivable land\" requirement means checking land record ownership, comparing owner names against the applicant's Aadhaar name (and potentially family members, though that's harder to verify automatically). If none of the owner names match at the best available level, I'll flag it as action_required pointing the applicant to the circle office to fix the record, with a note that family-member ownership should still be acknowledged. I'm folding this into the broader name-consistency check across Aadhaar, bank passbook, and land record documents.\n\nI'll treat Aadhaar as the canonical reference name and compare every other document's name against it, routing any fixes to the non-Aadhaar document; if Aadhaar itself is missing, I'll fall back to the first available document name in priority order.\n\nNow I'm mapping each document type to its appropriate fix location — bank branch, Assam revenue circle office, Food & Civil Supplies office or Gram Panchayat, Aadhaar Seva Kendra, or the birth/death registrar — and defining a FixWhere enum covering these places plus options like PM-Kisan portal, block office, and CSC, to be localized later.\n\nI'm also working out field-level validation: checking IFSC format and account number length (9–18 digits) on the bank passbook, deciding invalid values trigger action_required while low-confidence fields (below a ~0.75 threshold) get flagged as needs_verification unless they're already action_required.\n\nFor mismatches, I think treating them as needs_verification is more honest since they could just be OCR errors—better to ask the user to confirm than assume failure. For the confidence model itself, I'm defining ExtractedDoc with a fields object and a confidence record per field, and deciding that a missing confidence entry defaults to 1 (treated as confidently/manually entered), which I should document clearly.\n\nFor Aadhaar, I'm only storing the last 4 digits via regex matching, never the full number, for privacy. I'm narrowing the field list down to essentials like name, dob, aadhaarLast4, accountNo, ifsc, ownerNames, husbandName, deceasedName, and rationCardNo, using a non-strict zod object schema that just strips unknown keys rather than enforcing strictness.\n\nFor duplicate documents of the same type, I'll just use the first one found. I'm also working out the schema shape—id, category, result, bilingual messages, fixWhere, provenance, optional nameMatch—settling on `provenance` to match the task file naming, and deciding that document-level checks like name consistency should inherit the scheme's base provenance rather than needing a separate field.\n\nFor the report structure, I'm going with schemeId, schemeVersion, asOfDate, eligibility, readiness, checks array, and including benefit for convenience even though the spec doesn't strictly require it. For the benefit schema, I'm defining centralShare, stateTopUp, period, effectiveDate, provenance, and an overrides array for time-bound changes like Orunodoi's central share adjustments.\n\nFor Orunodoi specifically, the base values are centralShare 0, stateTopUp 1250, monthly period, with an October override bumping stateTopUp to 1500 — benefitOn should compute total as null only when stateTopUp is null, and flag isOverride when applicable, verified by the October test expecting total 1500. PM-KISAN needs an instalments field since its 6000/year is split into three 2000 payments, and I'm making effectiveDate optional since PM-KISAN's isn't clearly documented. For the override test, I'll construct a scheme version with the override embedded directly since the base scheme doesn't have one — that override is specific to this test case.\n\nNow I'm defining exclusions like pension_10k_plus, bilingual question text, ageRange with min/max/provenance tied to aadhaar DOB, externalChecks with bilingual labels and fix locations, nameConsistencyDocs per scheme (PM-KISAN needs aadhaar/bank_passbook/land_record, Orunodoi needs ration_card/aadhaar/bank_passbook, widow scheme needs aadhaar/bank_passbook), and ensuring bank format checks trigger whenever a bank_passbook document is present.\n\nFor eligibility logic, I'm deciding: any verified exclusion or failed requirement or out-of-range age marks not_eligible; otherwise provisional triggers or unknown/unanswered criteria mark needs_verification; otherwise it's likely_eligible.\n\nFor readiness, I'm checking the document, name, field, and criterion categories—if any require action (including verified-failed criteria), readiness is action_required; if any are needs_verification, readiness follows suit; otherwise it's ready_for_review. I'm checking this against test cases like the four-wheeler and PM-KISAN bank name scenarios to confirm the logic holds.\n\nNow I'm designing a property test confirming that any possible_match name result never yields an \"ok\" check, then moving into the normalization algorithm for the name matcher—transliterating non-Latin scripts, uppercasing, stripping non-letter characters, and removing honorific tokens like SHRI, SMT, and DR before comparison.\n\nI'm reasoning through edge cases: joined tokens without spaces should count as equal (\"RAMESHKUMAR\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\"), while variant spellings like DEVI vs DEBI should fall into possible_match rather than same. I'm also sketching a canonicalization map for common token variants like KR→KUMAR.\n\nFor honorific and alias handling, I'm mapping multiple spellings of names to a single canonical form, like MD/MOHD/MUHAMMAD all collapsing to MOHAMMAD, or SK/SHAIKH collapsing to SHEIKH, so that after canonicalization a joined match counts as possible_match. I'm also deciding that cross-script transliteration should never count as an exact \"same\" match since romanization is imprecise — at best it should be scored as possible_match when the names align after transliteration.\n\nFor the actual transliteration logic, I'm working out a simple scheme to romanize Devanagari by handling inherent vowel (schwa) deletion — keeping the implicit \"a\" after consonants except when dropped at word-final position or overridden by matras/virama — so words like रमेश and कुमार come out as \"RAMESH\" and \"KUMAAR\" rather than overly literal transliterations.\n\nI'm extending this to Bengali/Assamese script conventions too, noting quirks like the inherent vowel sounding like \"o\" but romanizing names as \"a\", the Assamese-specific ra and wa characters, and how \"ব\" in Bengali corresponds to \"V\" sounds in Devanagari (so দেবী canonicalizes to DEVI instead of DEBI). I'm setting up this mapping so phonetic folding can later match names across scripts despite spelling variance.\n\nNow I'm testing whether a simple whole-string Jaro-Winkler threshold would actually work against the spec's test cases — like \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\", which share a long common prefix and might score suspiciously high despite being a legitimate mismatch. That suggests whole-string JW alone isn't reliable, and I probably need token-level alignment where each token pair must independently clear the similarity threshold.\n\nWorking through the Jaro-Winkler match window for Ramesh/Suresh: only 4 characters align (R, E, S, H), giving jaro=0.778 with no prefix bonus, which falls below the 0.85 threshold — so this pair is a mismatch.\n\nNow checking initials handling: \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" should resolve to initials_only since the single-letter token R matches the first letter of RAMESH, and KUMAR matches exactly. I'm also verifying \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" against \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" where KR canonicalizes to KUMAR, giving a possible_match, and confirming joined forms like \"RAMESHKUMAR\" equal \"Ramesh Kumar\" when spaces are stripped. Now I need to handle cases where the token counts between the two names differ.\n\nFor Jaro-Winkler, I'm deciding the threshold should be 0.85 applied per-token on normalized forms, which feels like the right defensive interpretation, and also considering computing an overall score from the joined canonical strings, treating an exact match as score 1. I also need to handle reordered tokens, like \"SHARMA RAMESH\" vs \"RAMESH SHARMA\", by trying a sorted or permutation-based alignment so these still register as a possible_match.\n\nI'm also worried about symmetry — in-order alignment is naturally symmetric, and standard Jaro-Winkler is theoretically symmetric, but the greedy matching step in typical implementations could occasionally produce asymmetric results if I'm not careful, so I need to verify that my implementation truly treats both input orders the same way.\n\nThe \"same as itself\" property is tricky since honorifics like \"Mr\" normalize to empty too. I think the safer approach is to treat empty-vs-empty as mismatch defensively, and restrict the property generator to alphabetic tokens that aren't pure honorifics, filtering out strings where normalizeName produces an empty result.\n\nI also need to handle non-Latin scripts correctly — a name using Devanagari or Bengali compared to itself must still register as a match even without transliteration. The fix is to compare raw normalized forms (NFC, uppercase, punctuation/spaces/honorifics stripped) before any transliteration step, so same-script identical strings match directly, and only fall back to transliterated Latin tokens when comparing across different scripts.\n\nI'm sketching the matching algorithm step by step: compare raw and canonical token joins for equality (same/possible_match), check sorted tokens for reordering, then for equal-length token lists compare position-by-position using exact match, Jaro-Winkler similarity, or initial-matching logic, classifying the result as initials_only, possible_match, or mismatch depending on which checks pass.\n\nI'm testing edge cases like \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" (initials_only) and tricky similar-but-different names like \"SUNIL\" vs \"SUNITA,\" computing their Jaro-Winkler score by hand to confirm it lands just above the 0.85 threshold, which is acceptable since possible_match still routes to needs_verification rather than an automatic ok.\n\nI also want to update the old threshold documentation in systemPatterns to match the new scoring spec, and I'm planning out the transliteration mapping table covering Devanagari and Bengali/Assamese Unicode ranges, including how to handle consonants, vowels, matras, virama, anusvara, chandrabindu, visarga, and nukta marks.\n\nI'm confirming the shared offset table holds since ব aligns with Devanagari ब, while Bengali য (offset matching Devanagari य) needs a J override for its actual pronunciation. I'm also noting the special cases: ৰ, ৱ, য়→Y, ড়→R, ঢ়→RH, and the Devanagari nukta-form consonants starting at 0958 that need their own mappings.\n\nStill mapping consonants and vowel signs...\n\nStill mapping transliteration codes...\n\nI'm checking Bengali vowel letters and matras against the same codepoint offsets as Devanagari, confirming they align correctly, and now I need to handle word-final schwa deletion where a trailing consonant with no inherent vowel sign should drop the implicit \"a\" sound.\n\nTesting this on examples like \"रमेश\" → RAMESH, \"कुमार\" → KUMAR, and \"शर्मा\" → SHARMA confirms the transliteration logic works, including handling the compound conjunct क्ष as \"KSH\" and name variants like \"देवी\"/\"দেৱী\" becoming DEVI or DEBI depending on script.\n\nI should also check honorifics - श्री reliably becomes SHRI, and श्रीमती becomes SHRIMATI in both Devanagari and Assamese script. I'm building out a list of honorific variants to recognize: SHRI, SHREE, SRI, SREE, SHRIMATI, SMT, SMTI, MR, MRS, MS, MISS, KUMARI, LATE, and possibly SUSHRI.\n\nI'm skipping \"KU\" since it's too ambiguous and risks clashing with real name tokens. For KUMARI, since it can also appear as a genuine surname like \"Rinku Kumari,\" I'll only strip honorifics when they appear as leading tokens rather than anywhere in the string, applying this repeatedly to handle multiple leading honorifics safely. Now I'm setting up script detection logic to tag tokens as Latin, Devanagari, or Bengali based on character ranges.\n\nFor comparing names across scripts, I'm treating them as matching if their script sets are equal, so \"Ramesh कुमार\" and an equivalent purely-Devanagari or purely-Latin version would still count as the same under this looser rule, and trailing whitespace shouldn't break equality either.\n\nFor age calculation, I'm writing a function that takes a date of birth and an as-of date and returns integer years by comparing month-day tuples, subtracting one year if the as-of month/day falls before the birth month/day. I'm working out the edge case for people born on February 29 — deciding to treat their birthday as reached on March 1 in non-leap years, which aligns with common legal convention in India.\n\nI validate incoming date strings with a strict ISO date check so malformed dates like \"02-30\" get rejected, and the age function throws a RangeError for invalid inputs or when the as-of date precedes the birth date; the engine wraps this in a safe parse so failures route to a needs-verification state rather than crashing.\n\nNow I'm designing a function to mask Aadhaar numbers: it extracts digits, keeps only the last four, and formats the rest as masked groups, making sure the output never reveals more than four actual digits even if the input has fewer.\n\nI'm also sketching out the rejection code system — an enum mapping each code to bilingual titles and step-by-step resolution instructions in English and Assamese.\n\nFor the scheme registry, I'm planning an internal map that validates and loads built-in scheme rules at startup, and a `registerScheme` function that stores rules by ID while supporting multiple versions per scheme so older rule-pack versions remain accessible even after a newer one is published.\n\nFor testability, I'll expose a `createSchemeRegistry` factory alongside a default registry instance, letting tests build isolated registries instead of mutating global state. I'm still settling on a simple, consistent version string format for schemes.\n\nFor the exclusion questions, I need to write both English and Assamese phrasing, and since I'm not a native Assamese speaker, I should flag these translations for review by someone like Das rather than assume they're perfect. I'm drafting Assamese sentences using correct characters like ৰ and ৱ, checking phrasing such as asking whether any family member pays income tax.\n\nContinuing with income tax payer and practising professional exclusions, adding both English and Assamese translations for each.\n\nI also need to cover pension exclusions above ₹10,000/month, then move into the external PM-KISAN checks, starting with Aadhaar-bank account seeding verification.\n\nNext I'm drafting the ekyc_done item, explaining PM-KISAN eKYC completion via OTP on the portal or fingerprint at a CSC, with both English and Assamese phrasing, then moving into the land_verified item about checking land records at the circle office.\n\nNow I'm shifting to Orunodoi scheme requirements, starting with the mandatory ration card verification, then drafting the provisional nfsa_enrolled requirement asking whether the family is enrolled under NFSA on the ration card list, with Assamese translation.\n\nFor eligibility, I'm working through the \"any of\" criteria from the 2020 Orunodoi guidelines—widow status, disability, AAY card, or household income below ₹2 lakh—and drafting matching questions for each, considering also adding divorced/unmarried/separated women as a category under a single_woman flag.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the exclusions: elected representatives, professionals, government employees/teachers/pensioners, contractors or traders (possibly GST-registered), and large landholders owning more than 15 bighas, phrasing each as a simple yes/no question while noting these thresholds are provisional and need confirmation.\n\nI'll also add an \"other_cash_scheme\" question asking if the family already receives a similar state cash benefit, and mark the provenance as provisional, citing the Public Services Map secondary source for Orunodoi 3.0. For supporting docs I'll list aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, and self-declaration, with no external checks needed, and I'll note that name consistency should be checked across the ration card, aadhaar, and bank passbook where available.\n\nFor deciding the reference document in name-consistency checks, I'm leaning toward using aadhaar as the preferred anchor when present, falling back to whichever document appears first otherwise—since aadhaar is the more universal identity anchor compared to ration card ordering differences between schemes.\n\nNow moving to the Widow scheme, I'm setting the age range to 40–79 with verified provenance, and adding a verified requirement asking whether the household is BPL.\n\nFor mandatory documents I'm listing aadhaar, death certificate, bank passbook, and BPL proof, all marked provisional since they're commonly required but need local confirmation. The benefit is a central share of 300 per month with no state top-up, and I'm skipping external checks like bank seeding since they're not specified. I also need to figure out where to place the Assamese-language messages for these checks—likely in a separate source file rather than directly in evaluate.ts.\n\nI'm defining the fixWhere enum values like bank, aadhaar centre, circle office, and others, using null when there's no fix needed, then drafting bilingual place labels for each in messages.ts.\n\nAdding more office and document label translations...\n\nI'm deciding to simplify the death certificate label generically rather than specifying \"husband's,\" and considering adding docType IDs validated against a pattern, with an optional docLabels map in the scheme rule for extensibility to unknown doc types from other tasks. Now I'm moving on to drafting the function-based message templates.\n\nFor the missing-document message, I'm building bilingual text that names the missing document, optionally flags it as provisional needing local confirmation, and tells the user to get it before applying, alongside a lookup table mapping each doc type to where to fix it (aadhaar centre, bank, circle office, ration office, registrar, gram panchayat, etc.), followed by a simpler \"document found\" confirmation message for present docs.\n\nNow I'm handling the name-matching checks across documents: exact matches get a simple confirmation, possible matches or initials-only cases get a \"needs verification\" message explaining the discrepancy and suggesting the user ask the issuing authority to standardize the name, and full mismatches get an \"action required\" message showing both names and instructing the user to get the document corrected.\n\nFor land records specifically, I'm adding a note about family-member ownership. I'm also covering unreadable or missing name fields with a generic verification prompt, plus format checks for IFSC codes and account numbers (flagging malformed or missing values), and I'm starting to think through how criterion-level checks like exclusions, requirements, \"any of\" conditions, and age thresholds should be structured and labeled.\n\nNow I'm drafting the external and supporting criterion checks, always falling back to a needs_verification message explaining that photos alone can't confirm these facts, and defining the answers schema as nullable booleans where a \"true\" exclusion answer triggers ineligibility. I'm also writing the bilingual (English/Assamese) messaging for exclusion criteria, both for verified and provisional triggers.\n\nI'm adding a confirmAt location field to SchemeRule so provisional messages can point users to the right office (Gram Panchayat, CSC, or block office depending on the scheme), then handling the remaining answer states: unanswered prompts, \"no issue\" for non-triggered answers, and action_required vs needs_verification outcomes depending on whether the rule was verified or provisional, plus logic for anyOf-type requirement groups.\n\nFor age checks, I'm defining the eligible range logic (40–79), suggesting Old Age Pension for applicants 80+, and flagging missing or low-confidence DOB as needing verification, reading the age from the Aadhaar document by default. I'm setting a confidence threshold of 0.8 below which any field triggers needs_verification, and categorizing engine readiness checks into document, name, field, and criterion types as mandatory, while external and supporting checks are treated differently.\n\nEligibility is driven only by criterion and age checks; readiness status follows the same checks, so a failed or provisional criterion escalates to action_required or needs_verification accordingly. I'm also planning to add disclaimer message constants for English and Assamese, and sketching out the overall report schema fields like schemeId, schemeVersion, asOfDate, eligibility, and readiness.\n\nI want a property test confirming that a possible_match name-check never produces an \"ok\" result — generating name pairs via token substitutions (like KUMAR→KR, DEVI→DEBI) to reliably trigger possible_match cases and verify they map to needs_verification. I'm also planning the PM-KISAN fixture test data using a sample Aadhaar name.\n\nFor the actual scenario test, I'm confirming that a matching Aadhaar/bank/land record with identical names across all three sources yields likely_eligible status with ready_for_review readiness, while the Aadhaar-bank seeding check itself stays at needs_verification. I also want to verify that landholding size has zero effect on eligibility — adding a 50-hectare property shouldn't change the outcome, and I should check that no PM-KISAN criteria or exclusions reference land size at all.\n\nNow I'm working out a widow fixture's age calculation carefully, picking a date of birth that lands exactly on the \"as of\" date boundary so the age comes out to 39, since birthday timing matters for whether someone counts as having just turned a year older.\n\nI'm also sketching out eligibility tests for schemes: the widow case with BPL status yes and all docs present, benefit totals for central/state shares, and Orunodoi logic where missing ration card triggers action_required while owning a four-wheeler pushes eligibility to needs_verification. I'm also noting the benefitOn function signature taking a scheme rule and date, with overrides handling.\n\nNow I'm writing out how overrides are matched by date range, with later-listed entries taking precedence when ranges overlap, returning a benefit object with shares, period, and provenance info. I'm defining validation rules for overrides and scheme criteria, plus specifying provenance tracking at the scheme level for mandatory and supporting document checks.\n\nNow I'm drafting the SchemeRule schema itself, covering ID format, versioning, names, provenance, confirmation location, benefit structure, and document requirements for mandatory and supporting docs, along with name consistency document types.\n\nI'm wrestling with sourceUrl: should it be required for every rule, or nullable since some provisional T3 overrides lack an official notice URL? I'm landing on making sourceUrl nullable but adding a refinement rule that verified status requires a source URL to be present. Still deciding on optionality for clause, effectiveDate, and verifiedOn as ISO date fields.\n\nNow I'm defining the Check schema with fields like id, category, result enum, bilingual messages, fixWhere, provenance, optional docType, and an optional nameMatch object with level and score — thinking through where not_applicable gets used, like for an optional document such as an Orunodoi bank reference that wasn't submitted.\n\nI'm leaning toward treating missing supporting documents as needs_verification (advisory yellow) rather than not_applicable, since the report should flag them as commonly-asked items to confirm rather than silently skipping them. For name checks tied to absent optional docs, I'll just skip emitting a check entirely rather than using not_applicable, reserving that enum value for other edge cases like certain external checks.\n\nReadiness calculations should exclude not_applicable items, and low-confidence extracted fields like DOB should propagate into related criteria like age verification.\n\nNow I'm structuring the `evaluate` function itself — it takes the scheme, documents, answers, and as-of date, validates the as-of date format, and parses the docs and answers with zod schemas at the entry point for safety even though the engine assumes typed input. Validation failures should throw, with the expectation that the API route handles upstream validation separately.\n\nI'm defining the ExtractedDoc schema, specifying fields like name, DOB, Aadhaar last-4 digits, account number, IFSC, owner names, area in hectares, husband/deceased names, date of death, and ration card number, plus a confidence record keyed by field name with values between 0 and 1.\n\nUsing zod 4's partialRecord for the confidence map since keys don't need to be exhaustive, and tightening the account number regex to digits-only (9-18 length) and IFSC to a trimmed uppercase pattern. Now moving on to setting up the scheme registry file.\n\nI'm sketching out the overall file structure: types, messages (labels/places/templates), a transliterate module separate from nameMatch, plus age/aadhaar/benefit/evaluate/rejectionCodes modules, and the pm-kisan/orunodoi-3/nsap-widow scheme files feeding into the registry index — alongside matching test files for each core module.\n\nFor fixtures, I'm deciding where sample A/B/C data should live since they're mentioned in the brief and useful for testing, leaning toward a small `src/core/samples.ts` module even though it's slightly beyond the strict spec scope.\n\nI'll write the tests first to confirm they fail against missing modules, then implement the actual code afterward, double-checking the exported schema names across types.ts match what the tests expect.\n\nFor naming conventions, I'm settling on using the same identifier for both the zod schema and its inferred TypeScript type (e.g. `SchemeRule` as both value and type), which matches how the task describes registerScheme validating data against SchemeRule. I'm also sketching out the nameMatch function signature next.\n\nNow I'm planning the remaining module functions: name normalization and Jaro-Winkler scoring, transliteration, age calculation, Aadhaar masking, benefit date logic, the core evaluate function producing a Report, rejection code enums with fix steps, and the schemes index for lookup.\n\nI'm moving on to writing tests, starting with normalizeName and nameMatch cases covering honorifics, spacing, and transliteration edge cases involving Assamese names.\n\nI'm covering transliteration match cases (Devanagari/Bengali vs Latin spellings), reordered name matches, nickname-to-full-name matches like \"Md\" vs \"Mohammad\", and edge cases like empty strings or initials-only matches. I'm also setting up a property test ensuring a normalized name always matches itself.\n\nI'm defining a random name generator combining scripts and common name tokens, then outlining test properties: symmetry of match scoring, score bounded between 0 and 1, Jaro-Winkler symmetry, and that possible_match results never count as a perfect \"ok\" match. I also need to handle a \"fixWhere circle office\" transformation into \"circle_office\" as part of the spec, so now I'm moving on to writing out the actual test files starting with Step 0.\n\nNow I'm updating appInfo to switch the language from Hindi to Assamese, verifying the app title, English tagline, and Assamese tagline text, checking that Assamese-specific Unicode characters like ৰ appear correctly, and confirming APP_LANG is set to \"as\" for use in the layout's lang attribute, alongside a longer app description.\n\nI'm also drafting the description text explaining that KaagazCheck checks welfare-application papers and flags fixes needed, without guaranteeing approval, and setting up the evaluate test file with imports for the evaluation logic, scheme lookup, and name-matching utilities.\n\nStill writing test helpers...\n\nI'm setting up test cases for the orunodoi scheme, verifying answers like nfsa_enrolled and income_below_2l produce correct eligibility states, including a property test confirming orunodoi never yields not_eligible since all its criteria are provisional. For the widow scheme test, I'm building answer data with bpl_household plus documents like Aadhaar, death certificate, bank passbook, and BPL proof, all cross-referencing Sunita Devi's name.\n\nI'm also checking readiness logic for a case with 40 answers (ready_for_review, no external checks), a missing death certificate triggering action_required with a registrar fix location, and bpl false correctly yielding not_eligible. For low-confidence fields, I'm verifying that a dob confidence of 0.5 pushes both age check and eligibility to needs_verification, that low-confidence bank names trigger name verification in PM-KISAN, and that invalid IFSC codes produce action_required with a bank fix location. Finally, I'm writing a regression test for large landholding to confirm that even with an extra large_landholding flag, the scheme still resolves to likely_eligible and ready_for_review, with no criterion referencing hectare or landholding thresholds.\n\nNow I'm writing benefit override tests: checking that a scheme override for a specific date range applies a state top-up, that boundary dates are inclusive, that dates outside the range fall back to the base amount, and that the base scheme without overrides returns the default total. I'm also verifying PM-KISAN's benefit structure (central share, no state top-up, yearly period, three instalments) and starting to check the widow scheme's benefit values.\n\nFor the schemes test file, I'm confirming the scheme list contains the three expected IDs, each validates against the schema, and provenance statuses match documentation expectations per scheme (verified vs provisional for exclusions, requirements, mandatory docs, and benefits). I'm also checking that every question text includes both Assamese script and English, and starting to verify the PM-KISAN exclusion rules.\n\nFor the registry tests, I'm checking that createSchemeRegistry supports registering a cloned scheme, retrieving it via getScheme and listSchemes, throwing on duplicate id+version registration, replacing the latest version while keeping old versions retrievable, and rejecting invalid rules -- while also confirming a default registerScheme exists without mutating shared state. I'm then moving into the aadhaar masking tests, checking that a sample Aadhaar number gets properly masked.\n\nNext I'm verifying masking behavior with shorter digit strings and property-based tests ensuring the output always preserves the last four digits when at least four are present. For age calculations, I'm testing birthday boundaries, leap-year edge cases, invalid date handling, and property-based invariants like ageOn(d,d)=0 and monotonicity. Finally, for rejection codes I'm confirming all eight codes have non-empty English and Assamese steps (with actual Assamese script present), that an unknown code still returns fallback steps, and checking the RejectionCode type's safety.\n\nNow I'm moving on to actually writing the test files, aiming to be efficient but thorough.\n\nTooling is confirmed, so I'll proceed: first switching appInfo.ts and layout/page copy to Assamese and refreshing the memory-bank docs, then writing tests-first for the core modules (types, transliteration/name matching, age/aadhaar/benefit checks, scheme registry, and samples), with a design rule that external/supporting-doc checks stay \"needs_verification\" without downgrading the overall readiness verdict.\n\n"}}
