{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:43:08.045Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm sketching out the data model for the evaluate test, defining rule status types, provenance fields like source and verification date, and a document type enum covering things like aadhaar, bank passbook, land record, and other proof categories.\n\nI'm also thinking through extracted document fields with confidence scores and how answers map question IDs to yes/no responses. For the clean test case, I'm reasoning that external checks like bank seeding should surface as needs_verification at the check level without dragging down the overall readiness verdict, since the sample expects ready_for_review overall alongside a flagged bank seeding check.\n\nSo I'm resolving this by treating external checks as a separate category with an `affectsVerdict: false` flag, while possible_match, initials_only, and low confidence issues do downgrade readiness to needs_verification. I'm also considering eligibility logic — likely_eligible for the clean case, but needs_verification when exclusion questions go unanswered — and starting to think through how the land record owner check should compare against the applicant's name.\n\nFor the land record, I'll compare each owner name in ownerNames[] against the Aadhaar name, flagging a mismatch as action_required with circle_office as the fix location. For the family-member scenario, I'm considering adding a question like land_in_family_member_name, and if answered yes, downgrading the mismatch to needs_verification instead of action_required, though I recognize this adds some complexity beyond the original scope.\n\nI'm separating name-consistency checks into distinct IDs — name_bank_passbook (aadhaar vs bank) and name_land_record (aadhaar vs land owners) — each with its own fixWhere value (bank, circle_office, etc.), and settling on an enum of valid fixWhere locations like bank, aadhaar_centre, circle_office, gram_panchayat, pm_kisan_portal, csc, registrar, food_civil_supplies_office, block_office, self, and none. If Aadhaar itself is missing, I can't run any name comparison at all, so that check would need to be skipped.\n\nFor the reference document when Aadhaar is absent, I'm falling back to the first available document that has a name field, in a defined priority order. I'm also introducing a low-confidence threshold (around 0.75) — any field feeding into a check (name, dob, ifsc, account number) that falls below this triggers a needs_verification status rather than a flat match/mismatch, including the edge case where a match looks correct but confidence is low enough that it should still be confirmed.\n\nI'll settle on: any check whose underlying field has low confidence becomes needs_verification regardless of pass/fail, with a message like \"we couldn't read this clearly, please confirm.\" That keeps things consistent across document and criterion checks. Now I'm defining the check structure itself—fields like id, kind, verdict, result, and bilingual messages.\n\nFor the provenance field naming, there's some ambiguity since different parts of the spec use `provenance` vs `ruleProvenance`—I'll go with `provenance` to stay consistent with how SchemeRule criteria reference it elsewhere.\n\nFor eligibility logic, a failed verified criterion should mark that check as action_required, push eligibility to not_eligible, and also bubble up to readiness as action_required per the doc's definition. Then for aggregating overall readiness, I'm working out the priority order: any action_required check (excluding external/info types) wins first, otherwise needs_verification takes precedence.\n\nBut provisional criteria complicate this — a provisional trigger like the Orunodoi four-wheeler case should push eligibility to needs_verification rather than not_eligible, and readiness should follow the same needs_verification path since provisional rules fall under that category per the doc. For eligibility aggregation specifically, I'm treating any action_required from verified criteria as not_eligible, any needs_verification as needs_verification, and otherwise likely_eligible, noting Orunodoi's dependency on verified mandatory documents like the ration card.\n\nI'm separating readiness (missing ration card triggers action_required there) from eligibility (which depends only on criteria). For Orunodoi's provisional anyOf questions (widow, PwD, etc.), answering none as yes should push eligibility to needs_verification per the doc's rule about exclusionary answers, meaning most applicants land in needs_verification unless they confirm one of these categories — and I'm checking whether the ration card validity question maps to the mandatory document check for eligibility purposes.\n\nI'll fold the ration card requirement into mandatoryDocs for readiness instead, letting eligibility derive from the criteria list directly.\n\nNow I'm designing a generic, data-driven criteria model in SchemeRule so new schemes like IGNOAPS (age 60-79, BPL) can be registered easily. I'm defining criterion types: question-based criteria with exclusion logic and provenance/fix metadata, age-range criteria tied to document fields like DOB, \"any of\" groups for alternative qualifying questions, and external criteria for things that can't be verified from documents alone.\n\nI'm also working through how mandatory documents should behave when their provenance is only provisional—like the widow's death certificate—since schemes.md says a missing one should still produce action_required even though provisional rules normally shouldn't give definitive pass/fail results.\n\nI'm deciding whether BPL proof belongs as a mandatory doc alongside aadhaar, death_certificate, and bank_passbook, since the mandatory list explicitly mentions it; I'll include bpl_proof as its own docType in the mandatory set rather than treating it as supporting.\n\nFor name consistency, I need to compare names across documents per scheme — PM-KISAN uses aadhaar, bank_passbook, land_record, while Orunodoi uses ration_card and aadhaar/bank_passbook when available, preferring aadhaar as the reference when present and falling back to the first available doc otherwise. I'll structure this as a nameConsistency object with an ordered docTypes list plus provenance, where the reference is aadhaar if present, else the first doc in the list.\n\nFor field-level checks like IFSC format and account number validation on bank_passbook, I'm designing a data-driven fieldChecks array with id, docType, field, regex pattern as a string, labels, and provenance — keeping everything JSON serializable. When a required field is missing, I'll treat it as action_required rather than needs_verification, with a message telling the user we couldn't locate that field and to check the document.\n\nFor land ownership verification, I'm extending the name-consistency logic to use ownerNames from land_record documents, factoring in whether the applicant could be a family member listed as owner — this means possibly adding a dedicated scheme question to support that case in the UI, though I want to keep the implementation generic rather than hardcoding it.\n\nThinking through the UI, I need a `questionsFor(scheme)` helper that derives question lists from the criteria (exclusions, anyOf, BPL checks, etc.), with any extra standalone questions like the land ownership one handled separately from the main criteria structure.\n\nFor the name consistency config, I'm including an optional `familyQuestion` field alongside doc type and owner names field — death certificates don't have a reliable cross-reference for husband's name so I'll skip that check. For widow status, I'll treat it as a verified eligibility criterion tied to the death certificate evidence, adding a dedicated question for it.\n\nFor the age criterion, I'm deriving date of birth from Aadhaar as of the application date — missing or low-confidence DOB triggers needs_verification, while age 80+ falls into a not_eligible path.\n\nNow I'm defining the Answers type as a record mapping strings to booleans, and sketching the ExtractedDoc shape with optional fields like name, dob, aadhaar digits, account details, owner names array, husband/deceased names, and ration card number, each paired with an optional confidence score between 0 and 1. I'm deciding that a missing confidence value should default to 1, treating manually entered fields as fully confirmed by the user.\n\nI'm making sure the aadhaarLast4 field only allows a four-digit string to avoid storing full Aadhaar numbers, and I'm shaping the Report type to include eligibility, readiness, checks, benefit info, and disclaimers alongside supporting docs for the UI. I'll export these as zod schemas doubling as TypeScript types, so something like SchemeRule can be used both as a validator and a type in tests.\n\nFor the scheme registry, I'm designing `src/core/schemes/index.ts` with functions to get, list, and register schemes, validating each rule with the zod schema before storing it. Since new rule-pack versions get published while old ones are retained, I'm keeping a map of scheme id to an array of versions, throwing if the same id+version is registered twice, and letting getScheme return either the latest or a specific version while listSchemes surfaces the latest per id.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the FixWhere enum covering places like bank, aadhaar centre, circle office, and others, and whether it needs bilingual labels. I'm leaning toward putting a small `fixWhereLabel` map in core rather than i18n.ts, since it's minimal and helps the T2 layer later.\n\nFor the Assamese text itself, I need to write it carefully using the correct characters (ৰ and ৱ) and keep sentences simple, flagging that native speaker review is still needed per the brief. I'm working through key vocabulary terms—Aadhaar, bank passbook, land record/jomabondi, ration card, death certificate—choosing the clearest Assamese phrasing for each.\n\nContinuing through the terminology list: BPL proof, name match/mismatch, missing documents, office confirmation, bank branch, circle office, Gram Panchayat, Aadhaar centre, and phrasing for yes/no eligibility questions like income tax status.\n\nI'm also covering government employee, pension, professional categories (doctor, engineer, lawyer, chartered accountant, architect), constitutional posts, widow status, age, four-wheeler ownership, land ownership, and eKYC terms, noting I'll treat these translations as a working draft.\n\nNow I'm planning the message-generation design: checks produced by the engine using templates in a `src/core/messages.ts` module, with functions returning bilingual {en, as} pairs, plus a DOC_LABELS map for document type labels in both languages. For testing, I'm deciding to centralize sample documents A/B/C in `src/core/samples.ts` so they can be reused across test suites, using placeholder names like Ramesh Kumar for fixtures.\n\nFor the first evaluate test case (clean PM-KISAN sample A), I'm laying out the document set: Aadhaar details matching the applicant name and DOB, a bank passbook with matching name and valid account/IFSC, and a land record listing the owner name. With all exclusion answers false, I expect eligibility to come out likely_eligible, readiness ready_for_review, and the aadhaar-bank seeding check to return needs_verification.\n\nNow I'm working through variant cases: a mismatched bank name producing needs_verification readiness, a mismatched land owner name producing action_required with a fix pointing to the circle office, a missing land record forcing action_required on the land document check, and an income tax \"yes\" answer flipping eligibility to not_eligible. I'm also considering whether large landholding should actually affect eligibility at all — the regression expectation is that landholding size has no effect, so I'm testing by adding a landAreaHectares field to the land record and confirming that a large value like 25 hectares still produces the same likely_eligible, ready-for-review result as the clean case.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the possible_match logic: adding a matchLevel property to each check so that whenever a name comparison returns \"possible_match\" rather than an exact match, the corresponding check result should never evaluate to \"ok\" — I want a property-based test that randomly generates aadhaar and bank names and verifies that any non-\"same\" match level always yields a non-ok result, plus a direct test case for low-confidence aadhaar extraction producing a needs_verification outcome.\n\nI also want to confirm the Report schema validates output, that evaluate() is deterministic across repeated calls, and that provisional-criteria schemes like Orunodoi never produce a not_eligible result since they lack verified failing criteria.\n\nFor the Widow pension scheme, I'm working out sample date-of-birth values relative to the 2026-10-10 as-of date to test age boundaries at 40, 39, 79, and 80, then checking benefit calculations (centralShare 300, no stateTopUp) for a widow/BPL household, along with a sample case missing the death certificate document.\n\nMissing document should trigger action_required readiness, while age 80 should produce an Old Age Pension message and a non-BPL household should return not_eligible. For Orunodoi, missing ration card gives action_required readiness, owning a four-wheeler pushes eligibility to needs_verification, and when no anyOf conditions are met it should also be needs_verification. I'm now reasoning through the case where ration card and aadhaar names match, the widow anyOf condition is satisfied, and no exclusions apply — since provisional rules shouldn't yield a definitive pass or fail, likely_eligible seems like the right non-definitive outcome here.\n\nFor Orunodoi, I'm noting the valid ration card as a mandatory doc with external NFSA verification needed, and treating nfsa_enrolled as a provisional exclusion question. I should flag the supporting docs checklist for confirmation and check name consistency across ration card, aadhaar, and bank passbook, while deciding whether to note income certificate as not required in the scheme notes.\n\nI'm adding a notes array with provenance for things like Orunodoi's \"no income certificate required\" rule and the October one-time note. Then I'm mapping out external checks: PM-KISAN needs aadhaar-bank seeding, active bank account, eKYC, and land verification; the widow scheme doesn't list external checks so I'll leave that empty; and Orunodoi needs ration card validity confirmed by the food supply office.\n\nFor exclusion logic, I'm defining question criteria with an `excludeIf` boolean, where unanswered questions trigger a \"needs_verification\" status. For the anyOf eligibility groups, I'm modeling it as an array of questions each with their own id, label, and provenance, where the group counts as satisfied if any one question is answered true, provisional if any are still pending, and excluded only if all are answered false.\n\nI'm settling on separating criteria into `requirements` (must answer yes) and `exclusions` (must answer no) arrays rather than a unified excludeIf flag, which keeps the data model cleaner and matches the naming convention already established. Each Question will carry id, bilingual text, provenance, and failure messages for when the criteria isn't met.\n\nFor provisional eligibility messaging, I'm designing the engine to compose a message like \"This may make you ineligible: {failEn}. Confirm at {where}\", where the office location comes from a per-scheme `fixWhere` field. I need to create bilingual labels for these office locations in the core module, so I'll add a `FIX_WHERE_LABELS` constant.\n\nNow I'm fleshing out other field check types: ageRange needs below/above-minimum messages (with an optional hint to suggest Old Age Pension for widows above the max), and mandatoryDocs needs \"Missing: {label}. Get it from {where}\" messaging with a provisional caveat to confirm locally. For supportingDocs, I'm deciding these should appear in the report as a separate checklist rather than as pass/fail checks, since they're informational.\n\nFor name consistency, I'm designing a structure with a preferred document order (like Aadhaar first, then ration card) to pick a reference name, then comparing other present documents against it — skipping absent docs, flagging \"needs verification\" if no reference document exists, and allowing scheme-specific follow-up questions like the family name question for land-based schemes.\n\nFor matching names, I'd pull the best-matching owner name for land records (by rank and score) versus a simple name field for other docs, and generate mismatch messages naming both documents and correction locations — acknowledging these carry personal data so they belong in reports, not logs. I'm also sketching a check object with id, kind (document/name/field/criterion/external), and verdict fields to standardize how each validation result is represented.\n\nNow I'm working out how readiness aggregates: it rolls up every check except external ones, so any action_required or needs_verification status bubbles up to the overall readiness state, including cases where eligibility fails or criteria remain provisional. Eligibility itself aggregates separately — any action_required check flips the whole eligibility verdict to not_eligible.\n\nI'm setting a low-confidence threshold of 0.75 for check fields, and defining how evaluate validates inputs — parsing the scheme rule, accepting typed answers, and validating asOfDate as an ISO date string, throwing if invalid. I'm also sketching the benefit model structure with fields for central share, state top-up, period, installments, effective date, provenance, and an overrides array supporting date ranges with optional share adjustments and bilingual notes.\n\nNow I'm working through how overrides apply: each override must start on or after the base effective date, and benefitOn(scheme, date) computes the actual benefit for a given date by checking overrides first, falling back to base values, and returning the central share, top-up, total, period, installment count, override flag, notes, provenance, and effective date — with override shares being optional so they fall back to base values when omitted, and ignoring the effective date if it's in the future relative to the query date. For PM-KISAN specifically, I'm setting central share to 6000 per year, paid in 3 installments.\n\nI'm also sketching out the provenance schema — making sourceUrl nullable since some sample notices lack a URL, with optional clause and effective date, plus required verifiedOn date, version string, and status fields. Both scheme and provenance versions are set to \"2.0.0\" to match rule pack v2.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how the evaluate module and test samples connect — three sample cases (A, B, C) each bundling a scheme ID, documents, answers, and bilingual labels, which I'll use to build inline test documents via helper functions. I'm also finalizing the registry signature as getScheme(id, version?) returning a SchemeRule or undefined, and standardizing ID naming conventions like doc_<docType>, name_<docType>, field_<id>, and age-related fields.\n\nWith the ID scheme for req_<qid>, excl_<qid>, anyof, and ext_<id> settled, I'm moving on to writing the evaluate.test.ts file.\n\n"}}
