{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:50:02.540Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I need land_in_family_member_name to be recognized as a valid answer key for PM-KISAN, since land records may show a family member as owner rather than the applicant. I'm checking how my scheme tests iterate through requirements, eligibilityAnyOf, and exclusions to figure out where this question should be registered.\n\nFor the name check structure, I could attach an optional family-owner question directly to each document name check, and have questionsFor collect those embedded questions too -- that keeps things simple without disrupting provenance tests. I also want to verify ready_for_review behavior for PM-KISAN cases with mismatched land ownership names, exclusion questions answered, and edge cases like name tokens that normalize to empty (e.g., just \"Smt.\" alone).\n\nI'm deciding that if a name normalizes to empty, that should trigger needs_verification rather than a hard mismatch, since it's really an unreadable name rather than a confirmed mismatch. For the reference document design across schemes, I'm setting Orunodoi's reference to ration_card with checks against aadhaar and bank_passbook, PM-KISAN's reference to aadhaar with checks against bank_passbook and land_record, and working out the widow scheme's reference now too.\n\nMissing reference or compared documents should just mark name checks as not_applicable rather than failing them, since the doc-level check already flags the missing document itself. For land records, I'm picking the best-matching owner name among possibly multiple owners. For bank passbook fields like IFSC and account number, I'm normalizing IFSC to uppercase/trimmed and stripping spaces from account numbers, with missing passbook or fields leading to not_applicable or needs_verification, and pattern failures escalating to action_required. I'm also working through how exclusion questions interact with verification status—verified-and-yes answers should push eligibility to not_eligible with action_required.\n\nFor requirement questions like bpl_household or is_widow, verified-and-no answers trigger action_required/not_eligible, while provisional-and-no needs verification; I'm treating is_widow as verified since it's a core scheme criterion. For anyOf eligibility groups, any single \"yes\" answer makes it ok, while all-no or unanswered-with-no-yes triggers needs_verification or action_required depending on provisional status. For age/ageRange checks, I'm pulling DOB from Aadhaar when available, preferring it over other documents.\n\nMissing or invalid DOB, or low-confidence extraction, always triggers needs_verification, while out-of-range ages escalate to action_required if verified. Above-max ages get a scheme-specific hint message. External checks (ext_) always resolve to needs_verification since they can't be auto-verified. The overall eligibility verdict aggregates across eligibility-kind checks: any action_required makes it not_eligible, any needs_verification makes it needs_verification, otherwise likely_eligible — this holds because only verified criteria produce action_required and provisional ones produce needs_verification instead.\n\nFor readiness, I'm computing it from all non-external checks: action_required wins if any check (including an eligibility check tied to an unmet verified criterion) is action_required, and needs_verification otherwise if any non-external check needs it. External checks like bank seeding don't factor into the readiness verdict at all — they stay as their own needs_verification state without pulling readiness down, which matches the test expectations.\n\nI'm setting a low-confidence threshold constant at 0.8, below which a field gets flagged needs_verification, and treating missing confidence values as fully confident by default (e.g. manual entries or samples). I'm also working out document handling — missing mandatory docs trigger action_required with provenance tracking, while a present doc with low-confidence doc-type classification can just be skipped. Then I'm shaping the final report structure: scheme identifiers, as-of date, eligibility and readiness verdicts, the checks array, benefit info, a supporting docs list with bilingual labels and presence flags, plus notes and disclaimer text.\n\nI need to make sure the report never exposes a full Aadhaar number — only the last four digits should ever be stored or shown, enforced via a schema regex requiring exactly four digits. I'm also reconciling naming between the spec and tests: the field is called `provenance` in the check schema, not `ruleProvenance`, since that's what the tests expect. Now I'm defining the fixWhere enum values (bank, aadhaar_centre, circle_office, csc, pm_kisan_portal, block_office, self, none, etc.) and deciding that passing checks should default to \"none\" for fixWhere.\n\nI'm adding \"food_supply_office\" and \"birth_death_registrar\" as fixWhere options for ration card and death certificate cases, and settling on \"none\" for cases like income tax exclusions where there's nothing to physically fix at an office. For name mismatches, I'm mapping each proof type to its correct office: bank passbook to the bank, land records to circle office, Aadhaar to Aadhaar centre, and ration card to the food supply office.\n\nNow I'm weighing where message templates should live—projectbrief says all UI and check messages belong in one src/i18n.ts with English/Assamese keys, but check logic in the core module needs messageEn/messageAs per check. I'll put the check message templates in src/core/messages.ts for now and leave a note that the later i18n.ts work might re-consolidate them, flagging the Assamese strings for native-speaker review.\n\nFor document types, I'm listing out the DocType enum—aadhaar, bank passbook, land record, ration card, death certificate, bpl proof, voter ID, age proof, address proof, self declaration, income certificate, other—and considering whether to keep it a strict enum or allow a free string for extensibility. I'll stick with an enum plus a labels map so the UI gets consistent display text, since the scheme eligibility checks only need a handful of these document types covered.\n\nI also need a Question structure with bilingual text fields, provenance, and a \"where to ask\" hint, plus logic for provisional exclusions where I prompt users to confirm at the Gram Panchayat. I'll give each scheme a default confirmAt location and let individual questions override it, and add short bilingual labels to questions so exclusion messages can reference what the user answered concisely.\n\nI'm now shifting to designing the name-matching algorithm, which feels like the most critical piece to get right.\n\nI keep expanding the honorific list (KUMARI, LATE, SH, DR...) and working through tricky edge cases like \"Late Shri Ram-Prasad\" or \"MR. R.K. Sharma\" to make sure only leading honorifics get stripped while legitimate name tokens or initials like \"M.S.\" aren't mistakenly dropped.\n\nI'm testing transliterations across scripts: \"শ্রীমতী\" to \"SHRIMATI\", Bengali \"সুনীতা দেবী\" to \"SUNITA DEBI\", Assamese \"ৰমেশ\" to \"RAMESH\", and Hindi names like \"रमेश कुमार शर्मा\" to \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMA\". I'm working out the core algorithm for Indic abugida scripts — each consonant carries an inherent 'a' vowel unless followed by a virama or vowel sign, and the final inherent schwa gets dropped at word-end, as seen breaking down \"रमेश\" into R+A, ME, and final SH.\n\nChecking a few more examples confirms the pattern holds: \"कुमार\" to KUMAR, \"शर्मा\" to SHARMA using virama rules, \"सुनीता\" to SUNITA, \"देवी\" to DEVI, and \"श्री\" to SHRI. Medial schwa deletion is trickier but I don't think I need to handle it precisely, since Jaro-Winkler similarity scoring should tolerate small spelling variations and still produce a possible match.\n\nNow checking Bengali examples: \"ৰমেশ\" to RAMESH, \"সুনীতা\" to SUNITA, \"দেবী\" to DEBI, \"শ্ৰীমতী\" to SHRIMATI, \"শৰ্মা\" to SHARMA. I'm also noting how anusvara, chandrabindu, and visarga diacritics map to N, N, and H respectively across scripts.\n\nI'm mapping the remaining special cases: nukta gets ignored for simplicity, khanda ta becomes T, and I'm listing out the independent vowel equivalents (A, I, U, RI, E, AI, O, AU) and their matra forms across Devanagari and Bengali, including the two-part Bengali O/AU vowel signs that need NFC normalization. I'm also deciding to treat Assamese ৱ as W.\n\nNow I'm working out the consonant mappings, noting that Devanagari and Bengali share the same ISCII-style offset layout, so I can build one shared lookup table keyed by offset from the block start, with Bengali-specific overrides like ড়/ঢ় for R/RH sounds and য/য়/জ distinctions for J/Y, since ব at the same offset as ब naturally maps to B.\n\nContinuing through the rest of the consonant range: cha, chha, ja, jha, nya, retroflex ta/tha/da/dha, na variants, pa, pha, ba, bha, ma, ya, ra variants, la variants, va, sha/ssa, sa — each mapped to its standard romanized letter.\n\nThen I'm covering ha, plus the nukta-formed extras (qa, kha, gha, za, dda, ddha, fa, ya) and the Bengali-specific letters like ড়, ঢ়, য়, ৰ, ৱ, and ৎ which has no inherent vowel.\n\nNow I'm listing the independent vowel letters and their offsets — a, aa, i, ii, u, uu, vocalic r, vocalic l, e, ai, o — each with its code position in the vowel block.\n\nI'm continuing through au and the Devanagari candra/short e and o variants, then mapping the dependent vowel signs (matras) to their corresponding vowel sounds, along with the virama, nukta, anusvara, candrabindu, and visarga marks, while noting which marks like avagraha and length marks get skipped, digits get stripped, and danda punctuation converts to a space.\n\nNow I'm working out schwa deletion rules — dropping the inherent \"a\" at the end of words except for single-consonant words like \"न\" which stay as \"NA\" — and planning the implementation by iterating through characters while tracking a pending vowel flag after each consonant is output.\n\nFor the non-Latin case I need to NFC-normalize first so composed forms like Bengali vowel signs stay intact, then do the transliteration, then only afterward NFKD-strip any leftover combining marks and uppercase. I'm also working through a tricky edge case with Bengali ড় where NFC treats it as a composition exclusion, decomposing into the base consonant plus nukta, which then just falls back to the plain consonant since nukta gets ignored.\n\nInstead I need an explicit nukta map so that consonant+nukta combos like ড়→R, ঢ়→RH, য়→Y, জ়→Z, ফ়→F are handled correctly, while other nukta forms just ignore the diacritic. I'm planning to track the last emitted consonant so when a nukta appears I can go back and replace it according to the map, likely using an array of output segments rather than a plain string so replacement is easy. I also need script detection logic distinguishing Devanagari and Bengali code point ranges to compute the correct offset for lookups.\n\nNow I'm working out the comparison logic between two normalized names: handling empty inputs as mismatches, detecting if either name uses Indic script, and defining \"same\" as identical after formatting normalization (case, punctuation, spacing, joined vs. spaced words, honorifics) while reserving \"possible_match\" for cases involving transliteration across scripts.\n\nChecking \"R AMESH KUMAR\" style splits confirms they'd still match since comparisons are symmetric regardless of Jaro or JW prefix handling. If names still don't match exactly, I need to tokenize both names and canonicalize common variant spellings — like MD/MOHD/MOHAMMED all mapping to MOHAMMAD, DEBI to DEVI, SK/SHEIKH variants, and similar phonetic substitutions — using a small lookup table before comparing tokens.\n\nI'm also considering whether to fold letters like W→V→B so names like DEVI and DEBI naturally align, though per the matching rules this case already falls under \"possible_match\" either way, so I'll keep an explicit variant table alongside the JW score rather than relying purely on phonetic folding.\n\nNow I'm working out how to detect initials — cases like \"R. Kumar\" matching \"Ramesh Kumar\", or \"R K Sharma\" matching \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" — by checking whether single-letter tokens align with the first letters of the corresponding full tokens on the other side.\n\nI need to decide priority ordering: check initials_only before variant/JW scoring — if one side has a single-letter token aligning with a full word at the same position, classify as initials_only regardless of similarity score, ranking below possible_match but above mismatch. Then for possible_match, I'm defining it as canonical token sequences matching after variant normalization.\n\nI'm worried that \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" vs \"Rameshwar Sharma\" could score high enough on JW to falsely register as a possible match, so I need to trace through the Jaro-Winkler matching window calculation carefully to see if \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" would cross that threshold too.\n\nGiven that whole-string JW is too lenient for names with long shared prefixes, I should switch to a token-level comparison instead—breaking names into tokens, requiring the same token counts, and checking each token pair rather than relying on raw string similarity.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether whole-string JW alone works as the rule, since it might incorrectly pass \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" as a match. I'll go with per-token JW ≥ 0.85 requiring matching token counts instead, documenting this as a deliberate deviation, and also need to handle cases where token counts differ due to joined name pairs.\n\nI'm deciding this edge case should resolve as possible_match rather than ok, since that outcome is always flagged for verification anyway and errs safe. For the matching rule, I'll require every token pair to hit JW ≥ 0.85, not just the average. For scoring, I'll use the JW of the normalized spaced strings (not the canonical compact form) rounded to three decimals, keeping it symmetric and bounded at 1.\n\nTransliterated names like \"रमेश कुमार शर्मा\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" normalize identically and would score 1 even though they're flagged possible_match, which is acceptable since the docs make clear score never promotes a result to ok by itself. I'll avoid artificially capping non-identical scores below 1 and instead just rename my test title so it doesn't falsely claim only identical inputs score 1.\n\nFor the reflexivity property, nameMatch(s,s) needs to hold for any s, checking that trivial equality in the compact format always works, and walking through edge cases where normalizeName might end up empty even though the compact format matches, like with Tamil script names.\n\nFor honorific dropping, I'm checking leading tokens against the honorifics list after transliteration and uppercasing, confirming \"SMT.\" gets dropped so that comparing two honorific-only strings correctly yields no match rather than a false positive.\n\nThen I'm defining normalizeName to transliterate, strip diacritics via NFKD, uppercase, replace non-letters with spaces, collapse whitespace, and drop leading honorifics -- verifying \"MR. R.K. Sharma\" reduces to \"R K SHARMA\" as expected. I'm also confirming the token-sorting and initials-matching logic stays symmetric, and that transliteration won't throw on weird unicode inputs from property-based fuzzing, including edge cases for jaroWinkler symmetry with surrogate pairs.\n\nTo be safe, I decide to normalize the Jaro computation by always ordering inputs as (shorter, longer) before running the greedy matching, avoiding any subtle asymmetry in match counts between directions.\n\nI then settle on the Jaro-Winkler prefix boost, applying the standard 0.1 scaling factor up to a 4-character prefix, only when the base Jaro similarity exceeds 0.7, and verify it against the MARTHA/MARHTA example to confirm it yields 0.961. I also define empty-string edge cases: both empty returns 1, one empty returns 0, matching the expected identical-string behavior.\n\nTesting name variants, I check \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" against \"SUNITA DEBI\" — after normalization and DEBI/DEVI canonicalization these resolve to a possible match. Then I verify initials-based matching: \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" and \"R K Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" both correctly resolve as initials-only matches by comparing single-letter tokens positionally against full tokens, and I'm now checking \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" for similar single-letter token handling.\n\nI'm also confirming \"SHARMA RAMESH KUMAR\" matches \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" via sorted canonical comparison, and \"Md Rafiqul Islam\" matches \"Mohammad Rafiqul Islam\". For edge cases like \"R\" vs \"S\", since the letters aren't equal, initials matching should fail and fall through to a token-level similarity check, which would also register as a mismatch given the low similarity between single letters — so I'm refining the logic to require each initial pair to match exactly or each full-token pair to meet a similarity threshold.\n\nChecking the \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" vs \"Rameshwar\" case confirms mismatch since token counts differ and no initial or sorted match applies — all the matching steps remain symmetric, including positional initial checks and sorting. For transliteration, I'm confirming Latin text just gets uppercased without stripping punctuation.\n\nI trace through consonant-virama-matra sequences like \"श्री\" and \"शर्मा\" to verify the inherent-A insertion logic works correctly, flushing a pending \"A\" only when the next character isn't part of a conjunct, which produces \"SHRI\" and \"SHARMA\" as expected. Testing a Bengali example \"শ্ৰীমতী\" similarly confirms the pending-A logic transliterates correctly to \"SHRIMATI\".\n\nI check anusvara handling too — in \"संजय\" the anusvara forces an early flush of the pending vowel before continuing, giving \"SANJAY\" — confirming the lookahead-based word-end detection is sound. Now I'm moving on to write the actual transliterate.ts file, then shift to validating date logic for ageOn, checking whether ISO date parsing correctly rejects invalid dates like February 30th.\n\nI decide to write my own date parser rather than lean on zod for this, using a regex plus a UTC round-trip check to catch invalid dates. For the age calculation, I work out the leap-year birthday edge case by comparing encoded month-day values, verifying the logic against several test dates around February 29th and confirming each gives the expected age.\n\nFor property testing dates, I plan to generate valid dates via fc.date and rely on ISO string comparison matching chronological order. Then I think through maskAadhaar: stripping non-digits, returning a masked placeholder if too short, otherwise showing \"XXXX XXXX \" plus the last four digits, and I start sketching the shape of a SchemeRule benefit object with fields like centralShare, stateTopUp, period, and optional instalments and effective date.\n\nI'm designing BenefitOverride to require \"to\" dates on or after \"from\", with optional centralShare/stateTopUp that fall back to the base scheme if omitted, and a benefitOn function that resolves the correct override (preferring the most recently listed one if several match) and computes totals, periods, and provenance fields. I'm also considering whether sourceUrl in provenance should be nullable to support provisional rules without an official URL yet, and whether to require verification before treating a rule as authoritative.\n\nFor the schema layer, I'm deciding to use z.input types so registerScheme accepts raw input shapes like version strings directly, and confirming the test cases for overrides and registry spreads match this structure correctly.\n\nI'm designing the registry as a module-level map keyed by scheme id holding arrays of versioned rules, with getScheme resolving the latest by semver comparison when no version is given, and registerScheme rejecting duplicate id+version pairs. I'm validating ids against a lowercase-hyphenated regex and versions against a semver pattern, confirming the test's \"Bad Id!\" and explicit version lookups align with this design, and planning to add a list-type helper alongside get/register.\n\nNow I'm defining the question schema, requiring each question's English text to end in \"?\" and mapping each scheme's requirements, eligibility, exclusions, and name-check prompts into that shape. I'm also sketching the SchemeRule structure with fieldChecks for IFSC/account-number validation plus nameChecks and notes, since the provenance test expects all three to be included.\n\nI'm thinking through field-specific normalization for the evaluate step—stripping whitespace and hyphens, uppercasing where needed, so account numbers like \"3001 2345 678\" validate correctly against the regex.\n\nI'm also sketching the structure for external checks (with label, instructions, and fix location) and a supporting docs section, including scheme-level notes that can be null when not applicable, plus a per-doc presence report.\n\nFor mandatory docs, I'm debating whether provisional rules should still be allowed to give a definitive pass/fail—since the golden rule says provisional rules shouldn't be definitive, but the spec and tests expect missing mandatory docs (like a death certificate) to trigger action_required even under provisional provenance. I'm reconciling this by treating action_required as a readiness signal rather than an eligibility determination, which keeps it consistent with the provisional rule.\n\nFor the widow case, I'm working through which documents are required and checked: Aadhaar and bank passbook both trigger action_required if missing, the death certificate just needs to be present (name matching against the applicant isn't necessary since it holds the husband's name), and BPL proof doesn't need a name check. I'm also confirming the is_widow and bpl_household verification flags map correctly to not_eligible when false, and starting to think through the PM-KISAN external data checks next.\n\nI'm listing provisional exclusions like elected representatives, professionals, govt staff/pensioners, contractors, large landholders, four-wheeler owners, and similar scheme beneficiaries, using \"excl_\" prefixed ids. For the anyOf eligibility criteria, I'm narrowing to the four explicitly listed categories—widow, PwD, AAY cardholder, and income below ₹2 lakh—while noting the \"etc.\" in the source docs leaves some ambiguity, and flagging a possible conflict since official guidance says no income certificate is required despite income being a criterion.\n\nFor PM-KISAN, the land record document covers the landholding requirement, plus I'm adding a check for whether land is in a family member's name versus the applicant's, triggering a \"needs_verification\" response with a message to confirm at the circle office if there's a mismatch flagged by the name check. I'm also setting low-confidence name matches on the ownerNames field to trigger needs_verification.\n\nFor the name check logic on land records, I'm picking the best match from the ownerNames array by rank then score, flagging \"Owner name not readable\" if that array is empty or missing. For mismatches I'm referencing the document's fixWhere and labels in the message, for possible matches I'm using the docs' own message, and for initials-only cases I'm asking the person to write the full name matching Aadhaar. For Orunodoi, the reference document is the ration card, checking against Aadhaar and bank passbook, with messaging that tells the person which document to correct to make the names identical.\n\nI'm also thinking through category prefixes like doc_, name_, field_, excl_/req_/anyof/age for eligibility, and ext_ for external checks, making sure name check IDs won't collide if a scheme has multiple checks on the same document type. For the evaluate function signature, I need to validate the asOfDate input and consider parsing docs and answers through zod schemas for defense, even though scheme validation already happens at registration time.\n\nIf there are multiple docs of the same type I'll just use the first one, and answers map to a record of string-to-boolean where unanswered values are undefined. I'm working out the messaging logic for exclusions and requirements: verified exclusions answered \"yes\" should be action_required with no fix path, provisional exclusions answered \"yes\" should prompt verification at the confirming office with a suggested question, and unanswered questions should prompt the user to answer with needs_verification status.\n\nI'm defining a \"fixWhere\" enum with options like \"applicant\" (user answers/confirms themselves) and \"none\", and working through the remaining cases: exclusion answered \"no\" means not excluded, requirement answered \"yes\" satisfies it, and requirement answered \"no\" triggers either action_required or needs_verification depending on verification status. For anyOf question groups, I'll pull provenance from the first question in the group. I also need to write careful Assamese translations for the UI text, marking them for native review, and I'm building out office label translations for things like bank branches in both English and Assamese.\n\nI'm continuing to map out the remaining location and role options — supply office, registrar, applicant, and the \"nothing to fix\" case — each in both English and Assamese.\n\nI also need to lay out the document labels: Aadhaar card, bank passbook, land record, ration card, husband's death certificate, BPL proof, voter ID, and age proof, each bilingual.\n\nNow I'm working through the rejection codes, figuring out which ones route to bank correction versus Aadhaar centre correction—name mismatch and Aadhaar-not-seeded issues both point back to the bank as the primary fix.\n\nI'm mapping the remaining codes: land not verified goes to the circle office, eKYC pending points to the PM-KISAN portal, pending state action routes to the block office, and anything unclear defaults to the CSC. Then I move on to defining the extracted document fields—name, date of birth with loose validation, Aadhaar last four digits, account number, IFSC, owner names, husband's name, and land area in hectares.\n\nFor the remaining fields like ration card number and address, I'm keeping only the minimal personal data needed and considering making the schema strict so it rejects any unexpected fields like a full Aadhaar number, which helps with privacy even though the account number itself still needs to stay since the check depends on it.\n\nFor confidence scoring, I'm using a partial record keyed by field name with values bounded between 0 and 1, leveraging zod's partialRecord with the FieldName enum.\n\nNow I'm sketching out the sample data: three fake applicant samples (A, B, C) covering a clean PM-KISAN case, a case with bank details and land info, and a widow case missing a death certificate, each with document sets and answers consistent with their scenario, plus checking that the schemes test data is structured correctly.\n\nI'm verifying listSchemes returns the latest version for each scheme since the widow scheme registration happens later in test order, then tracing the import paths and circularity between schemes, types, name-matching, age, and benefit modules. Now I'm defining the RuleProvenance shape with source URL, title, clause, effective date, verification date, version, and status, plus a refinement ensuring verified entries always have a non-null source URL.\n\nNext I'm sketching out the full Scheme type: id, version, bilingual names and descriptions, a confirmAt location for provisional rules, benefit details, mandatory and supporting docs with optional notes, name reference and matching rules, field checks, an optional age range with bilingual hint messages, plus requirements, eligibility conditions, exclusions, and external links.\n\nI'm defining the age check logic: pulling DOB from a configurable document type (defaulting to Aadhaar), then generating bilingual messages depending on whether the applicant's age falls within, below, or above the allowed range, with extra hints for the above-range case.\n\nI'm also setting the engine's disclaimer text clarifying that it flags preventable mistakes but doesn't guarantee approval, and finalizing the enum of possible confirmation locations (bank, aadhaar centre, circle office, gram panchayat, block office, CSC, PM-Kisan portal, supply office, registrar, applicant, or none) before starting on types.ts.\n\nNow I'm structuring the check schema with fields for id, kind, result, bilingual messages, fix location, provenance, and an optional name-match result, making sure optional fields serialize cleanly through JSON without breaking report equality checks. Then I shift to drafting the Assamese message strings, working out how to properly attach locative suffixes to office names like gram panchayat.\n\nI realize appending suffixes directly gets awkward with names containing parentheses, like revenue circle office, so I decide it's safer to just avoid parenthetical labels and instead use a simpler \"স্থান: {office}\" pattern for location references rather than trying to inflect every office name correctly.\n\nNow I'm drafting the message functions for document status: one for present documents and one for missing documents that notes the office to get them from.\n\nNow I'm drafting the \"name matches\" template with examples like আধাৰ কাৰ্ডৰ সৈতে and ৰেচন কাৰ্ডৰ সৈতে, then working through the \"possible match\" case where names look similar but may differ across documents — noting officials might still reject it and advising the person to ask the relevant office to make them identical, while being mindful that including actual names in these messages involves personal data.\n\nI'll add the Assamese version of the initials mismatch too, then draft a general \"mismatch\" template: name on {doc} (\"{b}\") doesn't match {ref} (\"{a}\"), asking the office to correct {doc} accordingly, with both English and Assamese phrasing.\n\nI'm also drafting a \"family owner\" case where the land record owner differs from {ref} because it's registered under a family member's name, with guidance to confirm at {office} whether this is accepted for immediate family (husband, wife, minor children), again in both English and Assamese.\n\nI'm also writing cases for unreadable or low-confidence name reads on {doc}, prompting the user to check the photo, type the name manually, or confirm the detected name matches the document.\n\nNow I'm drafting field validation messages — confirming when a field's format looks correct versus flagging it as invalid and directing the user to check it at {office}. For sensitive fields like account numbers, I'm considering privacy: showing only the last 4 digits (e.g., \"ends in 1234\") rather than the full number, while IFSC codes can be shown in full since they aren't personally identifying.\n\nFor invalid account formats, I'm shifting to show expected digit counts instead of raw values — \"Account number has 4 digits; expected 9–18\" — and generalizing the error message to use a hint pulled from the field's validation rule, like \"11 characters, like SBIN0001234\" for IFSC. I'm also drafting messages for missing fields and low-confidence OCR reads, prompting the user to check the photo or confirm manually, and starting on the exclusion confirmation message for when someone answers \"yes\" to a disqualifying group question, in both English and Assamese.\n\nContinuing with the English and Assamese message pairs for exclusion, unanswered, and requirement states—covering met requirements, unmet requirements with a \"no\" answer, and provisional requirements needing confirmation at the office.\n\nStill writing Assamese translations...\n\nContinuing with the low-confidence date-of-birth message and the external verification disclaimer, both in English and Assamese, followed by the start of a broader disclaimer about what help is being offered.\n\nNow I'm drafting field labels for IFSC code and account number, plus the Assamese phrasing for document references like Aadhaar card, bank passbook, and land records (jamabandi).\n\nI'm simplifying the land record label to avoid awkward postposition attachment, keeping it as just \"land record\" with jamabandi noted parenthetically, and moving on to draft the PM-KISAN scheme questions, starting with whether anyone in the family holds or has held a constitutional post.\n\nI'm continuing the translations: government employee status excluding MTS/Class IV/Group D, income tax payment in the last assessment year, and beginning the practising professional question.\n\nI'm also translating the land ownership question about whether the plot is registered under a spouse or minor child's name instead of the applicant's own, then noting the PM-KISAN sourcing details and exclusion category reference for provenance tracking.\n\nNow I'm drafting the external verification items like Aadhaar-bank seeding (with bilingual labels and instructions to check at the bank branch) and account activity status, continuing to build out this list of checks a user would need to confirm.\n\nI should fix the odd apostrophe to \"বায়োমেট্ৰিক\". For land_verified, I'm noting the check instructions: verify beneficiary status on pmkisan.gov.in or ask the circle office, phrased in Assamese too.\n\nNow I'm defining field validation for PM-KISAN, starting with the IFSC pattern check on the bank passbook, requiring the standard 11-character format like SBIN0001234.\n\nI'm also adding the account number pattern, requiring 9 to 18 digits with matching Assamese hints. I'm weighing whether Orunodoi/widow schemes should have similar field checks too, but since the docs don't specify IFSC validation for widow pension, I'll keep these detailed field checks limited to PM-KISAN to stay aligned with the documentation. I'm mapping out the nameChecks for PM-KISAN referencing aadhaar, bank passbook, and land record fields, then starting on the Orunodoi 3.0 (Assam) scheme entry.\n\nFor Orunodoi, I'm setting confirmation at the gram panchayat level, marking the ration card as mandatory and verified with supply office as the fix location, and noting the state top-up benefit of ₹1250/month. I'm adding a note that no income certificate is required per the official page, in both English and Assamese, and deciding not to add an override for the reported one-time ₹1,500 October 2026 payment since docs treat it as a temporary anomaly rather than the recurring benefit amount.\n\nI'm also adding a verified note about preliminary selection being based on an undertaking-cum-checklist (Annexure A), and listing eligibility requirements including NFSA ration enrollment (provisional, bilingual phrasing), plus an \"any of\" condition covering widow status, presence of a person with disability in the family, or AAY category.\n\nNow I'm noting exclusion conditions (elected representatives, professionals, government staff/pensioners, contractors/traders, large landholders, four-wheeler owners, or those covered by similar state schemes), with the \"four_wheeler\" exclusion flagged for its test id. I'm also provisionally listing supporting documents—Aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, self-declaration—with a note to confirm the current list, phrased bilingually in English and Assamese.\n\nFor name consistency checks, I'm considering how aadhaar and bank passbook names should match the ration card as a document-consistency rule rather than something sourced directly from the scheme page—this is more of a generic DBT practice, similar to how PM-KISAN verifies names against Aadhaar. I'm weighing whether to mark this check's status as provisional given the golden rule against definitive pass/fail, leaning toward treating a name mismatch as requiring action rather than an outright failure.\n\nI'm marking ration_card_valid as verified with fixWhere set to the supply office, phrasing the guidance generically as asking whether the ration card is active and valid rather than naming a specific applicant. Orunodoi doesn't need bank-related external checks, so I'll skip those, and now I'm moving to the Widow Pension scheme details—its bilingual name, and the verified age range of 40–79.\n\nFor Widow Pension I'm defining the verified requirements (is_widow, bpl_household) with their bilingual question text, setting the central benefit at 300 per month with no state top-up, and listing provisional mandatory documents like aadhaar and death certificate with their issuing authorities.\n\nI'm also noting that bank_passbook and bpl_proof need confirmation at the block office, there are no external checks like DBT seeding specified in the source docs, and I'm sourcing from the public services map for IGNWPS. Now I'm thinking through the schema design -- making sure question text ends properly, defining fixWhere values for exclusion versus unanswered cases, and sketching out the Check.kind enum and report schema structure with schemeId and scheme fields.\n\nI'm also working out the readiness logic: it should be true exactly when there's no non-external blocking check, while eligibility should only be computed from \"eligibility\" kind checks, not document completeness. I'm setting a low-confidence threshold around 0.8 so that fields like IFSC or account number trigger needs_verification when confidence is low, matching the 0.4 and 0.3 test cases.\n\nNow I'll start writing the types file.\n\n"}}
