{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:54:28.630Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"Now I need to design transliteration and name matching, working through Devanagari and Bengali/Assamese scripts against expected test cases like converting Hindi and Assamese names into their Romanized equivalents.\n\nI'm working out the schwa deletion rule: consonants carry an inherent 'a' sound that gets dropped at word-final position or before a virama, but surfaces as 'a' when followed by another consonant without a matra. I trace through each example word by word to confirm the pattern holds consistently across both scripts.\n\nStill mapping out consonant and vowel tables...\n\nContinuing the remaining consonants ৰ/র as R, ল as L, ৱ as W, and the sibilants শ/ষ/স as SH/SH/S, plus handling nukta forms ড়/ঢ় and the anusvara/visarga marks with NG/N/H conventions, while keeping non-letter characters like spaces passing through unchanged.\n\nI need to figure out the schwa deletion rule more carefully: if a consonant is followed by anusvara, visarga, or chandrabindu, the inherent 'a' should still be pronounced (like in \"sanjay\"), so I should treat those marks as not blocking the vowel. For nukta handling, I'm considering normalizing with NFC first, though some precomposed nukta forms like U+0958 are composition exclusions, so I need to decompose explicitly rather than rely on NFC alone.\n\nI realize NFC is actually the better normalization choice here, since it recomposes vowel signs like ো while still leaving composition-excluded nukta letters (য়, ড়, क़, etc.) split into base+nukta form — exactly the behavior I need for the transliteration map.\n\nNow I'm thinking through name normalization: transliterate to uppercase, then decide how to handle characters from scripts without a mapping. Stripping unknown letters entirely would be bad since a name in an untransliterated script would normalize to an empty string and always mismatch, so I should preserve unknown letters as-is and only replace non-letter/non-digit characters with spaces.\n\nI'm also working out honorific stripping against the test cases — things like \"Late Shri Ram-Prasad\" should collapse to \"RAM PRASAD\", \"MR. R.K. Sharma\" to \"R K SHARMA\", and \"Smt.\" alone to an empty string. I'm building a list of honorifics to repeatedly strip from the front: SHRI, SHREE, SRI, SMT, SHRIMATI, SRIMATI, MR, MRS, MS, MISS, KUMARI, KU, LATE, SUSHRI, and considering whether DR belongs in that set.\n\nRight, if both normalize to the same honorific set they should mismatch rather than match, so that case stays as-is. For nameMatch itself, I need to normalize both names first, treat empty results as mismatch, then check equality allowing for spacing differences, since joined versus spaced tokens (like transliterated names) should still count as the same when formatting differs but content matches.\n\nAfter dropping honorifics, I compare joined uppercase raw tokens with spaces ignored, so \"RAMESHKUMAR\" matches \"RAMESH KUMAR\" — same approach works across scripts since it's just token concatenation. Mixed-script comparisons that fail the raw check fall back to Latin transliteration equality, and I note this space-ignoring join could over-merge edge cases like \"RAM ESHKUMAR\" but that matches the documented behavior, so I'm moving on to exporting normalizeName.\n\nTesting normalizeName confirms it strips honorifics like \"Smt.\" correctly and joins remaining tokens with a single space. Now I'm second-guessing whether Jaro-Winkler's match-window algorithm is truly symmetric — greedy character matching from one string's perspective versus the other's might produce different match counts, and I'm not fully certain the standard implementation guarantees symmetry.\n\nTo be safe, I'll enforce symmetry by ordering inputs canonically before computing the score. I verify the classic MARTHA/MARHTA example gives Jaro 0.9444 and Jaro-Winkler 0.9611 as expected, check that completely different strings score 0 and identical strings score 1, and consider the edge case of two empty strings.\n\nMoving to transliteration handling: if the Latin-joined forms of two tokens are equal (as with Devanagari transliterated to Latin), I'd treat that as a possible match with a JW score of 1, even though the scoring nuance there is less important. Next I'm thinking through canonicalizing tokens using a variant map, like mapping \"KR\" to \"KUMA\" for common abbreviations.\n\nFor spelling variants like MOHD/MUHAMMAD mapping to MOHAMMAD, DEBI to DEVI, and SK/SHAIKH to SHEIKH, I want canonicalized joined tokens to count as a possible match too — checking that \"SUNITA DEBI\" canonicalizes to match \"SUNITA DEVI\" works correctly. I'm also starting to consider how initials like \"R. Kumar\" should match against full first names like \"Ramesh Kumar\".\n\nI should canonicalize tokens first before comparing initials so variant spellings don't interfere. For reordering, I'll sort canonical tokens and compare equality for a possible_match, and otherwise fall back to Jaro-Winkler on the joined canonical names with a threshold around 0.85 for possible_match, while making sure genuinely mismatched names like \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs similar-looking names don't get incorrectly matched.\n\nI'm tracing through the character-by-character alignment to count matches and find transpositions, getting 15 matches with a few positions out of order (like the space and A landing in different relative spots), which I'll need to compare against the original ordering to tally transpositions for the Jaro score.\n\nWith JW+prefix bonus this pushes the score to 0.892, which crosses the 0.85 threshold as a possible match—but that contradicts what the test expects, so whole-string JW clearly isn't sufficient here. I need a token-level approach instead: split names into tokens and require the token counts to align, so something like \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" having an extra unmatched token against \"Rameshwar Sharma\" correctly forces a mismatch, while individual token comparisons like \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" still get evaluated via JW similarity.\n\nChecking \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\": KR canonicalizes to KUMAR, so equal → possible_match. For token-level comparisons with equal counts, I align positionally and require each pair to meet the 0.85 JW threshold; reordering is handled separately through sorted equality checks, and space-removed joins like \"RAMESHKUMAR SHARMA\" versus split variants are covered by the same-string comparison after stripping spaces, though edge cases like \"RAMESHKUMAR\" vs \"RAMESH KR\" need more thought since canonicalization happens before space removal.\n\nI also need to think about missing tokens — \"Sunita Devi\" vs \"Sunita\" is clearly a mismatch, but \"Ramesh Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" (a missing middle name) is trickier since it's common in real-world records. Under a defensive token-count-based approach this would flag as mismatch requiring exact match, which seems reasonably safe even if less user-friendly, since a missing middle name isn't really an abbreviation case like initials would be.\n\nNow I'm working through what score to actually report for transparency — using Jaro-Winkler on canonical joined strings works fine for equal-length matches, but produces confusingly high scores for mismatches with differing token counts. I'm considering ways to penalize token-count mismatches, like scaling the JW score by the ratio of token counts, though this feels like a hacky workaround rather than a clean solution.\n\nFor possible_match with equal counts, I'm requiring every positional pair to hit JW ≥ 0.85 (minimum, not average) as a defensive threshold, which should still work fine for transliteration variants like \"RAJAKUMAR\" vs \"RAJKUMAR\". For initials matching, I'm requiring equal counts where each position is either canonically equal or one side is a single letter matching the other's starting letter, with non-initial positions needing exact canonical equality rather than just JW similarity — otherwise something like \"R. Kumaar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" could falsely qualify.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to loosen that and allow non-initial positions to also pass via JW ≥ 0.85 instead of requiring exact equality, since both outcomes end up as needs_verification anyway. All these comparisons are symmetric by construction since they rely on sorted tokens and JW distance is itself symmetric, so I don't need to worry about ordering issues. I'm also working through how raw-equality checks should work when transliteration matches but the raw strings differ, normalizing by removing spaces and uppercasing before comparing.\n\nFor pure Latin tokens, matching transliteration should be sufficient since they're identical to their own transliteration. I'm double-checking edge cases: diacritics like \"Ramésh\" survive the letter-extraction regex, and honorific tokens dropped before transliteration are also dropped from raw comparison, so the two stay consistent. I'm also verifying that a name compared against itself always returns a perfect match score as long as normalization doesn't produce an empty string.\n\nThe token-splitting regex covers Devanagari marks and digits correctly, so splitting on punctuation like danda should work fine, and the uppercase edge case for German ß is irrelevant here. I'm confirming symmetry holds for the \"same\" comparison since it's just comparing joined canonical strings, and I want the score computed uniformly from tokenScore on canonical latin tokens so equal tokens always yield 1.\n\nNow I'm building out the variant mapping table for common name spellings — grouping KR/KMR variants under KUMAR, MD/MOHD/MOHAMMED/etc under MOHAMMAD, DEBI under DEVI, SK/SHAIKH/SHEK/SHAIK/SHEKH under SHEIKH, and considering regional transliteration variants like Bengali/Assamese spelling differences.\n\nI'm also testing whether initials-based names like \"SK Abdul\" vs \"Sheikh Abdul\" and \"Md Rafiqul Islam\" vs \"Mohammad Rafiqul Islam\" correctly resolve as possible matches after canonicalization, while making sure single letters stay distinct from multi-letter initials like KR or KMR. Now checking whether \"R K Sharma\" matches against a fuller expanded name like \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma.\"\n\nI'm laying out the full matching priority order: exact empty check, raw string equality, canonical string equality, sorted canonical token equality, then falling into initials-based comparison and equal-count token matching as fallback tiers.\n\nFor the equal-count tier, I'm using per-token Jaro-Winkler similarity with a 0.85 threshold to call it a possible match, which is stricter than a pure normalized-form comparison but safer against false positives — I'll note that choice in the log. I'm also sanity-checking edge cases, like comparing a single initial against a full name, confirming the initials-detection step handles that before the JW threshold ever gets evaluated, and now I'm working out the token scoring function itself: when token counts match it's the mean of positional JW scores, otherwise it falls back to a greedy pairing approach.\n\nI'll clamp the mean to avoid floating-point overshoot past 1, and round the final score to three decimals for display purposes. Now I'm sketching the evaluate function signature, thinking through date validation and how the scheme rules get parsed before scoring.\n\nFor document checks, I'm defining the logic: each mandatory document produces a check result keyed by doc type, marking it as satisfied if present with a confirming message, or flagging it as needing action with guidance on where to obtain it, pulling the fix location and provenance from the requirement definition, and noting when a document is only provisionally required.\n\nFor missing documents under provisional rules, like a widow's death certificate, the spec is explicit that absence should still trigger action_required rather than a silent pass, so I'm following that even though provisional rules generally shouldn't produce definitive pass/fail eligibility verdicts — readiness status isn't the same as an eligibility failure.\n\nNow I'm working through name consistency checks, determining the reference document per scheme (aadhaar for PM-KISAN, ration card for Orunodoi) and comparing names across the other provided documents like bank passbook against that reference.\n\nFor cases where the reference document itself is missing, I'm deciding whether to mark the comparison not_applicable or fall back to comparing among whatever documents are actually present — leaning toward a simple fallback where if the primary reference is absent, I pick another available document as the comparison baseline instead of skipping the check entirely.\n\nFor the actual name comparison logic, I'm working out how to match \"name\" as a single string versus \"ownerNames\" as a list, scoring each candidate against the reference using tiers like exact match, possible match, initials-only, or mismatch, and picking the best-ranked owner when there are ties by score. I also need to flag cases where a document is present but the name couldn't be extracted, or where OCR confidence is too low to trust the match.\n\nFor the final decision mapping: same name → ok; possible_match, initials_only → needs_verification; mismatch → action_required unless the user has indicated the land is in a family member's name, in which case it drops to needs_verification with guidance to bring proof of relationship. Low confidence on either side pushes everything to needs_verification since we can't trust the reading, even in mismatch cases.\n\nThen for field-level checks, I'm handling missing documents as not_applicable, unreadable or low-confidence values as needs_verification, and normalized values that don't match the expected pattern (like IFSC codes) as action_required, with everything else passing as ok.\n\nNext, for age eligibility: I derive DOB from the ageRange field, treating missing docs, low-confidence DOBs, invalid date strings, or DOBs after the as-of date all as needs_verification. If age falls in range it's ok; out of range depends on provenance -- verified data becomes action_required with not_eligible status, while provisional data stays as needs_verification, and I append a hint if the age exceeds the maximum.\n\nFor fixWhere on age, ok and out-of-range cases get \"none,\" while missing data points the applicant to add the document rather than visit the Aadhaar centre.\n\nFor requirements, each yes answer is ok, verified no becomes action_required with eligibility failure, provisional no becomes needs_verification with a caveat about possible ineligibility, and unanswered items prompt the user to answer. I'm mapping specific requirement IDs like is_widow, bpl_household, and nfsa_enrolled to their verified/provisional status, then moving on to handle the AnyOf logic.\n\nFor a single anyOf check, any yes answer passes it; if all are answered no it fails (verified→not_eligible, provisional→needs_verification), and if none are yes but some are unanswered it needs verification. I'm mapping out the Orunodoi scheme's anyOf criteria (widow, PwD, AAY, income under ₹2 lakh) against the test case where only widow is set, confirming the needs_verification outcome works correctly, and now I'm wondering whether I need a superRefine check to enforce unique question IDs across requirements, anyOf, exclusions, and the family owner question within a scheme.\n\nI'm deciding to add that superRefine for uniqueness since in zod 4 refinements don't wrap the object type, so SchemeRule stays usable as before. For exclusions, I'm handling the logic where answering \"no\" clears the exclusion, answering \"yes\" triggers ineligibility (or needs_verification for provisional schemes with a message about confirming at the Gram Panchayat), and leaving it unanswered also needs verification — checking this against the PM-KISAN income_tax exclusion test case.\n\nFor fixWhere, I'm falling back to the scheme's confirmAt value when the question doesn't specify its own, so something like orunodoi's four_wheeler exclusion correctly resolves to \"gram_panchayat\". Now moving to external checks, which get id'd as `ext_${id}`, kind \"external\", and always apply.\n\nFor verdicts: eligibility becomes not_eligible only if an eligibility-kind check is action_required with verified provenance, needs_verification if any eligibility check needs verification, otherwise likely_eligible — and I note Orunodoi should never hit not_eligible since all its criteria are provisional. Readiness stays separate from eligibility and depends on document-based checks: action_required takes priority, then needs_verification, otherwise ready.\n\nI'm checking test cases against this logic — PM-KISAN with clean docs should hit ready_for_review even if an external check needs verification, since external checks are excluded from readiness; income-tax exclusion failures should push readiness to action_required; and the widow case at age 39 correctly triggers action_required. For the widow at age 40 with all documents present, I'm verifying the name match between bank passbook and Aadhaar, and wondering whether bpl_proof also needs a name consistency check per the stated rule, which currently only mentions aadhaar and bank_passbook.\n\nI'm deciding against adding IFSC/account format checks for the widow pathway since the doc scopes field checks to PM-KISAN only, even though they'd arguably be useful across all DBT schemes — staying within spec. For widow external checks, the doc doesn't list any beyond is_widow and bpl_household verification, and I'm confirming the test's IFSC value is valid regardless. I'm also deciding to skip any husband-name cross-check against the death certificate since it isn't specified, and noting there's no listed external check like BPL list status for the widow case.\n\nFor documents, bpl_proof is mandatory with provisional provenance per the doc's listing, and I've built the widow test docs and sample C accordingly — sample C includes bpl_proof but is missing the death certificate.\n\nFor Orunodoi, tracing through: ration card and name checks pass, nfsa_enrolled requirement passes, exclusions all clear, but the external ration card validity check should resolve to needs_verification rather than a clean pass, since golden rule 2 says provisional rules never yield a definitive pass — so readiness should reflect that, not a straightforward ready_for_review.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether a \"no\" answer to a provisional exclusion should count as \"ok\" or should instead be flagged as needs_verification, since treating it as \"triggered only\" would let Orunodoi reach likely_eligible while still carrying a note that the rule is provisional and the official list may differ.\n\nNow I'm working out the report structure—benefit status by date, supporting docs with presence flags and bilingual labels, and the disclaimer text. I need to decide where document-type and fix-where labels live, since the brief wants all fixed UI strings centralized in one i18n file, but I'm weighing whether the core logic (which must stay pure) can safely import from that file or needs its own label set.\n\nCore check messages should live separately in a pure module like `src/core/messages.ts`, with T2's i18n file reusing them rather than duplicating. I'm also drafting Assamese translations for document labels like Aadhaar card, bank passbook, and land records, making sure to use the correct Assamese characters, and flagging these for native-speaker review as the brief requires.\n\nStill listing document types and fix-location options in both English and Assamese...\n\nFilling in the remaining term mappings for \"applicant\" and \"none,\" then moving to the message templates and rejection-code fix steps. Now I'm detailing PM-KISAN's provenance data—source URL, verification date, version—plus specifying that confirmation should happen at the block office and that Aadhaar serves as the name reference.\n\nFor name checks, I'm defining bank passbook and land record mismatches, noting fixes go through the bank or circle office, with a special case where Aadhaar centre updates might resolve bank name mismatches too. I'm adding field checks for IFSC and account number patterns, exclusion question texts, and external verification steps like Aadhaar-bank seeding, account activity, eKYC via portal OTP or CSC biometric, and land verification at the circle office, then starting on the benefit's central share amount.\n\nI'm leaving the effective date null since the source doesn't specify it, and I'm careful with the landholding note to avoid any wording that would trigger my regex checks for \"hectare\" or \"landholding_size\" — so I'll phrase it as removal of the earlier size limit in 2019 without using forbidden terms.\n\nNow for Orunodoi: I'm citing the official Dhemaji district scheme page as primary provenance, noting it was last updated 8 May 2025 (though that's not the effective date), and adding the Public Services Map page as a secondary, provisional source. I'm setting confirmation at the gram panchayat level and recording the benefit structure with zero central share, a state top-up of 1250 per month, sourced from the official version.\n\nI'm listing ration card as the mandatory document, verifiable at the supply office, with aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, and self-declaration as commonly requested supporting documents that should be confirmed locally since they're not firmly sourced. For name consistency checks, I'm cross-referencing against the ration card, aadhaar, and bank passbook, though I'm uncertain how to attribute provenance since this isn't an explicit scheme rule but a general DBT verification practice — I'm leaning toward citing it as provisional via the secondary source.\n\nFor PM-KISAN, I'll note the Rajasthan name-mismatch issue referenced in research.md and treat the Aadhaar-name-matching requirement as verified via the portal's \"Edit Aadhaar failure records\" feature. For NFSA enrollment, I'm marking it provisional, and compiling the eligible-category list (widow, PwD, AAY household, income under ₹2 lakh) as a provisional anyOf condition.\n\nI'll trim externalChecks to just ration_card_valid, and add notes clarifying no income certificate is required and that the Oct 2026 ₹1,500 figure was a one-time payment, not the recurring amount.\n\nFor the widow pension scheme, I'm sourcing IGNWPS details from the Public Services Map (aligned with MoRD), setting confirmAt to block_office since NSAP is handled there, and noting the benefit as a ₹300 central share per month with no state top-up, applicable to ages 40–79 with DOB verified via Aadhaar.\n\nNow I'm adding an aboveMax hint pointing people 80+ toward the Old Age Pension instead, along with verified requirements for widow status and BPL household, provisional mandatory documents (Aadhaar, death certificate, bank passbook, BPL proof) each tied to their issuing authority, and a note that these documents are commonly required but should be confirmed locally, plus setting Aadhaar as the name reference with bank passbook as a proven name check.\n\nName mismatches should trigger action_required per the documentation, even for provisional rules, so I'm keeping \"is_widow\" as a required field tied to the widow eligibility question. I'm also planning to include the three sample sets (A, B, C) with their scheme IDs, docs, and answers in English/Assamese, and setting up the scheme registry as a map from scheme ID to its rules.\n\nI'm working through registry functions — fetching the latest or specific version via semver comparison, listing all schemes at their latest version, registering new rule versions with validation against duplicates, and gathering all questions across requirements, anyOf conditions, exclusions, and family-owner questions for UI display. I'm also noting that since the registry tests mutate global state by registering new versions, I need to rely on Vitest's default test isolation to prevent cross-test contamination, though some tests still explicitly pin older versions.\n\nFor safety, I'm deep-freezing registered rules after parsing so callers can't mutate stored objects, while leaving room for tests to spread/copy new objects fine. Each scheme file will export its raw data parsed through the SchemeRule schema, and I need to define a semver comparison helper plus the rejection codes structure.\n\nNow I'm mapping each rejection code to its fix location — bank for Aadhaar/account issues, circle_office for land verification, pm_kisan_portal for eKYC, and block_office for pending state actions or unknown codes.\n\nI'm updating the app config with Assamese name \"কাগজ চেক\", tagline \"Check before you apply\", and setting the default language to \"as\", while noting the Geist font will fall back to system fonts for Bengali glyphs since it lacks that coverage. I also need to refresh the memory bank files — productContext, systemPatterns, and activeContext — before moving on to write the transliteration module.\n\n"}}
