{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:56:17.624Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm planning to split the nameMatch logic into two parts, the first running through jaroWinkler and the second handling the rest, while working through test cases like name order variations, casing differences, and spelling variants such as \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" vs \"SUNITA DEBI\".\n\nI'm checking honorific stripping and canonicalization cases: \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" should match canonically, \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" should match via initials logic, and \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" should mismatch due to length differences. I'm also manually computing the Jaro-Winkler similarity for \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" to confirm it falls below the 0.85 threshold and correctly registers as a mismatch. Now I'm moving into verifying transliteration handling for Devanagari script names like \"रमेश कुमार शर्मा\".\n\nBengali ৰমেশ resolves to RAMESH ✓, সুনীতা দেবী gives SUNITA DEBI ✓, and শ্ৰীমতী works out to SHRIMATI ✓. I'm also double-checking NFC normalization concerns: Bengali ো composes fine under NFC, but Devanagari nukta letters like ज़ are composition exclusions, so NFC would decompose them instead.\n\nChecking Bengali য় and ড় too, both excluded and decomposed properly, with ৰ staying unchanged under NFC.\n\nNow testing cross-script matches: Devanagari \"रमेश कुमार शर्मा\" against Latin \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" — joined forms differ at the raw level but match once canonicalized, giving a possible_match. Same pattern holds for \"সুনীতা দেবী\" against \"Sunita Devi\", since SUNITA DEBI equals SUNITA DEVI canonically. I'm now checking whether \"ৰমেশ শৰ্মা\" compared against a version with extra whitespace, \"ৰমেশ  শৰ্মা\", still matches at the raw level.\n\nNext I'm verifying title/honorific abbreviations: MD expands to MOHAMMAD, and SK expands to SHEIKH, both producing matches. Word-order permutations like \"SHARMA RAMESH KUMAR\" against \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" also match once sorted. For \"R K Sharma\" against \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\", I'm confirming the initials R/RAMESH and K/KUMAR align correctly with SHARMA matching exactly, giving an initials_only match, while making sure single-letter initials like K don't accidentally collide with any variant table entries like CH→CHANDRA. Then I'm setting up the empty-string edge case.\n\nNow I'm checking honorific stripping: \"Smt.\" alone normalizes to empty and scores zero mismatch, same with Devanagari titles like श्रीमती being dropped properly. I verify cases like \"Late Shri Ram-Prasad\" reducing to RAM PRASAD, \"MR. R.K. Sharma\" reducing to R K SHARMA, and plain names like \"Sunita Kumari\" passing through unchanged. Then I confirm the reflexive property that any non-empty normalized name matches itself with a perfect score, and move on to checking symmetry of the match function.\n\nSymmetry holds because the word-score function's pairwise Jaro-Winkler comparisons are themselves symmetric, and the mean over equal-length token sequences or over greedy shorter/longer pairings doesn't depend on argument order — the shorter list is determined purely by length, not which side is \"first.\" Floating-point sums over identical value sequences also come out identical regardless of direction, so the in-order mapping of token comparisons produces the same results either way.\n\nThe greedy tie-break logic defaults safely when all scores are zero since best stays unset. Across every matching level — joined, sorted, initials-only, and the full per-token Jaro-Winkler pass — symmetry is preserved because I explicitly order the string pair before computing the common prefix, which cancels out the inherent asymmetry Jaro's greedy character matching could otherwise introduce.\n\nI'm checking that jaroWinkler stays bounded in [0,1] via the min clamp, and verifying known reference cases like MARTHA/MARHTA (0.961) and ABC/XYZ (0) hold after the ordering fix. I also want to confirm wordScore stays within [0,1], and consider edge cases from property-based string generators where tokenized inputs might transliterate down to empty strings.\n\nChecking edge cases with combining marks and honorific stripping for Devanagari names like \"श्री रमेश\" versus \"रमेश\" — the honorific detection via the Latin \"SHRI\" pattern correctly drops it so raw comparison still matches. I'm also confirming that joined versus spaced word forms, like \"RAMESH KUMAR\" vs \"RAMESHKUMAR\", resolve to the same match, which is acceptable given the spec.\n\nFor readiness evaluation, I need to treat it as the worst outcome across all non-external checks: missing documents or name mismatches push it to action_required, unmet verified criteria also trigger action_required per the scheme rules, while provisional or unanswered items should instead map to needs_verification.\n\nExternal checks like bank seeding are an exception — clean results still allow ready_for_review overall even though that specific check reports needs_verification on its own. I'll encode that distinction in the tests.\n\nFor eligibility specifically, a failed verified criterion means not_eligible, while a provisional trigger, unanswered question, or unknown age maps to needs_verification, with everything else landing on likely_eligible. These eligibility checks (age, requirements, anyof, exclusions) also feed into the overall readiness calculation.\n\nChecking through the widow pension cases: age 39 fails the age check and comes out not_eligible with action_required readiness, while age 40 needs all docs present (aadhaar, bank_passbook, bpl_proof, death_certificate) plus matching names between aadhaar and bank_passbook, and valid IFSC/account number formats, to reach ready_for_review.\n\nI'm deciding field checks like IFSC format should only apply to PM-KISAN since the docs explicitly mention it there, not universally across schemes. For widow pension, I'm not adding any external checks since docs don't list any, but for Orunodoi I'm including a ration card validity check since that aligns with the rule that document photos can't prove external facts but official verification can confirm validity.\n\nFor widow requirements, both is_widow and bpl_household need verification since the death certificate alone doesn't confirm them, and unanswered questions should trigger needs_verification status. I'm also setting a low-confidence threshold of 0.8 for DOB extracted from Aadhaar, below which age-related fields need verification, while missing confidence entries default to confirmed. The age-80 case should trigger the above-max hint pointing to Old Age Pension, and widow benefit totals remain null as expected.\n\nFor Orunodoi, nfsa_enrolled is provisional, and the anyOf logic (widow/pwd/aay/income_below_2_lakh) needs to handle cases where only one condition is true and others are unanswered — resulting in needs_verification unless all are explicitly \"no,\" which would trigger not_eligible. I'm also mapping exclusion conditions using an `excl_` prefix (like excl_four_wheeler) and requirement fields using a `req_` prefix to keep the id naming consistent.\n\nFor PM-KISAN I'm checking excl_income_tax_payer and excl_govt_employee as exclusion ids. For Orunodoi, I'm noting it should never produce not_eligible since all its criteria are provisional and the ration card check is really a document readiness check, not an eligibility determination — same logic applies to the broader eligibility rule, which only triggers not_eligible from verified criteria. I'm also working out the name consistency checks across ration_card, aadhaar, and bank_passbook, using ids like name_aadhaar and name_bank_passbook referenced against ration_card, with the expectation that these become not_applicable if those documents weren't provided.\n\nFor PM-KISAN, if a mandatory document is missing, the corresponding name check is also not_applicable since the doc check already flags it, and the same applies if the reference document itself is missing — the message should note that comparison isn't possible. I'm confirming Orunodoi's \"no ration card\" case correctly yields action_required readiness while leaving name checks not_applicable when the reference is missing, and I'm checking the provisional exclusion messaging points users to confirm at the Gram Panchayat with the appropriate fixWhere. I'm also listing Orunodoi's supporting docs: aadhaar, bank_passbook, voter_id, age_proof, and address_proof.\n\nNow I'm adding self_declaration as a provisional supporting doc with a present flag, and writing the supportingDocsNoteEn pointing users to confirm the current list at the Gram Panchayat. For the widow scheme, I'm treating the mandatory docs list as provisional provenance with a note that it's commonly required and should be confirmed locally, and ensuring a missing provisional mandatory doc still triggers action_required in the doc check message.\n\nFor PM-KISAN, I'm setting mandatory docs to aadhaar, bank_passbook, and land_record as verified, and building name checks for bank_passbook and land_record that specify where to fix a mismatch — bank for the passbook, circle office for the land record, with a familyOwnerQuestion flag for land held in a family member's name. I'm naming each check `name_${docType}` so the logic can point users to exactly which document to correct and where to go.\n\nThe family-owner question only matters when there's a mismatch, so it staying unanswered otherwise is fine, though the UI might ask it regardless—that's a secondary concern.\n\nFor field validation, I'm checking IFSC and account number patterns against their field IDs, treating missing values as needs_verification since they could stem from unreadable photos, while missing documents entirely map to not_applicable and low-confidence fields also trigger needs_verification. I'm normalizing values by stripping spaces and hyphens before validation.\n\nFor external checks, each one always resolves to needs_verification with a specific fixWhere location—bank for aadhaar seeding and account status, pm_kisan_portal for eKYC, and circle_office for land verification, matching the expected rejection codes. Name comparisons follow the same pattern: if either the reference or compared name field has confidence below 0.8, I flag it as needs_verification even though the match result is still reported, consistent with the test case where a 0.4 confidence bank name triggers this behavior.\n\nFor overall readiness, I'm computing it as the worst outcome among non-external checks, prioritizing action_required over needs_verification over ok/not_applicable, so that \"ready_for_review\" is derived exactly from this hierarchy. For eligibility, I'm treating any eligibility-kind check that resolves to action_required as a verified failure (since provisional checks never produce action_required), which lets me determine not_eligible status directly from that outcome.\n\nI'm also checking whether PM-KISAN needs an explicit \"owns_cultivable_land\" question — since the land record check already covers that requirement and the test case doesn't ask about it, I'll leave it out and rely on the land record presence/owner check to establish eligibility.\n\nFor the report test, I need every check's Assamese message to include a truthy fixWhere field (treating \"none\" as a valid string), with provenance timestamps matching the expected date. I also need to make sure the PM-KISAN pack avoids the word \"hectare\" anywhere, since the large-landholding test checks that land area isn't used at all — so instead of referencing the removed 2-hectare limit explicitly, I should phrase it as \"regardless of how much land you own\" to sidestep that forbidden term.\n\nI'm also checking that the Aadhaar/account number regex doesn't accidentally match the 11-digit account number, and deciding not to echo sensitive values like account numbers in messages while still allowing names and IFSC codes to appear since this is the user's own report. I need to confirm the asOfDate validation throws a RangeError for invalid dates, and think through whether the evaluate function should also validate the incoming SchemeRule type.\n\nFor extracted documents, I'm deciding to skip an extra zod validation pass for performance since property tests cover this adequately, but I'll handle invalid DOB strings by falling back to needs_verification. When multiple documents of the same type exist, I'll just use the first one, and I'm drafting a disclaimer clarifying this tool catches mistakes but doesn't guarantee approval since the department makes final decisions. For the age check, I'm working out all the branches — missing doc, missing or invalid DOB, low confidence, or DOB after the as-of date all route to needs_verification with a message asking the user to confirm their date of birth, and I'm starting to handle the case where age falls below the minimum.\n\nAge above max also triggers the same needs_verification path with an additional hint. I'm walking through test cases like a 79-year-old widow checked against specific DOB and as-of dates to confirm the 79 and 80 boundaries work correctly. For requirement questions, true answers pass through as ok, false answers split into verified action_required or needs_verification depending on confidence, and undefined answers prompt the user to answer; I'm also deciding fixWhere should default to the scheme's confirmAt location when the question doesn't specify one. For exclusions, answering yes triggers a verified action_required result explaining the scheme excludes people in that category.\n\nFor anyOf checks, I need any true answer to resolve as ok, any undefined to trigger needs_verification, and all false answers to produce either action_required or needs_verification depending on verification status, with provenance pointing to the first provisional or otherwise the first question in the list. I'm confirming the possible_match wording matches the required test phrase about officials possibly rejecting mismatched names. Now I'm thinking about centralizing document and fixWhere labels for English and Assamese into a shared labels file.\n\nFor UI-facing messages I'll keep `src/i18n.ts` as the T2 layer, while pure core labels go into `src/core/labels.ts`, noting in the log that the UI i18n file should merge or re-export from core. For Assamese text I'll use proper characters like ৰ and ৱ, flagging in the log that these strings should eventually be reviewed by a native speaker.\n\nNow I'm drafting the document type labels in both English and Assamese — covering Aadhaar card, bank passbook, land record (jamabandi), ration card, husband's death certificate, BPL proof, and voter ID card.\n\nI'm continuing the list of locations with the Gram Panchayat, block office, CSC, PM-KISAN portal, food & civil supplies office, and birth & death registrar in both English and Assamese.\n\nNow I'm working through the rejection codes and mapping each one to the entity responsible for fixing it, starting with bank-related issues like name mismatch with Aadhaar, Aadhaar not seeded, invalid IFSC, and inactive accounts—all of which point to the bank as the place to resolve them. I'm noting Assamese script conventions, particularly the use of ৰ for 'r' and ৱ for 'w', flagging this for native speaker review to ensure accuracy.\n\nThen I move to land verification issues routed to the circle office, eKYC pending cases directed to the PM-Kisan portal, pending state action handled at the block office, and unknown issues also defaulting to block office. I'm drafting sample cases—one with bank name \"RAMESH KR SHARMA\" and land owner \"Rameshwar Sharma\" for confirmation, and another involving a widow missing a death certificate.\n\nI'm also sketching out the scheme registry functions: retrieving a scheme by ID and optional version, listing the latest of each scheme, listing all versions, registering new scheme rules with validation and duplicate-version checks using semver comparison, and generating the questions needed based on requirements, alternative conditions, exclusions, and family ownership.\n\nI'm checking that registering schemes in tests mutates a global registry safely since vitest isolates modules per file by default, and confirming that an explicit version lookup for nsap-widow still matches correctly. I note that registerScheme correctly throws on invalid IDs via zod validation, and I'm planning to write scheme files compactly—possibly factoring out a provenance helper and a question builder to keep the larger PM-KISAN rule pack manageable. For the benefit override test, I'm confirming that a null sourceUrl with provisional status parses correctly, and that an override with an end date before the start date gets rejected.\n\nI'm also verifying that overriding instalments to 3 for pm-kisan matches the expected object shape, and checking the aadhaar property test's regex only matches ASCII digits—meaning maskAadhaar's non-digit stripping might behave unexpectedly with non-ASCII digit characters like Arabic-Indic numerals, since those aren't matched by \\D either.\n\nFor the age property test, I'm confirming that fast-check's noInvalidDate option is available in version 4 and that ISO string generation works correctly across the 1900-2100 range. Now I'm moving to write nameMatch in two separate parts.\n\n"}}
