{"ts":"2026-10-10T08:56:40.366Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm checking symmetry of the scoring function: for different-length words, the short/long split stays the same regardless of argument order, so the greedy pairing produces identical results either way. For equal-length words, the in-order mean and sorted comparisons are also symmetric since they just pair up corresponding or sorted elements.\n\nI'm also verifying self-consistency—comparing a name to itself should always yield a match, and I'm walking through edge cases like stray tokens with empty latin transliterations getting filtered out. Testing examples like \"Smt. Sunita Devi\" vs \"SUNITA DEBI\" and \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" to confirm the canonical-join and initials-only logic behaves as expected across script and abbreviation variations.\n\nNow checking \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" correctly mismatches due to differing token counts, then working through the Jaro-Winkler calculation by hand for \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" to confirm the matching window and character alignment produce the expected similarity score.\n\nTransitioning to initials matching logic: \"R K Sharma\" against \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" resolves as initials_only, and similar patterns like \"SK Abdul\" vs \"Sheikh Abdul\" and \"Md Rafiqul Islam\" vs \"Mohammad Rafiqul Islam\" check out as expected matches. Now I'm considering transliteration cases with Devanagari script names.\n\nTracing through the syllable-by-syllable conversion of \"रमेश कुमार शर्मा\" to \"RAMESH KUMAR SHARMA\" and \"सुनीता देवी\" to \"SUNITA DEVI,\" confirming these raw-to-canonical mappings produce possible_match results despite differing from the Latin spellings. Starting to work through \"श्री\" as another transliteration test case.\n\nThe nukta exclusion handles ড় and similar decomposed consonants correctly, while composite vowels like ো stay intact under NFC since they're not excluded. Testing transliterate confirms spaces and case work as expected, and normalizeName correctly strips honorifics like \"Late Shri\" and abbreviates titles like \"Mr. R.K.\"\n\nI check that reordered names match via sorting, empty inputs score zero, and Jaro-Winkler gives expected values for MARTHA/MARHTA and completely different strings. Now I'm verifying the identity property holds for arbitrary strings using the Unicode-aware tokenizer pattern matching letters, marks, and numbers.\n\n"}}
