{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:00:36.033Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm checking that case normalization works correctly for matching names, including uppercase variants and Devanagari script tokens, and considering how honorifics might affect equality comparisons.\n\nTesting initials matching for cases like \"R. Kumar\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar\" where token counts differ but initials align, and verifying that names with different token counts like \"Rameshwar Sharma\" vs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" correctly mismatch.\n\nNow I'm working through the Jaro similarity calculation by hand for \"Ramesh\" vs \"Suresh\" to confirm it falls below the threshold, tracing character matches within the window and computing the final score.\n\nI'm checking that abbreviation expansion like \"KR\" to \"Kumar\" works correctly in the canonical join logic, then confirming the overall matching functions are symmetric with respect to argument order — checking exact match, canonical join, initials-only, and word-score comparisons all behave consistently whether inputs are swapped, including how ties are resolved deterministically in the greedy matching for different-length name sets.\n\nNow I'm considering edge cases around Unicode normalization — stray combining marks that transliterate to nothing, tokens with digits, special uppercase conversions like the Turkish İ, and confirming self-matching a string against itself always resolves correctly even when normalization produces empty strings.\n\nI'm also tracing through transliteration of honorifics like \"श्री\" and \"শ্রীমতী\" in Devanagari and Assamese scripts, verifying consonant clusters and inherent vowels produce the expected Latin outputs like \"SHRI\", \"SHRIMATI\", and \"RAMESH\".\n\nChecking more examples like \"सुनीता देवी\" and \"रमेश कुमार शर्मा\" to confirm consistent vowel-insertion rules, then I pause to note a subtlety around NFC normalization and nukta characters like \"ज़\", since these composition exclusions could affect how transliteration handles certain consonant-diacritic combinations.\n\nvs \"Ramesh Kumar Sharma\" sorted matches as possible. \"R K Sharma\" vs full name reduces to initials comparison, flagged initials_only.\n\nTesting title stripping: \"MR. R.K. Sharma\" strips to \"R K SHARMA\", \"Late Shri Ram-Prasad\" strips to \"RAM PRASAD\", \"Sunita Kumari\" stays as-is, and \"Smt.\" alone strips to empty.\n\nEdge cases bug me: reversed order like \"Kumari Sunita\" vs \"Sunita Kumari\" could mismatch under the raw-token \"same\" check since honorific stripping only affects one side, and names with diacritics like \"José\" won't equal \"JOSE\" directly but could still match via Jaro-Winkler. I'm now verifying the Jaro-Winkler calculation for MARTHA vs MARHTA comes out to roughly 0.944.\n\nI'm confirming the Jaro-Winkler function is symmetric since I sort inputs, and surrogate pairs should index consistently either way. Now I'm drafting Assamese-language labels for document types like Aadhaar card, bank passbook, and land record, noting these translations need native review since my Assamese is rough.\n\nAdding the \"other\" document option, then listing FixWhere locations like bank branches, Aadhaar centres, circle offices, Gram Panchayat, and block offices with their Assamese translations.\n\nI need a labels file with bilingual document names, fix-locations, and disclaimers, then an evaluate function that checks mandatory documents against the scheme requirements — marking each as provided or flagging it as missing with guidance on where to obtain it, followed by name-matching checks against a reference document.\n\nFor the name comparisons, I'm handling edge cases where a document is missing or the reference name is unavailable (skipping or flagging for verification), then computing name match confidence for single names or lists of owner names, treating low-confidence OCR reads as needing manual verification.\n\nFor the match outcomes: an exact match passes unless confidence is low, while possible matches and initials-only cases always need verification. For mismatches, I'm deciding that if the user already confirmed a family-owner question I flag it for verification, otherwise it's action-required — and I'm settling on always treating low-confidence mismatches as needing verification rather than action-required, since the reading itself might be wrong and the UI resolves confidence issues through confirmation first.\n\nNow I'm checking which fields actually get compared against which reference document per scheme — Orunodoi compares ration_card against aadhaar and bank_passbook, PM-KISAN compares aadhaar against bank_passbook and land_record, and widow pension compares aadhaar against bank_passbook. I'm working through what happens for Orunodoi if the ration card itself is missing, since that would break the whole comparison chain and I need to decide how to handle that gap.\n\nI'm also setting a low-confidence threshold constant at 0.8, and thinking through the PM-KISAN land record case where the land might be registered under a family member's name instead of the applicant's — I'd added a specific question flag for that scenario, like \"land_in_family_member_name\", asking whether the land is recorded under a husband, wife, or minor child.\n\nIf there's a mismatch there, I want to surface a needs_verification note about officials possibly requesting proof of relationship, using verified PM-KISAN provenance since the family definition is already confirmed. I'm also checking that the test suite requirements hold — every question needs both English and Assamese text, with English questions ending in \"?\", and provenance needs to be tracked across requirements, exclusions, and family owner questions.\n\nNow I'm moving to build out field checks per scheme, iterating through each field check to construct an id and resolve the matching document.\n\nFor each field check, I'm handling missing documents, unreadable values, and normalizing extracted text for comparison — flagging low-confidence matches as needing verification and failed matches as action_required, since those could stem from OCR misreads. Then I'm setting up the age eligibility check, pulling date of birth from the relevant document and flagging cases where it's missing or not in proper ISO format as needing verification.\n\nI'm now sorting out what result code applies when a verified criterion actually fails eligibility versus when it's only provisional — distinguishing between action_required for failed checks and needs_verification for unconfirmed ones, since test cases like age 39 or excluded income tax payers should resolve to action_required.\n\nFor requirement questions I'm mapping undefined answers to needs_verification prompts, true answers to ok, and false answers to either action_required or needs_verification depending on whether the criterion was verified. Applying this to widow and Orunodoi cases — is_widow and bpl_household as verified requirements, nfsa_enrolled as provisional — before moving on to defining the \"anyof\" eligibility check.\n\nFor each exclusion question, undefined answers need verification, false is fine, and true triggers either action_required if verified or needs_verification otherwise with a note about confirming at the Gram Panchayat. I'm checking Orunodoi's exclusion list and confirming the test for excl_four_wheeler, plus making sure PM-KISAN's text about \"regardless of holding size\" doesn't accidentally match the landholding-size exclusion regex.\n\nI need to phrase the PM-KISAN note carefully to avoid the word \"hectare\" — saying the earlier land-size limit was removed in 2019 instead of referencing hectares directly, since that field only appears in ExtractedDoc, not the scheme JSON. Then I'm defining external checks as always needing verification with a message pointing to what can't be seen from a photo and how to check manually, followed by working out the overall eligibility verdict logic based on which checks are of kind \"elig\".\n\nFor the verdict rules: not_eligible only applies if a verified eligibility check actually fails, needs_verification applies if any eligibility check still needs confirmation (like a missing DOB or Aadhaar affecting the age check), and otherwise it's likely_eligible. I'm making sure provisional provenance never triggers action_required within eligibility kind — only verified failures can cause not_eligible, so I'll build a helper that enforces this distinction. Then I move to readiness logic, where any non-external check marked action_required should propagate up.\n\nFor readiness, any non-external needs_verification should also surface as needs_verification, and otherwise default to ready_for_review — external checks like bank seeding always get flagged needs_verification themselves but shouldn't block overall readiness, matching the clean-docs test case. I'm now checking the widow test scenario, where age 40 should resolve to ready_for_review, meaning the widow document pack must have no non-external needs_verification when docs are clean — so I'm reviewing what counts as mandatory docs for that pack.\n\nFor documents, missing presence always triggers action_required regardless of provisional status, while present docs are simply marked ok — matching the test expecting death certificate absence to produce action_required with provisional provenance. I'm also checking name-matching rules between bank passbook and aadhaar for the widow case, noting death certificate and BPL proof don't need name checks, then considering whether widow cases have any external checks of their own.\n\nFor the widow scheme, I'll keep externals empty since the spec doesn't list any and the test outcome works either way with ready_for_review. For Orunodoi, I'm adding ext_ration_card_valid as the external check (verifying the ration card is active and the name matches, confirmed at the supply office), and tracing through the non-external questions like nfsa_enrolled to confirm all conditions resolve to ready_for_review.\n\nWorking through the Orunodoi exclusions, I'm confirming they all resolve false (no trigger) and that name checks against Aadhaar and bank pass, while skipping field checks like IFSC since a bank passbook isn't mandatory. I'm also reasoning that provisional exclusion criteria answered favorably (like not owning a four-wheeler) shouldn't trigger needs_verification, since that status should only apply when a provisional criterion is actually triggered.\n\nFor supporting documents, I'm listing each one with docType, labels, presence, and provenance — for Orunodoi that's aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, and self-declaration, all flagged as provisional with a note to confirm the current list locally. I'm considering whether to add a similar \"confirm locally\" note for widow scheme documents, and thinking notes might need to live on the scheme object rather than the Report schema since Report doesn't have a dedicated notes field.\n\nI'm wiring benefit.report to call benefitOn with the scheme and as-of date, drafting a disclaimer in English and Assamese clarifying this tool catches preventable mistakes but doesn't guarantee approval since the department decides, and working out provenance attribution for doc-level and \"any of\" checks, plus the overall ordering of checks: docs, names, fields, age, requirements, any-of, exclusions, then external.\n\nI'm verifying no test ever prints a full Aadhaar number or raw account number in messages — only things like IFSC are safe to echo. I'm also confirming every check message includes Assamese text and a truthy fixWhere value, setting the provenance verified date, and drafting the \"unanswered question\" message template in both languages. For the PM-KISAN clean test case, I'm checking that no checks come back as action_required and that readiness depends on the family owner question being answered.\n\nFor land ownership, I'm confirming it's verified indirectly through the land record and name check rather than a separate requirement question, since pmNo doesn't address it directly and adding a dedicated check would break the clean test case. I'm also confirming readiness mirrors the eligibility status (needs_verification) when there's an unanswered exclusion question, and starting to trace through the income tax \"yes\" scenario as an exclusion trigger.\n\nFor the low-confidence name test, I verify that a 0.4 confidence score on the name field correctly flags it as needing verification, which propagates to overall readiness. I confirm the \"ready for review\" property holds as long as there's no external blocking condition, and that \"not applicable\" results count as non-blocking. I'm also examining edge cases in name matching, like when a reference name is just a title \"Smt.\" with no actual name tokens — deciding whether to treat the normalized empty name as unreadable and flag for verification, or handle it another way.\n\nThen I move to defining the sample test cases A, B, and C with their scheme IDs, labels, documents, and answers — Sample A being clean PM-KISAN data, Sample B having mismatched names between bank and land records, and Sample C representing a widow missing a death certificate. I'm working through Sample C's eligibility logic, calculating age from the DOB and checking BPL and widow status flags, making sure the readiness outcome correctly resolves to action_required.\n\nI'm deciding to keep names realistic but mark these records with an isSample flag and sample labels, using fake but plausible identifiers like a last-4 digit placeholder for Aadhaar numbers.\n\nNow I'm building the scheme registry module: setting up a map keyed by scheme ID holding version-sorted rule arrays, a function to register new scheme rules that validates and prevents duplicate id+version registrations, and accessor functions to fetch the latest or a specific version, list all schemes, and list available versions for a given scheme ID.\n\nI'm also checking that test isolation holds across files since Vitest isolates modules per test file, confirming invalid scheme IDs correctly throw via the zod schema, and verifying the `SchemeRule` parser handles default empty arrays properly without needing extra test-reset utilities.\n\nNow I'm validating a benefit override scenario where omitting `centralShare` falls back to the base value, totaling 1500 correctly, while malformed overrides with from>to ranges properly fail safeParse. Moving into the scheme packs themselves, I'm starting to define provenance source constants for PM-KISAN, including the official portal URL, title, and verification date.\n\nI'm working through the Assamese translation for exclusion criteria, noting that PM-KISAN exclusions apply at the family level (anyone holding or having held a constitutional post), so I need to phrase the question to ask about family members rather than just the individual applicant.\n\nContinuing to draft the Assamese translations for the remaining eligibility questions—income tax payment, practising professionals, and pension thresholds.\n\nAdding more labels for income tax payer, practising professional, and pension amounts in both English and Assamese. I also need to verify the test checks that every question text ends with a question mark, and I'm drafting the family-owner question about land recorded under a spouse's or minor child's name instead of the respondent's own.\n\nNow I'm adding field validation for the IFSC code: a pattern of four letters, a zero, then six alphanumeric characters, with matching labels and hints in English and Assamese, plus noting that this format comes from RBI standards rather than the pmkisan documentation.\n\nFor account number I'm setting a nine-to-eighteen digit pattern with bilingual labels, and making sure the escaped backslash in the pattern string matches exactly what the test expects. I'm also starting to lay out the external verification checks specific to PM-KISAN.\n\nAdding the Aadhaar-bank seeding check with bilingual labels and instructions to verify at the bank branch, plus beginning the account-active status check.\n\nThen for land_verified, I'm noting to check at the revenue circle office whether the land record is seeded/verified for PM-KISAN. I'm also laying out the PM-KISAN benefit details—central contribution of 6000 per year paid in 3 instalments, no state top-up—before moving into the mandatory documents section.\n\nI'm mapping each document to its verification location—Aadhaar at the Aadhaar centre, bank passbook at the bank, land record at the circle office—and noting how names should be cross-checked across these sources, including resolving bank name mismatches through the bank or Aadhaar centre. For where applicants confirm their registration, I'm leaning toward \"csc\" since PM-KISAN enrollment typically happens through Common Service Centres or the agriculture office. I'm also drafting a note clarifying that the old land size limit was removed in 2019, so any amount of cultivable land now qualifies.\n\nNow I'm setting up source citations for the Orunodoi scheme, pulling the official Assam government scheme page and noting its last-updated date for verification purposes.\n\nFor benefit details, I'm recording the central and state contributions (stateTopUp of 1250 per month) as officially sourced, then listing mandatory documents like the ration card verified against the supply office, along with supporting documents such as aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, and self-declaration, marking the self-declaration as applicant-sourced and provisional since the exact fixWhere location isn't fully confirmed.\n\nI'm also noting that name consistency checks between the ration card, aadhaar, and bank passbook aren't actually scheme rules but rather our own document-readiness checks, so I'm treating their provenance as secondary and provisional — a \"same\" result would be marked ok but flagged as provisional rather than treated as a strict scheme criterion.\n\nMoving on to NFSA enrollment, I'm drafting the requirement as a provisional question asking whether the family receives rice through the ration card under the National Food Security Act.\n\nI'm filling in the remaining provisional fields with their Assamese translations: PwD household member, AAY card status, and yearly household income under ₹2 lakh, each phrased as a simple yes/no question with a short label.\n\nNow I'm moving to the exclusion criteria, starting with checking whether anyone in the family currently holds or previously held an elected position (MP, MLA, or panchayat/municipal member), and translating this into Assamese for the gram panchayat context.\n\nContinuing with the govt employee/pensioner question, then adding contractor_or_trader and large_landholder questions, each with their Assamese translations.\n\nNext I'm drafting the four_wheeler question, deciding to keep it simple as \"car, jeep or other four-wheeler\" with its Assamese version, then moving on to the similar_state_scheme question about whether the family already receives money from a similar state scheme.\n\nI'm also working through the external verification item for ration_card_valid, phrasing how an official checks the card's validity and whether the family is listed, with guidance to verify at the food & civil supplies office or a fair price shop.\n\nI'm noting that no income certificate should be required for this scheme, and I'm considering whether to flag the reported one-time ₹1,500 payment for October 2026 as a temporary change rather than a recurring benefit, since the built-in pack doesn't currently include that override.\n\nI confirm the test expects benefitOn for October 15, 2026 to return 1250 with no overrides, so I'll add a provisional note about confirming the one-time payment locally, along with guidance that selection uses an undertaking-cum-checklist (Annexure A) to be filled in carefully, confirmed at the gram panchayat level. Then I move to setting up the source citation for the Widow pension scheme, referencing the Public Services Map entry for IGNWPS aligned with the Ministry of Rural Development statement.\n\nFor the widow scheme I'm defining the benefit structure: central contribution of 300 with no state top-up, applicable to ages 40-79, with age range validated against Aadhaar DOB, and an above-max-age hint in both English and Assamese pointing users to IGNOAPS if they're 80 or older — matching the test's expectation that the English message references \"Old Age Pension.\"\n\nNow I'm adding the eligibility requirements, verified questions asking whether the applicant is a widow and whether their household is below the poverty line per Government of India criteria (in both English and Assamese), with the fix-where guidance pointing to the block office or gram panchayat, plus mandatory documents like Aadhaar and death certificate.\n\nI'm sorting the provisional mandatory documents list — aadhaar, bank passbook, BPL proof, death certificate — to match expected test output, and deciding to treat the name consistency check across Aadhaar and bank passbook as verified since both have source URLs available.\n\nI'm also drafting notes about confirming requirements locally at the block office, including caveats about Assam's state top-up scheme, and checking that sample widow cases correctly include BPL proof without the death certificate. Next I'm moving on to building rejection codes.\n\nI'm defining a rejection-code schema with fix steps per code — including titles and remediation steps in English and Assamese, and where to go to fix each issue (bank for name mismatches, circle office for unverified land, PM-KISAN portal for pending eKYC). Now I'm starting on labels.ts.\n\n"}}
