{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:02:01.968Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm building out provenance helpers for the schemes, starting with PM-KISAN: verifying source against pmkisan.gov.in, confirming exclusions and mandatory documents (aadhaar, bank passbook, land record), and marking core eligibility criteria and external checks as verified.\n\nI'm defining PM-KISAN exclusion question IDs (constitutional post holders, govt employees, income tax payers, professionals, high pensioners), name-consistency checks against aadhaar/bank/land documents, field validation patterns for IFSC and account numbers, and external verification checks like seeding, account status, eKYC, and land verification.\n\nNow I'm moving on to Orunodoi, setting up mandatory documents (ration card) and provisional requirements around NFSA enrollment and eligibility categories like widows and persons with disabilities.\n\nI'm reasoning through the \"any of\" logic for Orunodoi's provisional eligibility—widow, disability, Antyodaya card, income threshold—making sure that if widow is answered false and other conditions are unanswered, the result correctly resolves to needs_verification rather than a false negative, then moving on to defining the exclusion list (elected representatives, professionals, govt staff/pensioners, contractors/traders, large landholders, four-wheeler owners).\n\nI'm now checking the PM-KISAN test that forbids any mention of hectare or landholding size in the pack's text, so I need to phrase any note about land eligibility without using \"hectare\" or similar flagged terms—something like \"regardless of how much land you own\" instead.\n\nFor Orunodoi, I'm thinking through the external ration card validity check as provenance-verified, and working out the name consistency logic across ration card, aadhaar, and bank passbook—treating the latter two as conditional checks that become not_applicable if the documents aren't provided, while considering how provenance should be marked for these when supporting docs are only provisional.\n\nA mismatch between ration card and Aadhaar names is really a document-consistency fact rather than a direct eligibility signal, but per the spec, a missing mandatory doc or name mismatch should still trigger action_required for readiness regardless of provenance status, so I'll keep that rule consistent across the board.\n\nSince provisional rules never give a definitive pass or fail per golden rule 2, but document disagreement is a real factual problem regardless of the rule's provisional status, I'll settle on mismatch always mapping to action_required and log that reasoning.\n\nFor the widow scheme, I'm working out mandatoryDocs across provisional sources—aadhaar, death certificate, bank passbook, bpl proof—sorted alphabetically to match the expected test output, plus the age range, DOB source, and age-max hint pointing to Old Age Pension.\n\nNow I'm adding bpl_household as a verified requirement, deciding which names need cross-checking (aadhaar and bank passbook, but not the husband's death certificate), setting the benefit amount and cadence, and choosing block_office as the confirmAt authority since widow pension applications route through block/GP level in Assam. I'm also confirming that a missing mandatory doc always triggers action_required regardless of status, and that failing the age-39 test case should flow through to an action_required readiness outcome due to unmet verified criteria.\n\nI'm settling on 0.8 as the low-confidence threshold that triggers needs_verification, then starting to outline the evaluate function logic — checking the ISO date, grouping documents by type, and walking through mandatory document requirements to mark each as satisfied or action_required.\n\nNext I'm working through the name-matching checks: pulling the reference document's name, comparing it against each required document's name fields (or owner names), and flagging cases where documents are missing, names aren't readable, or there's no reference to compare against as not_applicable or needs_verification before computing the best name match.\n\nI'm then layering in confidence scoring — low-confidence matches or possible/initials-only matches get flagged as needs_verification rather than ok, while true mismatches become action_required unless there's a \"family ownership\" answer explaining the discrepancy, in which case it's still flagged for verification with a note to confirm at the circle office. I also want to make sure the output never prints a full Aadhaar number.\n\nFor field-level checks, I'm handling missing documents as not_applicable, missing values as needs_verification, normalizing the value before regex testing, and treating low-confidence reads as needs_verification even on a pass — with account numbers never echoed in full in any message. For age checks, I'm pulling DOB from the document fields and flagging missing or low-confidence DOB as needs_verification before computing age.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining rule categories: requirement answers map true to ok, false to action_required or needs_verification depending on verification status, and undefined to needs_verification; the anyOf check follows similar logic across a list, with provenance resolved by preferring \"provisional\" if any item in the group is provisional.\n\nExclusions and external checks follow the same true/false/undefined pattern, with external checks always flagged as needs_verification since they require outside confirmation. For overall eligibility, I derive the status by looking across eligibility-kind checks: any action_required means not_eligible, any needs_verification propagates up, otherwise it's likely_eligible -- and since action_required only ever arises from verified checks by construction, this logic holds. Readiness status follows similarly, scanning non-external checks for action_required or needs_verification before defaulting to ready_for_review.\n\nI'm verifying this against test cases like PM-KISAN's \"bank KR\" scenario (needs_verification readiness, likely_eligible overall) and income tax disqualification (not_eligible, action_required), plus confirming landholding size never affects the result since hectares aren't factored into any check. For supporting documents, I'm compiling a list per scheme with presence flags -- for Orunodoi this includes aadhaar, bank passbook, voter ID, age proof, address proof, and a provisional self-declaration -- alongside a benefit calculation via benefitOn(scheme, asOfDate), and handling confidence scores per field where undefined values need a fallback.\n\nI'm also designing message builders using bilingual labels for documents and fix locations, tracking name-check provenance from the rule, and making sure check IDs stay unique -- like scoping name checks by docType to avoid collisions across requirements, anyOf conditions, and exclusions. For Orunodoi's family land question and PM-KISAN's family owner question, I need questionsFor(scheme) to surface all of these consistently, with every question text ending in a question mark in both languages.\n\nFor the registry, I'm structuring it as a map from scheme id to sorted version arrays, with registerScheme validating against duplicates, getScheme resolving latest-or-specific version, and listSchemes/listSchemeVersions exposing what's registered -- keeping in mind that registry mutations are scoped per test file since Vitest isolates module state, so earlier version registrations won't leak into later lookups like fetching nsap-widow at version 2.0.0.\n\nI'm also sketching out the sample data in samples.ts: three fictional cases (A clean, B with name mismatches across bank/land docs, C a widow case missing a death certificate), plus supporting pieces like aadhaarLast4, a schemes/sources.ts for provenance constants, and confirming that benefit tests can legitimately set sourceUrl to null for provisional entries.\n\nNow I'm defining scheme rules using SchemeRule.parse with the SchemeRuleInput type, being careful with Assamese script details like distinguishing ৰ from ৱ, and starting to write out sources.ts.\n\n"}}
