{"ts":"2026-10-10T09:05:08.005Z","type":"agent_event","event":{"type":"content_end","contentType":"reasoning","reasoning":"I'm designing the evaluate function for scheme/docs/answers checks—validating the as-of date, then checking mandatory documents by type and presence, flagging missing ones as action-required with appropriate provenance and messaging, and moving into name-matching checks against reference documents.\n\nFor name checks, I'm thinking through edge cases: skip comparisons when the target doc is present, handle missing reference documents (like ration card for Orunodoi) as not_applicable with a clear \"can't compare\" message rather than breaking the ready_for_review logic, and flag missing name fields as needing verification since we couldn't read them.\n\nI'm settling on how to classify match results: same names pass as ok, possible matches or initials-only trigger needs_verification, and outright mismatches become action_required unless the family owner question was answered affirmatively, in which case it downgrades to needs_verification. Low confidence on either name reading forces needs_verification regardless of the match result, since we simply can't trust the OCR enough to be certain — and I want a property test confirming that a matched name at a different confidence level never resolves to \"ok\" by mistake.\n\nMoving on to field checks: missing documents skip entirely, missing or low-confidence values need verification, and normalized values failing their pattern become action_required. For age checks, I'm handling missing/invalid DOBs and low-confidence readings as needs_verification, with out-of-range ages becoming action_required (verified) or needs_verification (provisional), and catching date errors from asOfDate being before the DOB. Requirements follow a similar true/false/undefined logic, and the \"anyOf\" check passes if any answer is yes.\n\nFor exclusions, I'm treating true as action_required (with a Gram Panchayat confirmation message), false as ok, and undefined as needs_verification; external checks always resolve to needs_verification with a standard \"can't see this from a photo\" message. Finally, I'm defining eligibility logic that marks someone as not_eligible when any eligibility check is both action_required and verified.\n\nFor readiness computation, external checks shouldn't block it—action_required or needs_verification among non-external checks determines the result, otherwise ready_for_review, matching the test where a clean case with external needs_verification still gets ready_for_review. An unanswered exclusion makes both eligibility and readiness needs_verification, and a provisional exclusion (like answering yes to four-wheeler ownership) should push readiness to needs_verification per the docs. I'm now tracing through the Orunodoi clean test case where all answers are no except widow/NFSA eligibility criteria.\n\nVerifying that name checks across aadhaar, bank, and ration card match case-insensitively for \"Anjali Bora,\" and confirming supporting docs are tracked separately from checks rather than affecting them directly. For the widow test, I'm checking that bpl_proof and death_certificate docs are present without needing name verification on the certificate, while the bank name check for \"Sunita Devi\" passes, leading to an overall ready_for_review when age is valid.\n\nNow I need a Sample C representing a widow case missing a death certificate, alongside Samples A and B, all defined in samples.ts with schemeId, docs, answers, and bilingual labels. I'm also planning bilingual message builder functions inside evaluate.ts that pull from labels.ts, mapping scheme supportingDocs into entries with doctype, labels, presence, and provenance, and generating fixWhere guidance for missing documents like land_record pointing to the circle office.\n\nI'm setting a name-match confidence threshold at 0.8, checking confidence on the reference doc's name field against the target's name or ownerNames, and keeping all messages in Latin digits for simplicity since determinism matters and I'm avoiding Date.now anywhere. I also need to make sure no full Aadhaar numbers or other sensitive fields like IFSC ever appear in output messages, and that duplicate documents of the same type just use the first match found.\n\nI decide it's fine to include the two names in mismatch messages (e.g. \"Aadhaar says X, bank passbook says Y\") since that's shown to the user themselves and won't trigger the 12-digit regex test. Now I'm splitting the implementation into evaluate.ts and a separate messages.ts file to keep each chunk under size limits.\n\n"}}
